window.AE_BM_BANK = {"_comment": "Generated by tools/build_problem_bank.py from vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/. Do not edit by hand.", "subject": "business-management", "categories": {"short_theory_case": {"label": "Short theory cases", "points": 2.0}, "extra_practice_problem": {"label": "Extra Practice problems", "points": 4.0}}, "archetypes": {"unordered-items": "Unordered items", "reverse-engineering-ratios": "Reverse engineering & ratios", "narrative-case": "Narrative case", "year-on-year-cash": "Year-on-year & cash", "financing-leverage": "Financing & leverage"}, "units": {"1": "Topic 1 · The firm", "2": "Topic 2 · Value creation", "3": "Topic 3 · Financial management (I)"}, "counts": {"short_theory_case": 24, "extra_practice_problem": 30}, "items": [{"id": "C01", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "circular-flow", "unit": 1, "title": "SkyCourier: real and financial flows of a drone-delivery start-up", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "circular-flow", "factors-of-production"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 3-11", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C01 - SkyCourier Circular Flow of Income.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>SkyCourier S.L. delivers medicines by drone to villages in the Pyrenees. It rents a hangar from a local farmer, employs twelve pilots and technicians, and was financed by investors who bought its shares. Each month it charges the regional health service and several pharmacies for the deliveries. In 2025 the families of the valley spent part of their wages on SkyCourier's new home-delivery service, and the company paid its first dividend to its shareholders.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify SkyCourier's inputs and outputs, and classify the inputs as factors of production</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A business transforms inputs into outputs [Slides 3-4]. The classic factors are land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Output: a service (drone delivery), not a good.</li><li>Land: the hangar site; labour: pilots and technicians; capital: drones, equipment and the investors' money; entrepreneurship: the founders who coordinate and bear the risk.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>SkyCourier's <strong>output</strong> is a <strong>service</strong>, the delivery of medicines and parcels. Its <strong>inputs</strong> are the factors of production [Slides 3-4]:</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>In SkyCourier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land (natural resources)</td><td>The rented hangar site</td></tr><tr><td>Labour</td><td>The twelve pilots and technicians</td></tr><tr><td>Capital</td><td>Drones, charging stations, software; the money provided by investors</td></tr><tr><td>Entrepreneurship</td><td>The founders who bring the other factors together and take the risk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The business activity is precisely the transformation of these inputs into the delivery service.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Describe the real flows and the financial flows between SkyCourier and the households, naming the two markets in which they take place</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Every exchange has two sides that run in opposite directions [Slide 11]. Households own the factors and are the units of consumption [Slide 7].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Factor market: households deliver land, labour and capital; SkyCourier pays rent, wages and dividends.</li><li>Goods and services market: SkyCourier delivers the service; households pay for it.</li><li>Real and financial flows run in opposite directions; what the firm keeps is its profit.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The circular flow of income links firms and households through two markets [Slides 5-11]:</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Market</th><th>Real flow</th><th>Financial flow (opposite direction)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Factor market</strong></td><td>Households → SkyCourier: the hangar (land), work (labour), savings invested in shares (capital)</td><td>SkyCourier → households: rent to the farmer, wages to the staff, dividends to the shareholders</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Market of goods and services</strong></td><td>SkyCourier → households: the delivery service</td><td>Households → SkyCourier: the price of the deliveries, paid out of their wages</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The flows close a circle: the wages paid by SkyCourier return to it, in part, when the families buy its home-delivery service. The <strong>real flows</strong> are the factors and the service; the <strong>financial flows</strong> are their monetary compensation, running the other way [Slide 11]. What SkyCourier keeps after paying all the factors is its <strong>profit</strong>, from which the dividend was paid.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C02", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "functional-areas", "unit": 1, "title": "Orbital Fasteners: sorting a day of activity into the functional areas", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "functional-areas", "management-functions"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 13-15", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C02 - Orbital Fasteners Functional Areas.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Orbital Fasteners S.A. makes titanium bolts certified for satellites. On a single Monday: (1) the purchasing team ordered 2 tonnes of titanium bar; (2) the CNC lines produced 40,000 bolts; (3) the sales engineer presented the company at a space-industry fair; (4) the finance department negotiated a bank loan to buy a new thread-rolling machine; (5) it collected an invoice from a satellite integrator and paid the titanium supplier; (6) HR interviewed two quality inspectors; (7) the plant manager set the production targets for the next quarter and checked last month's scrap rate against the plan; (8) the IT team upgraded the traceability database.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify the eight activities into real, financial and management functions</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Real functions follow the product (supply, production, marketing, support); financial functions follow the money (financing, investing, paying and collecting); management functions are planning, organization and control [Slide 13].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Real: 1 (supply), 2 (production), 3 (marketing), 6 and 8 (support: HR, information systems).</li><li>Financial: 4 (financing and investing), 5 (collecting revenues and paying for inputs).</li><li>Management: 7 (planning and control).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Function</th><th>Group [Slide 13]</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Ordering titanium</td><td>Supply (acquisition of inputs)</td><td>Real</td></tr><tr><td>2. Producing bolts</td><td>Production</td><td>Real</td></tr><tr><td>3. Trade fair</td><td>Marketing</td><td>Real</td></tr><tr><td>4. Bank loan for a machine</td><td>Financing and investing</td><td>Financial</td></tr><tr><td>5. Collecting and paying</td><td>Receiving revenues and paying for inputs</td><td>Financial</td></tr><tr><td>6. Hiring inspectors</td><td>Support (Human Resources)</td><td>Real</td></tr><tr><td>7. Targets and scrap check</td><td>Planning and control</td><td>Management</td></tr><tr><td>8. Traceability database</td><td>Support (Information Systems)</td><td>Real</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain how the real and the financial cycles relate, and what engineers contribute to the management of a firm like this one</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The financial functions run alongside the real cycle [Slide 13]; recall the role of engineers in business management [Slide 15].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>The financial cycle mirrors the real one: funds raised → inputs paid → revenues collected → capital and interest returned.</li><li>Engineers: project planning, time management, problem solving, attention to detail, data processing, technical knowledge.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The <strong>financial cycle runs alongside the real cycle</strong> [Slide 13]: the company raises funds (the loan of activity 4), uses them to pay for inputs (the titanium of activity 1, paid in activity 5), turns the inputs into bolts and sells them, collects the revenue (activity 5) and finally returns capital and interest to those who lent the money. Without the financial functions the real cycle stops; without the real cycle there is no revenue to repay the funds.</p>\n<p>Engineers bring <strong>project planning, time management and problem solving, attention to detail, experience in data processing, computer skills and technical knowledge</strong> [Slide 15]: exactly what activity 7 (targets and scrap control) and activity 8 (traceability data) require.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C03", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "management-process", "unit": 1, "title": "Atlas Line Maintenance: the four stages of the management process in a ramp-up", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "management-process", "fayol"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slide 14", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C03 - Atlas Line Maintenance Management Process.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Atlas Line Maintenance S.L. has won a contract to perform the overnight checks of a low-cost airline at a new base. The station manager, Marta, has six months to be ready. She sets the goal of \"zero technical delays in the first summer\" and a plan to hire and certify 25 mechanics. She draws the shift structure and decides who is responsible for each aircraft type and which tools each shift needs. During the first weeks she holds daily briefings, encourages the team and mediates a conflict between the night and day shifts over handovers. Every Friday she compares the delays and the hours per check with the targets and adjusts the shifts.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the four stages of the management process in Marta's actions</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Henri Fayol's formulation: planning, organizing, leading, controlling [Slide 14].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Planning: the zero-delay goal and the hiring and certification plan.</li><li>Organizing: shift structure, responsibilities, tools.</li><li>Leading: daily briefings, motivation, resolving the shift conflict.</li><li>Controlling: weekly comparison of delays and hours with the targets.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Stage [Slide 14]</th><th>Content</th><th>At Atlas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Planning</strong></td><td>Defining goals, establishing strategy, developing subplans</td><td>\"Zero technical delays\"; plan to hire and certify 25 mechanics</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Organizing</strong></td><td>What must be done, how, and by whom</td><td>Shift structure, responsibilities per aircraft type, tools per shift</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Leading</strong></td><td>Directing and motivating, resolving conflicts</td><td>Daily briefings, encouragement, mediation between shifts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Controlling</strong></td><td>Monitoring that activities go as planned</td><td>Friday comparison of delays and hours per check with the targets</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain why the four stages form a cycle and what Marta does when she finds a deviation</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The four activities repeat around one common aim: achieving the organization's stated purpose [Slide 14].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Control results feed the next round of planning: the process is cyclical.</li><li>A deviation triggers corrective action: reorganizing the shifts or revising the plan, then leading the change.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The management process is a <strong>cycle</strong> around the organization's purpose [Slide 14]. Control is not the end: when the Friday review shows a deviation (for example, more hours per check than planned), Marta corrects it by <strong>reorganizing</strong> the shifts or by <strong>revising the plan</strong> (more mechanics, new targets), and she must then <strong>lead</strong> the team through the change. The new results are controlled the following week, so the four stages keep repeating while the base operates.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C04", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "entrepreneur", "unit": 1, "title": "An eVTOL founder: the four views of the entrepreneur and her rights", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "entrepreneur", "residual-income"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 16-18", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C04 - eVTOL Founder Views of the Entrepreneur.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Lucía, an aerospace engineer, noticed in 2023 that city hospitals needed faster transfers of organs than road ambulances could offer, and that nobody was serving that need. She designed an electric vertical-take-off aircraft with a new battery-cooling system, mortgaged her flat to fund the first prototype, and brought together a team of engineers, a supplier of carbon structures and a hangar owner. Today she signs every contract of the company, decides who works on each project, keeps whatever profit is left once everyone has been paid, and has been offered a large sum by an airline for her stake.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Relate Lucía's behaviour to the four views of the entrepreneur</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Coordinator; risk taker (Knight, 1921); innovator (Schumpeter, 1950); \"on alert\" (Kirzner, 1973) [Slide 17].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>On alert (Kirzner): she detected an unserved need.</li><li>Innovator (Schumpeter): a new aircraft and battery-cooling system.</li><li>Risk taker (Knight): she mortgaged her flat.</li><li>Coordinator: she brought together engineers, supplier and hangar owner.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>View [Slide 17]</th><th>Evidence in the case</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>\"On alert\"</strong> (Kirzner, 1973)</td><td>She detected a new business opportunity, a need nobody was serving</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Innovator</strong> (Schumpeter, 1950)</td><td>She introduced a new product with a new battery-cooling technology</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Risk taker</strong> (Knight, 1921)</td><td>She risked her own flat to finance the prototype</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coordinator</strong></td><td>She brought land (hangar), labour (engineers) and capital (supplier, funds) together</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Together these are the functions of the entrepreneur: coordinate and control production, ensure payment of the factors, take risky decisions, innovate, and detect opportunities [Slide 17].</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain which rights make Lucía \"the entrepreneur\" of her company</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Team production needs a controller, and the incentive to be one comes from four rights [Slide 18].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Right to the residual income (the profit left after paying the factors).</li><li>Right to control and reassign the other factors (who works on each project).</li><li>Being the central party in all contracts.</li><li>Right to sell these rights (the airline's offer).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>A firm is teamwork, and team production needs someone who controls it; the incentive to take that role comes from four <strong>rights</strong> [Slide 18]. Lucía holds all of them: she receives the <strong>residual income</strong> (the profit left once every factor is paid); she <strong>controls and reassigns</strong> the other factors (she decides who works on each project); she is the <strong>central party in all the contracts</strong> with the inputs (she signs them); and she can <strong>sell these rights</strong> (the airline's offer for her stake). The person who holds all these rights is, by definition, the entrepreneur.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C05", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "legal-forms", "unit": 1, "title": "Three engineers choosing a legal form for a composite-repair shop", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "legal-forms", "liability"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 20-24", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C05 - Three Engineers Choosing a Legal Form.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Ana, Bruno and Carla want to open a shop that repairs composite parts of light aircraft. Ana would invest 60,000 m.u. of savings; Bruno and Carla only their work. A single faulty repair could lead to claims far larger than everything they own, so they worry about losing their homes. They want to start quickly and cheaply, but in five years they hope to open three more shops with outside investors, and they want the business to continue if one of them leaves.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compare the sole proprietorship, the partnership and the corporation on the features that matter to the three engineers</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Look at the number of owners, liability, ease of starting, permanence and ability to raise funds [Slides 21-24].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Sole proprietorship: one owner only, unlimited liability: ruled out.</li><li>Partnership: easy to start, pooled talents; unlimited and joint liability, relative impermanence.</li><li>Corporation: limited liability, permanence, easy transfer of shares, greater financial capability; complex and costly, double taxation.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Sole proprietorship</th><th>Partnership</th><th>Corporation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Owners</td><td>One person</td><td>Two or more co-owners</td><td>Shareholders</td></tr><tr><td>Liability</td><td>Unlimited</td><td><strong>Unlimited and joint</strong></td><td><strong>Limited</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Starting</td><td>Simple</td><td>Few restrictions</td><td>Complex and costly</td></tr><tr><td>Permanence</td><td>Impermanent</td><td>Relatively impermanent</td><td>Permanent</td></tr><tr><td>Funds</td><td>Hard to raise</td><td>Pooled funds and talents</td><td>Greater financial capability</td></tr><tr><td>Taxation</td><td>No separate tax</td><td>No separate tax</td><td>Special and double taxation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A sole proprietorship belongs to <strong>one</strong> person [Slide 21], so it does not fit three owners. A partnership is easy to set up through a partnership agreement and pools Ana's money with Bruno's and Carla's skills [Slide 22], but each partner answers for the firm's debts with all their personal wealth, jointly [Slide 23]. A corporation is a separate legal entity whose owners have <strong>limited</strong> liability [Slide 24].</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Recommend a legal form and justify it with the engineers' goals</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Weigh their biggest fear (claims) and their plans (outside investors, continuity) against the cost of forming the company.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Recommend a corporation: limited liability protects their homes; permanence; easy entry of investors through shares.</li><li>Accept its drawbacks: cost of formation, regulation and disclosure, double taxation.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The <strong>corporation</strong> fits best. Limited liability addresses their main fear: a large claim would affect the company's assets, not their homes. It is <strong>permanent</strong>, so the business continues if one partner leaves, and ownership is <strong>easily transferred through shares</strong>, which is how the outside investors of the expansion would enter; it also has greater financial capability [Slide 24]. The price is a more complex and costly formation, government regulation and disclosure requirements, and double taxation; the engineers should accept these costs in exchange for protection and growth. The course also lists forms of partnership that limit liability: the limited liability partnership (LLP) [Slide 20] and the limited partnership, where some partners answer only up to what they invested [Slide 29]. An LLP would also meet the fear of claims, but the corporation fits better with the plans for outside investors and continuity.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C06", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "agency-problem", "unit": 1, "title": "Meseta Aerostructures: a CEO, dispersed shareholders and the agency problem", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "agency-problem", "corporate-governance"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 25-26, and Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slide 4", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C06 - Meseta Aerostructures Agency Problem.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Meseta Aerostructures S.A. is listed on the stock exchange; no shareholder owns more than 2% of its shares. Its CEO, a professional manager, is paid a fixed salary that grows with the number of employees under her. In three years she has bought two loss-making companies, moved headquarters to an expensive tower and ordered a corporate jet, while the share price has fallen by 30%. The annual report gives little detail on these decisions, and most small shareholders do not attend the general meeting.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain why this situation is an agency problem</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Who owns the company and who controls it? Compare their objectives and the information each side has [Slide 25].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Ownership (shareholders) is separated from control (the professional CEO).</li><li>The CEO pursues her own benefit (size, prestige) instead of the shareholders' (value).</li><li>Different access to information and different objectives; dispersed shareholders lack information and incentives to monitor.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>In large corporations the company is run by <strong>managers</strong>, professional employees, while it is owned by the <strong>shareholders</strong>: ownership is separated from control [Slide 25]. At Meseta, the CEO's pay rises with the number of employees, so her objective is <strong>size and prestige</strong> (acquisitions, tower, jet), not the value of the shares. Shareholders and managers have <strong>different objectives</strong> and <strong>different access to information</strong> (the report gives little detail), and with stakes below 2% no shareholder has enough information or <strong>incentive</strong> to assess her performance. The result is a conflict of interest between ownership and control: the <strong>agency problem</strong>.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Propose mechanisms that would align the CEO's decisions with the shareholders' interests</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Use the control mechanisms of [Slide 26] and the alignment mechanisms of Topic 2 [Slide 4].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Select a Board of Directors that supervises the CEO.</li><li>Contracts linked to goals (pay tied to profit or share value instead of headcount); incentive compensation.</li><li>Audits and indicators.</li><li>External discipline: the market of enterprises (takeover risk) and the labour market for managers.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Shareholders must be able to control how the manager acts [Topic 1, Slide 26]:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Select the Board of Directors</strong> so that it actually supervises the CEO and approves large acquisitions.</li><li><strong>Contracts linked to goals:</strong> replace pay tied to headcount with incentive compensation tied to profit or share value [Topic 2, Slide 4].</li><li><strong>Request audits</strong> of the acquisitions and expenses, and publish <strong>indicators</strong> that let shareholders judge performance.</li><li>Outside the firm, the <strong>market of enterprises</strong> (a falling share price invites a takeover that would replace the CEO) and the <strong>labour market</strong> for managers (her reputation) also discipline her [Topic 2, Slide 4].</li></ol>\n<p>Increasingly, shareholders with large blocks of shares take the decisions [Slide 26]; a group of institutional investors could build such a block at Meseta.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C07", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "other-forms", "unit": 1, "title": "A drone-spraying cooperative and a launcher joint venture", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "cooperative", "joint-venture"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 27-29", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C07 - Drone Spraying Cooperative and a Launcher Joint Venture.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Two situations in the same region:</p>\n<p><strong>Case 1.</strong> Forty vineyard owners cannot each afford a crop-spraying drone. They create an organization that buys three drones and hires two pilots; any vineyard owner of the area may join, each member has one vote in the assembly regardless of size, and at the end of the year what is left after paying the expenses is returned to each member in proportion to the hectares sprayed.</p>\n<p><strong>Case 2.</strong> A European launcher manufacturer and a Japanese engine company set up a company owned 50/50 for the sole purpose of developing a reusable upper stage over eight years. When the stage is certified, the company will be dissolved.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the form of business ownership in each case and justify it with its defining features</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compare Case 1 with the five principles of a cooperative [Slide 27], and Case 2 with the \"other forms\" of [Slide 29].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Case 1: cooperative association (open membership, democratic control, benefits in proportion to participation, user-members are owners); closest type: a buying co-op.</li><li>Case 2: joint venture: a special partnership for a specific task or project, common in international business.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p><strong>Case 1 is a cooperative association</strong> [Slides 27-28]: an incorporated organization whose <strong>user-members are also its owners</strong> and get back the revenue left after expenses. It follows the cooperative principles: <strong>open membership</strong> (any vineyard owner may join), <strong>democratic control</strong> (one member, one vote), and <strong>benefits distributed in proportion to each member's participation</strong> (hectares sprayed). Of the course's common types, the closest is a <strong>buying co-op</strong>, which helps members buy at lower prices (here, a spraying service); it is not an agricultural co-op in the course's sense, which helps member farmers <strong>market</strong> their products [Slide 28].</p>\n<p><strong>Case 2 is a joint venture</strong> [Slide 29]: a special type of partnership set up by firms to <strong>accomplish a specific task or project</strong>, the reusable upper stage, and dissolved once the task is done. It is very common in <strong>international business</strong>, as here.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain one advantage of each form for its members</p>", "hint_html": "<p>What could the members not achieve alone?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Co-op: access to expensive equipment and lower costs, with democratic control and fair sharing.</li><li>Joint venture: sharing a large, risky project and combining complementary know-how across countries without merging the parent companies.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The <strong>cooperative</strong> gives small vineyard owners access to equipment that none could buy alone, at cost, while keeping control democratic and sharing the surplus fairly. The <strong>joint venture</strong> lets two firms from different countries <strong>share the cost and risk</strong> of a long, uncertain project and <strong>combine complementary know-how</strong> (launcher and engines) without merging the whole companies; each parent keeps its independence in the rest of its business. These advantages are reasoning applied to the case; the deck defines the forms but does not list their advantages.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C08", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "enterprise-types", "unit": 1, "title": "Classifying three aerospace enterprises, and why firms exist at all", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "types-of-enterprises", "market-and-enterprise"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 1_The Firm_Types  Objectives.pdf, Slides 12 and 19", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C08 - Classifying Three Aerospace Enterprises.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Consider three enterprises:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>TitanMine</strong>, a private company with 35 employees that extracts titanium ore in one region and sells it only in its own country;</li><li><strong>AeroNational</strong>, a state-owned manufacturer of military transport aircraft with 9,000 employees, plants in three countries and customers in twenty;</li><li><strong>Wingtip Consulting</strong>, a one-person business that advises flying schools on safety management in several markets of one country.</li></ul>\n<p>A classmate claims: \"Firms are unnecessary; each engineer could simply sell their work to whoever needs it on the market.\"</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify the three enterprises using the economic and legal criteria of the course</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Economic: sector, size, scope of the business. Legal: ownership of the factors, form of ownership [Slide 19].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>TitanMine: primary, small, one market and national, private.</li><li>AeroNational: secondary, big, multi-market and multinational, state-owned; corporation.</li><li>Wingtip: tertiary, small, multi-market and national, private, sole proprietorship.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Criterion [Slide 19]</th><th>TitanMine</th><th>AeroNational</th><th>Wingtip Consulting</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sector</td><td>Primary (extraction)</td><td>Secondary (manufacturing)</td><td>Tertiary (services)</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>Small</td><td>Big</td><td>Small</td></tr><tr><td>Scope</td><td>One market, national</td><td>Multi-market, multinational</td><td>Multi-market, national</td></tr><tr><td>Ownership of the factors</td><td>Private</td><td>State-owned</td><td>Private</td></tr><tr><td>Form of ownership</td><td>Company (likely a corporation)</td><td>Corporation</td><td>Sole proprietorship</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The deck gives the criteria and their classes but no thresholds; reading 35 employees as small and 9,000 as big is the usual interpretation (extension).</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Answer the classmate using the course's explanation of why enterprises exist</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Scarce resources, unlimited wants, specialization and the two ways of coordinating it [Slide 12].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Wants are met through specialization (division of labour), which requires coordination.</li><li>Coordination can be done by the market or by the enterprise; the enterprise appears where there is a dissatisfied need that it coordinates better.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Economics studies how scarce resources are used to satisfy unlimited wants; those wants are met through <strong>specialization</strong>, the division of labour, and specialization requires <strong>coordination</strong> [Slide 12]. Coordination can be carried out either by the <strong>market</strong>, as the classmate suggests, or by the <strong>enterprise</strong>. Building a transport aircraft requires thousands of specialists working together on one product: coordinating them through separate market contracts would be impractical, so an enterprise appears to coordinate them (reasoning applied to the case, beyond the slides). In the course's words, an enterprise appears because there is a <strong>dissatisfied need</strong> [Slide 12]. Wingtip shows that, for simple services, one person selling directly to the market can also work.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C09", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "objectives", "unit": 2, "title": "Costa Airport: profit maximization or value creation?", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "business-objectives", "value-creation"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 3-7", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C09 - Costa Airport Profit Maximization or Value Creation.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>A private company operates Costa Airport. A new board member argues: \"Our only objective is to maximize this year's profit: let's double parking fees, cut the night shift of air-traffic support staff and postpone the runway resurfacing.\" The finance director replies that the board should instead aim to \"maximize the value of the company\", and the operations director warns that the airline customers, the unions, the city council and the regional government will all react to such measures.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain the limitations of profit maximization as the objective of the firm, using the board member's proposal</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The Classical Theory's objective has four limitations [Slide 3].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Profit is hard to define and quantify (this year vs future years).</li><li>It ignores the risk of achieving the profits (postponed resurfacing, safety, reaction of airlines).</li><li>The very idea of \"maximizing\" is questionable.</li><li>It gives an excessive role to the entrepreneur, whereas ownership and control are separated.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The proposal follows the classical objective, <strong>profit maximization</strong>, which has four limitations [Slide 3]:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Profit is difficult to define and to quantify:</strong> cutting maintenance raises this year's profit but lowers future profits (a degraded runway, a closure for emergency works).</li><li><strong>It ignores risk:</strong> the cuts raise the risk of incidents and of losing airlines, which a single profit figure does not show.</li><li><strong>The concept of maximization itself is questionable</strong> [Slide 3]; in practice managers aim at a minimum level of profit that satisfies the shareholders [Slide 4].</li><li><strong>It gives an excessive role to the entrepreneur:</strong> in a company like this, ownership and control are separated, and the board, managers and shareholders have different goals.</li></ol>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain why the finance director's objective is the one the course proposes, and how stakeholders shape it</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Organizational theory: objectives are the outcome of a negotiation among stakeholders, limited by survival and unequal power [Slides 5-6]. Recall the unified vision of survival, profit and growth [Slide 7].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Objectives result from negotiation among stakeholders (shareholders, customers, employees, State, society...).</li><li>Constraints: the firm must survive; groups have different bargaining power.</li><li>The resulting objective: maximize the value of the enterprise (create value), linking survival, profit and growth.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>According to <strong>organizational theory</strong>, the objectives of the firm are the <strong>outcome of a negotiation among its stakeholders</strong>: here the shareholders, the airlines (customers), the employees and unions, the city council and regional government (the State), suppliers, banks and society [Slides 5-6]. The negotiation is limited because the firm must <strong>survive</strong> and because <strong>not all groups have the same power</strong>. The objective that satisfies the most powerful group while respecting the others and the firm's survival is to <strong>maximize the value of the enterprise</strong>, to create value [Slide 6]. In the unified vision, value comes from a cycle in which sales generate profits, retained profits finance investments (such as the resurfacing) and these renew the products and markets [Slide 7]; sacrificing the investment for one year's profit breaks that cycle.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C10", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "stakeholders", "unit": 2, "title": "Closing an engine-parts plant: mapping the stakeholders and their power", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "stakeholders", "organizational-theory"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 4-6", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C10 - Engine Plant Closure and its Stakeholders.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>TurboCast S.A., a maker of turbine blades, plans to close its oldest plant, in a town of 8,000 inhabitants where it employs 600 people, and move production to a new automated plant. The shareholders support the plan; the main bank, which financed the new plant, wants it to happen fast. The unions call a strike. The regional government offers a subsidy if the plant stays open two more years. The engine manufacturer that buys 70% of the blades demands that deliveries are not interrupted. Local suppliers and environmental groups also make their voices heard.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the stakeholders in the case and what each one wants</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Around management stand shareholders, banks and financial agents, customers, suppliers, the State, employees and unions, and society [Slide 5].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Shareholders: higher profit and value through automation.</li><li>Bank: repayment of the loan for the new plant.</li><li>Employees and unions: keep their jobs.</li><li>State (regional government): employment in the region.</li><li>Customer (engine maker): uninterrupted deliveries.</li><li>Suppliers and society: local business, environment.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Stakeholder [Slide 5]</th><th>In the case</th><th>What it wants</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shareholders</td><td>Owners of TurboCast</td><td>Lower costs and higher value through automation</td></tr><tr><td>Banks and financial agents</td><td>Lender of the new plant</td><td>Repayment; a fast transition that makes the plant profitable</td></tr><tr><td>Employees and unions</td><td>600 workers</td><td>To keep their jobs and conditions</td></tr><tr><td>The State</td><td>Regional government</td><td>Employment and activity in the region</td></tr><tr><td>Customers</td><td>Engine manufacturer</td><td>Uninterrupted, on-time deliveries</td></tr><tr><td>Suppliers</td><td>Local suppliers</td><td>To keep their contracts</td></tr><tr><td>Society</td><td>Town, environmental groups</td><td>Local activity, responsible behaviour (CSR)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain, using organizational theory, how the final decision is likely to be reached</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The objective is negotiated, limited by survival and by unequal bargaining power [Slide 6].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>The decision is a negotiated outcome, not one group's wish.</li><li>It satisfies the group with the most bargaining power (here the engine maker, which buys 70%, together with shareholders and bank) while taking the others into account.</li><li>Constraint of survival: TurboCast must stay competitive.</li><li>A likely compromise: a phased closure (subsidy, two years), guaranteed deliveries, relocation or retraining.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Organizational theory sees the objective as the <strong>outcome of a negotiation among stakeholders</strong>, limited by two facts: the firm must <strong>survive</strong>, and <strong>not all groups have the same power</strong> [Slide 6]. Here the engine manufacturer has very high bargaining power (70% of sales), as do the shareholders and the bank; the unions and the government have less power but can impose costs (strikes, subsidies withdrawn). The likely outcome is a <strong>compromise</strong>: the move to the automated plant goes ahead, because survival requires competitive costs, but it is <strong>phased</strong> over two years with the regional subsidy, deliveries are protected with stock built in advance, and part of the staff is relocated or retrained. That decision satisfies the most powerful groups while taking the others into account, which is what maximizing the value of the enterprise means in practice.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C11", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "generic-environment", "unit": 2, "title": "A small-launcher start-up reads its generic environment", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "generic-environment", "pestel"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 11-13", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C11 - Small Launcher Generic Environment.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Altair Launch S.L. is developing a rocket for satellites under 200 kg. In its business plan it lists: interest rates have doubled in two years and venture capital is scarcer; the European Union has created a fund to secure independent access to space, and a new national space law sets licensing rules; additive manufacturing has made engine parts much cheaper, while reusable rockets are becoming a key technology; the country trains many aerospace engineers, but public opinion is increasingly worried about space debris and the noise of the launch site.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify each element into the four groups of factors of the generic environment</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Economic, political-legal, socio-cultural and technological factors [Slide 11]. (Many textbooks use PESTEL, which separates environmental and legal factors; the course uses four groups.)</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Economic: interest rates, scarce venture capital.</li><li>Political-legal: EU fund, national space law and licensing.</li><li>Socio-cultural: supply of trained engineers (education), public concern about debris and noise (value system).</li><li>Technological: additive manufacturing (key), reusability (incipient/key).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Group [Slide 11]</th><th>Element of the case</th><th>Effect on Altair</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Economic</strong></td><td>Higher interest rates, less venture capital</td><td>Financing becomes harder and costlier</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Political-legal</strong></td><td>EU fund for independent access to space; national space law and licences</td><td>Opportunity (public demand and funding) and a regulatory requirement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Socio-cultural</strong></td><td>Many trained engineers (level of education); concern about debris and noise (value system)</td><td>Easier hiring; pressure on the launch site and on debris mitigation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Technological</strong></td><td>Additive manufacturing; reusable rockets</td><td>Cheaper engines; a key technology that may become a must</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The course's four groups have no separate environmental factor: the table places the public's concern under the socio-cultural value system (public opinion), while the outside-in view of [Slide 13] also lists the natural environment among the forces around the industry. The PESTEL framework of other books would treat debris and noise as a separate environmental factor (extension).</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain the difference between the generic and the specific environment and why Altair needs both</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Who is affected by each layer, and what is analysed at the centre [Slides 11-13]?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Generic environment: factors that affect every firm in the country/economy.</li><li>Specific environment: the firm's own industry (rivalry, entry and exit barriers, substitutes, customers, suppliers), analysed with Porter's five forces.</li><li>The analysis moves from the outside in.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The <strong>generic environment</strong> affects all firms, whatever their industry: the interest rates or the space law hit every company in the country [Slide 11]. The <strong>specific environment</strong> is the environment of the firm's <strong>own industry</strong>: the rivalry among launch providers, entry and exit barriers, substitutes (for example rideshare launches on large rockets), and the relationships with customers and suppliers [Slide 12]. The analysis moves <strong>from the outside in</strong>, from the economy, technology and politics to the industry at the centre [Slide 13]. Altair needs both: the generic factors explain the conditions it cannot change, and the specific ones explain whether it can earn money against its direct competitors.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C12", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "five-forces", "unit": 2, "title": "Island regional airlines under Porter's five forces", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 20, "tags": ["theory-case", "porter-five-forces", "industry-analysis"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 12-15", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C12 - Island Regional Airline Five Forces.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Three airlines fly between the islands of an archipelago with similar turboprops and similar fares; none is clearly larger. Two manufacturers in the world build turboprops of that size, and a single company supplies the jet fuel at the airports. Most passengers are price-sensitive residents who compare fares online, plus the regional government, which buys tickets for public employees. A fast-ferry company has just started services between the two main islands. To start a new airline one needs aircraft, an operating certificate and slots at airports that are almost full.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Analyse the five competitive forces of this industry, rating each one as high, medium or low with a reason</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Rivalry, supplier power, buyer power, threat of entry, threat of substitutes, each with its determinants [Slides 14-15].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Rivalry: high (three similar firms, little differentiation, no leader).</li><li>Suppliers: high (two aircraft makers, one fuel supplier).</li><li>Buyers: high (price-sensitive residents; the government as a large buyer).</li><li>New entrants: low-medium (capital, certificate, full slots: legal and access barriers).</li><li>Substitutes: medium-high (fast ferry on the main route).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Force [Slides 14-15]</th><th>Rating</th><th>Determinants in the case</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Rivalry among existing firms</strong></td><td>High</td><td>Three firms of similar size (low concentration, no leader), similar product and fares (little differentiation)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bargaining power of suppliers</strong></td><td>High</td><td>Only two turboprop manufacturers and a single fuel supplier: high relative bargaining power</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bargaining power of buyers</strong></td><td>High</td><td>Residents are price-sensitive and compare online; the government buys in volume</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Threat of new entrants</strong></td><td>Low to medium</td><td>Capital requirements (aircraft), legal and regulatory barriers (operating certificate), access to \"distribution\" (airport slots almost full)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Threat of substitutes</strong></td><td>Medium to high</td><td>The fast ferry: buyers' propensity to substitute depends on its relative price and travel time</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Conclude on the attractiveness of the industry and suggest one strategic response for an airline</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The stronger the forces, the lower the profitability the industry allows. Connect your answer with the sources of competitive advantage [Slide 20].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Low attractiveness: four of five forces are strong.</li><li>A response: cost advantage (fleet standardization, high capacity utilization) or differentiation (schedule, connections, service) that weakens buyer power; possibly cooperation or consolidation to reduce rivalry.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>With strong rivalry, strong suppliers, strong buyers and a growing substitute, the industry is <strong>unattractive</strong>: the profit available is squeezed from every side, and only the entry barriers protect it. An airline can respond by building a <strong>cost advantage</strong> (a single aircraft type [Slide 21], high capacity utilization [Slide 22]) to survive the price war, or a <strong>differentiation advantage</strong> (frequent schedules, connections with long-haul flights, loyalty schemes for residents) that reduces buyers' price sensitivity [Slides 23-24].</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C13", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "barriers-to-entry", "unit": 2, "title": "Can a car-seat maker enter the aircraft-seat market? Barriers to entry", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "barriers-to-entry", "diversification"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 12, 15 and 25", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C13 - Aircraft Seat Market Barriers to Entry.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>ComfortAuto S.A., a large maker of car seats, wants to enter the market of passenger seats for commercial aircraft. Three established firms supply almost all airlines. Each new seat model must pass crash and flammability certification that takes two years. Airlines buy seats through long contracts linked to aircraft orders, and the established firms have decades of reputation for reliability. They produce in large volumes in plants built for this purpose, and they own patents on lightweight seat frames. ComfortAuto, however, has strong engineering, low-cost purchasing of foam and fabrics, and spare plant capacity.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the barriers to entry ComfortAuto faces, using the determinants of the threat of entry</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Capital requirements, economies of scale, absolute cost advantage, product differentiation, access to distribution channels, legal and regulatory barriers [Slide 15].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Legal and regulatory: two-year certification; patents.</li><li>Product differentiation: reputation for reliability.</li><li>Access to distribution channels: long contracts linked to aircraft orders.</li><li>Economies of scale: incumbents' volumes in dedicated plants.</li><li>Capital requirements and absolute cost advantages (patents, experience).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Determinant [Slide 15]</th><th>In the case</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Legal and regulatory barriers</strong></td><td>Two years of crash and flammability certification per model; patents on lightweight frames</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Product differentiation</strong></td><td>The incumbents' decades of reputation for reliability make airlines reluctant to switch</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Access to distribution channels</strong></td><td>Seats are sold through long contracts tied to aircraft orders, already held by the incumbents</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Economies of scale</strong></td><td>Incumbents produce large volumes in dedicated plants, so their unit cost is lower</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Absolute cost advantage</strong></td><td>Patents and accumulated experience (economies of learning)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Capital requirements</strong></td><td>Certification programmes and adapting a plant require large investment before any sale</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>These barriers make the threat of entry low and protect the profitability of the three incumbents.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Assess whether the diversification makes sense for ComfortAuto</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A diversification decision asks two questions: how attractive is the sector, and can the firm achieve a competitive advantage in it [Slide 25]?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Attractive sector: high barriers keep profitability high for those inside.</li><li>Competitive advantage: ComfortAuto has transferable strengths (engineering, cheap inputs, spare capacity) but lacks certification, reputation and contracts.</li><li>Related diversification: a gradual entry (a niche, a partnership or acquiring a small certified firm) is more realistic.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The diversification answers the two questions of [Slide 25] differently. <strong>Attractiveness:</strong> the same barriers that block ComfortAuto keep the industry profitable for those inside, so the sector is attractive. <strong>Competitive advantage:</strong> ComfortAuto brings real strengths (seat engineering, low-cost inputs, spare capacity), which makes this a <strong>related diversification</strong>, but it lacks certification, reputation and access to contracts. Entering head-on would be slow and risky; a more realistic path is to start in a niche (crew seats, business-jet seats), partner with an incumbent, or acquire a small certified firm.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C14", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "strategy-levels", "unit": 2, "title": "Nordic MRO Group: corporate strategy, business strategy and levels of decision", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "corporate-strategy", "business-strategy"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 8-10 and 16-19", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C14 - Nordic MRO Group Levels of Strategy.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>At its annual meeting, the Nordic MRO Group took these decisions: (1) to open a maintenance base in Morocco to serve African airlines; (2) to buy the company that overhauls the landing gear it used to outsource; (3) to compete in its engine-overhaul business by guaranteeing the shortest turnaround time in Europe, even at higher prices; (4) to schedule a second shift in the paint hangar during the summer; (5) to change the supplier of cleaning products next month.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify decisions 1–3 as corporate or business strategy, naming the dimension of scope or the source of advantage involved</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Corporate strategy decides <strong>where</strong> the firm competes (its scope: geographical, vertical, product); business strategy decides <strong>how</strong> it competes (cost or differentiation) [Slides 9, 17-19].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>1: corporate strategy, geographical scope.</li><li>2: corporate strategy, vertical scope (backward integration).</li><li>3: business strategy, differentiation advantage (turnaround time, price premium).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Type [Slides 9, 18]</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Base in Morocco</td><td><strong>Corporate strategy</strong></td><td><strong>Geographical scope</strong> (internationalization), part of the question \"which businesses should we be in?\"</td></tr><tr><td>2. Buying the landing-gear overhauler</td><td><strong>Corporate strategy</strong></td><td><strong>Vertical scope</strong> (backward vertical integration)</td></tr><tr><td>3. Shortest turnaround at higher prices</td><td><strong>Business (competitive) strategy</strong></td><td><strong>Differentiation</strong>: a valued, unique feature that earns a price premium [Slides 20, 23]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Corporate strategy answers \"which businesses should we be in?\" and business strategy \"how should we compete?\" [Slide 17].</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Place decisions 4 and 5 in the levels of decision, and state what makes a strategy successful</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Strategic, tactic and operative decisions [Slide 8]; the three pillars of a successful strategy [Slide 10].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>4: tactic decision (one area; it implements the strategy).</li><li>5: operative decision (routine).</li><li>The deck only names the three levels; their characterisation is the usual reading (extension).</li><li>Success: long-term, simple and agreed objectives; deep understanding of the competitive environment; objective appraisal of resources; effective implementation.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Decisions 1–3 are <strong>strategic</strong>: long-term, affecting the whole firm. Decision 4 (a summer shift in one hangar) is <strong>tactic</strong>: it concerns one area over a season and implements the strategy. Decision 5 (a cleaning supplier next month) is <strong>operative</strong>: routine and short term. The deck names the three levels [Slide 8]; this characterisation of each one is the usual reading (extension).</p>\n<p>A strategy succeeds through <strong>effective implementation</strong> resting on <strong>long-term, simple and agreed objectives</strong>, a <strong>deep understanding of the competitive environment</strong> and an <strong>objective appraisal of resources</strong> [Slide 10]. Nordic's differentiation on turnaround time, for instance, only works if it has the hangar capacity and staff (resources) to deliver it.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C15", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "cost-advantage", "unit": 2, "title": "LowOrbit: the drivers behind a cost-leadership strategy in satellite buses", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "cost-leadership", "competitive-advantage"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 20-22", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C15 - LowOrbit Satellite Bus Cost Leadership.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>LowOrbit S.A. sells the \"bus\" (platform) of small Earth-observation satellites at 40% below its rivals. It builds one standard design with interchangeable modules instead of custom platforms, and has designed every part to be machined in a single setup. Its line now produces 120 buses a year, and the hours per bus have fallen by half since the first 20 units as the teams repeated the same tasks. It bought a solar-cell supplier located next to the plant, keeps the factory running in three shifts, and its managers are paid for removing waste in every process.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the drivers of cost advantage present in LowOrbit</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Economies of learning, economies of scale, production techniques, product design, input costs, capacity utilization, residual efficiency [Slides 21-22].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Product design: one standard design, interchangeable modules, design for manufacture.</li><li>Economies of scale: 120 buses a year.</li><li>Economies of learning: hours per bus halved with repetition.</li><li>Input costs: ownership of a low-cost input (solar cells), location.</li><li>Capacity utilization: three shifts.</li><li>Residual efficiency: managers paid to remove waste (motivation, culture).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Driver [Slides 21-22]</th><th>Evidence at LowOrbit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Product design</strong></td><td>Standardization of designs and components; parts designed for manufacture (single setup)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Economies of scale</strong></td><td>120 buses a year: specialization and division of labour</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Economies of learning</strong></td><td>Hours per bus halved as routines improved</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Input costs</strong></td><td>Ownership of a low-cost input (the solar-cell supplier) and a location advantage</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Capacity utilization</strong></td><td>Three shifts spread the fixed costs over more units</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Residual efficiency</strong></td><td>Managerial efficiency, motivation and culture of eliminating waste</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain what a cost advantage is and what risk LowOrbit runs with this strategy</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compare the two sources of competitive advantage [Slide 20].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cost advantage: offering a similar product at a lower cost than rivals.</li><li>Risk: standardization limits customization; customers who value unique features may prefer differentiated rivals; rivals may copy the cost drivers.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>A <strong>cost advantage</strong> means offering a <strong>similar product at a lower cost</strong> than competitors, whereas a differentiation advantage obtains a <strong>price premium from a unique product</strong> [Slide 20]. LowOrbit's standard bus is its main cost driver, but it is also its main risk: customers whose missions need a custom platform will go to differentiated rivals, and competitors can copy standardization and scale. To keep the advantage it must keep reducing costs faster than its rivals (learning, scale) while keeping the product \"similar enough\" in performance to theirs.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C16", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "differentiation", "unit": 2, "title": "Aurum Interiors: tangible and intangible differentiation in business jets", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "differentiation", "competitive-advantage"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 20 and 23-24", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C16 - Aurum Business-Jet Interiors Differentiation.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Aurum Interiors S.L. designs cabins for private jets and charges about twice what its competitors charge. Its cabins use sound-absorbing panels that make them the quietest in the market and seats 20% lighter than average, and they are delivered with a personalised maintenance plan and a 24-hour technical hotline. Each cabin is signed by a well-known designer, carries a numbered plaque, and owners are invited to an exclusive yearly event. Aurum's account managers accompany each customer from the first sketch to years after delivery.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify the sources of Aurum's differentiation as tangible or intangible</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Tangible differentiation rests on observable characteristics (features, performance, packaging, complementary services); intangible differentiation appeals to image, status, identity and exclusivity [Slide 24].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Tangible: quietness (performance), lighter seats (materials/performance), maintenance plan and hotline (complementary services).</li><li>Intangible: designer's signature, numbered plaque, exclusive event (status, exclusivity).</li><li>The account managers: the whole supplier–customer relationship.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Type [Slide 24]</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Quietest cabins (sound-absorbing panels)</td><td>Tangible</td><td>Performance, observable and measurable</td></tr><tr><td>Seats 20% lighter</td><td>Tangible</td><td>Materials and performance</td></tr><tr><td>Maintenance plan and 24-hour hotline</td><td>Tangible</td><td>Complementary services</td></tr><tr><td>Designer's signature, numbered plaque</td><td>Intangible</td><td>Image, status, desire for exclusivity</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusive yearly event</td><td>Intangible</td><td>Identity and status of belonging to a select group</td></tr><tr><td>Account managers from sketch to after-sales</td><td>Whole relationship</td><td>Total customer responsiveness [Slide 24]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain how differentiation allows Aurum to charge twice the price, and what it must protect</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Porter: \"providing something unique that is valuable to the buyer beyond simply offering a low price\" [Slide 23].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Differentiation creates value for the customer, which earns a price premium [Slide 20].</li><li>The premium must exceed the extra cost of differentiating.</li><li>Aurum must protect what is valuable and hard to copy: reputation, design, relationship.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Differentiation means <strong>providing something unique that the buyer values</strong> beyond a low price [Slide 23]; because the customer values it, they accept a <strong>price premium</strong> [Slide 20]. Aurum's quiet, light cabins and its exclusive image create that value, so jet owners pay double. The strategy works only while the premium exceeds the extra cost of the panels, designers and services, and while the uniqueness lasts. The tangible features can eventually be copied; the intangible ones (brand, designer, relationship with owners) are harder to imitate and are what Aurum must protect.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C17", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "diversification", "unit": 2, "title": "Vega Avionics: reasons for and types of diversification", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "diversification", "corporate-strategy"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 25-26", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C17 - Vega Avionics Diversifying into Medical Devices.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Vega Avionics S.A. makes flight-control electronics. Its sales follow the aircraft-order cycle: in good years profits soar, in crises they collapse. The board studies three moves: (A) launching a line of electronic monitors for hospital intensive-care units, using its expertise in certified, fault-tolerant electronics; (B) opening a sales office in India to sell its current avionics to Indian aircraft makers; (C) buying a chain of hotels near airports because \"it is profitable and has nothing to do with aircraft cycles\".</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Give the reasons why Vega may want to diversify, and classify moves A, B and C</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Reasons: growth, risk, profit [Slide 25]. Types: related vs unrelated; forms: vertical, horizontal, geographical [Slide 26].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Reasons: risk (volatile earnings tied to the aircraft cycle), growth, profit.</li><li>A: horizontal diversification, related (shared technology).</li><li>B: geographical diversification of existing products, related.</li><li>C: horizontal diversification, unrelated.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Firms diversify for three reasons [Slide 25]: <strong>growth</strong>, <strong>risk</strong> (diversification reduces the volatility of earnings) and <strong>profit</strong>. Vega's main motive is <strong>risk</strong>: its earnings depend on one cycle, and businesses that do not follow it would stabilise them; growth and profit are secondary motives.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Move</th><th>Form [Slide 26]</th><th>Related or unrelated</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A. Hospital monitors</td><td>Horizontal diversification (new industry)</td><td><strong>Related</strong>: it expands Vega's existing line of certified electronics into a new market [Slide 26]</td></tr><tr><td>B. Sales office in India</td><td>Geographical diversification (new market)</td><td><strong>Related</strong>: same products, new market</td></tr><tr><td>C. Airport hotels</td><td>Horizontal diversification (new industry)</td><td><strong>Unrelated</strong>: it adds a new, unrelated product line [Slide 26]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Evaluate move C with the two questions of a diversification decision</p>", "hint_html": "<p>How attractive is the sector, and can Vega achieve a competitive advantage in it [Slide 25]?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>The hotel sector may be attractive, but Vega has no source of competitive advantage there.</li><li>Unrelated diversification reduces risk; whether it adds profitability depends on achieving an advantage in the new sector.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>A diversification decision asks <strong>how attractive the sector is</strong> and <strong>whether the firm can achieve a competitive advantage in it</strong> [Slide 25]. Hotels near airports may be profitable, but Vega brings nothing that would give it an advantage over hotel chains: no shared technology, customers or skills. The move would lower the volatility of earnings, but shareholders could achieve that themselves by investing in hotel companies (an argument beyond the slides). Move A, related to Vega's know-how, answers both questions far better.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C18", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "vertical-integration", "unit": 2, "title": "A launcher maker buys its engine supplier: vertical integration", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "vertical-integration", "corporate-strategy"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 19 and 26-27", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C18 - Launcher Maker Buying its Engine Supplier.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Helios Launch S.A. builds medium rockets. Its engines come from a single independent supplier that has raised prices twice in two years and delayed deliveries, and the two firms argue about who must pay for each design change. Helios is negotiating to buy the supplier. A director objects: engine technology is changing fast, the supplier also sells engines to other launch companies, and Helios has no experience in managing a factory of that size. Another director proposes, instead, to also buy the satellite-integration company that prepares customers' payloads before launch.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the type of vertical integration in each proposal and the advantages Helios seeks with the purchase of the engine supplier</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Backward integration moves toward the inputs; forward integration toward the customer [Slide 27].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Buying the engine supplier: backward vertical integration; buying payload integration: forward vertical integration.</li><li>Advantages: control of inputs, reduction of transaction costs (disputes over design changes), independence, better competitive position, reduction of information asymmetries.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The deck names forward and backward vertical integration [Slide 27]; in the standard reading (extension), backward integration moves toward the inputs and forward integration toward the customer. Buying the engine supplier is therefore <strong>backward vertical integration</strong>, and buying the satellite-integration company would be <strong>forward vertical integration</strong>. Both change the <strong>vertical scope</strong> of the firm, replacing a market interface with an administrative one [Slide 19].</p>\n<p>The advantages Helios seeks with the engine supplier [Slide 27]:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>control of inputs</strong>: no more unexpected price rises or delays;</li><li><strong>reduction of transaction costs</strong>: the disputes over design changes become internal decisions;</li><li><strong>independence</strong> from a single supplier and a <strong>better competitive position</strong>;</li><li><strong>reduction of information asymmetries</strong>: Helios will know the real costs and status of the engines.</li></ul>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Use the disadvantages of vertical integration to evaluate the director's objection</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Increased risk, decreased flexibility, loss of specialization advantages, increased administrative costs [Slide 27].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Decreased flexibility: tied to its own engine technology when it changes fast.</li><li>Loss of specialization advantages: the supplier's scale from selling to other launchers may be lost.</li><li>Increased administrative costs and risk: managing a large new factory without experience.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The director's points match the <strong>disadvantages</strong> of vertical integration [Slide 27]. When engine technology changes fast, owning the factory means <strong>decreased flexibility</strong>: Helios cannot simply switch to a better engine on the market. If rival launch companies stop buying from a supplier owned by a competitor, the factory loses volume and the <strong>advantages of specialization</strong>. Running a large factory without experience brings <strong>increased administrative costs</strong> and <strong>increased risk</strong>. A balanced conclusion: integration is worth it if engines are critical and the supplier abuses its power, but Helios should first consider alternatives such as a long-term contract or a second supplier.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C19", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "value-chain", "unit": 2, "title": "SkyEye Drones: mapping activities onto Porter's value chain", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "value-chain", "porter"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 2_ Value creation.pdf, Slides 28-31", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C19 - SkyEye Drone Maker Value Chain.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>SkyEye Drones S.L. makes surveying drones. Its activities include: (1) receiving and storing motors and cameras from suppliers; (2) assembling and testing the drones; (3) packing and shipping them to customers; (4) online advertising and demonstrations to surveying firms; (5) repairs and software updates after sale; (6) negotiating contracts with component suppliers; (7) recruiting and training technicians; (8) developing the flight software and testing new materials; (9) accounting, legal advice, quality assurance and general management.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify each activity as a primary or a support activity of the value chain, naming it</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Primary: inbound logistics, operations, outbound logistics, marketing and sales, service [Slide 30]. Support: procurement, human resource management, technological development, infrastructure [Slide 31].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Primary: 1 inbound logistics, 2 operations, 3 outbound logistics, 4 marketing and sales, 5 service.</li><li>Support: 6 procurement, 7 HRM, 8 technological development, 9 infrastructure.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Value-chain activity</th><th>Type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Receiving and storing components</td><td>Inbound logistics</td><td>Primary [Slide 30]</td></tr><tr><td>2. Assembling and testing</td><td>Operations</td><td>Primary</td></tr><tr><td>3. Packing and shipping</td><td>Outbound logistics</td><td>Primary</td></tr><tr><td>4. Advertising and demonstrations</td><td>Marketing and sales</td><td>Primary</td></tr><tr><td>5. Repairs and updates after sale</td><td>Service</td><td>Primary</td></tr><tr><td>6. Negotiating with suppliers</td><td>Procurement</td><td>Support [Slide 31]</td></tr><tr><td>7. Recruiting and training</td><td>Human resource management</td><td>Support</td></tr><tr><td>8. Flight software and materials</td><td>Technological development</td><td>Support</td></tr><tr><td>9. Accounting, legal, quality, management</td><td>Infrastructure</td><td>Support</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain what the value chain is for and where SkyEye's margin comes from</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The chain arranges resources into activities customers are willing to pay for; the management of the activities and of their linkages gives the competitive advantage [Slides 28-29].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>The activities together produce the margin [Slide 29], read (extension) as what customers pay minus the cost of all activities.</li><li>Competitive advantage comes from managing the activities and their linkages (e.g. software development linked to after-sales updates).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The value chain (Porter, 1985) shows that a firm is not a random collection of resources but a set of <strong>activities</strong> that produce goods customers are willing to pay for; together they produce the <strong>margin</strong> shown at the end of the chain [Slide 29], usually read (extension) as the difference between what customers pay and the cost of all the activities. The <strong>management of the activities and of their linkages</strong> is what gives the competitive advantage: for SkyEye, the link between technological development (activity 8) and service (activity 5) allows software updates that keep old drones competitive, a reason for customers to pay more.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C20", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "economic-financial-structure", "unit": 3, "title": "Rotorline: investing and financing decisions, economic and financial structure", "difficulty": 1, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "economic-structure", "financial-structure"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 3-6", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C20 - Rotorline Economic and Financial Structure.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>The finance manager of Rotorline S.A., a helicopter-tour operator, has two items on her desk this week. First, whether to buy a fourth helicopter now or wait until next spring, and whether to replace the old hangar. Second, how to pay for whatever is bought: a bank has offered a five-year loan, the shareholders could put in more money, or the company could keep this year's profit instead of paying dividends. She knows that the helicopter would earn about 9% per year and that the money to buy it would cost, on average, about 7%.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Distinguish the two types of decisions the finance manager faces and link each one to a side of the balance sheet</p>", "hint_html": "<p>What to invest in and when? How to obtain the money to invest? [Slide 3]. Economic and financial structure [Slides 4-5].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Investing decisions (helicopter, hangar, timing) → economic structure → assets (what is owned).</li><li>Financing decisions (loan, new equity, retained profit) → financial structure → equity and liabilities (what is owed).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The finance manager faces the two decisions of the finance area [Slide 3]:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Investing decisions</strong> — <em>what to invest in and when?</em>: the fourth helicopter (now or in spring) and the new hangar. They define the <strong>economic structure</strong>, the use of the financial resources in goods and rights: the <strong>assets</strong>, what the company owns [Slides 4-5].</li><li><strong>Financing decisions</strong> — <em>how to obtain the money?</em>: bank loan, new equity from shareholders, or retained profit. They define the <strong>financial structure</strong>, the sources of financing: <strong>equity and liabilities</strong>, what the company owes [Slides 4-5, 6].</li></ul>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Use the 9% and 7% figures to explain the objective of the company in financial terms</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Assets produce profitability; equity and liabilities have a cost [Slide 5].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Assets generate profitability; financing has a cost.</li><li>Objective: maximize value, which requires profitability &gt; cost.</li><li>9% &gt; 7%: the helicopter creates value.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The <strong>assets</strong> produce <strong>profitability</strong>, while <strong>equity and liabilities</strong> have a <strong>cost</strong>; the objective of the company is to <strong>maximize value</strong>, which requires that <strong>profitability exceed cost</strong> [Slide 5]. The new helicopter would earn about 9% per year while the funds to buy it cost about 7%, so it creates value: each year it returns 2 points more than it costs. If the cheapest financing available cost more than 9%, the same helicopter would destroy value, which is why the investing and financing decisions must be taken together.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C21", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "financing-map", "unit": 3, "title": "Nacelle Works: placing seven sources of financing on the map", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "financial-structure", "sources-of-financing"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 21-35", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C21 - Nacelle Works Mapping its Sources of Financing.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Nacelle Works S.A. manufactures engine nacelles. Its CFO lists how the company was financed last year: (1) profits of previous years kept in the company; (2) a capital increase subscribed by a venture fund; (3) bonds maturing in 2032; (4) a seven-year bank loan for a new autoclave; (5) a contract to use a robotic drilling cell for five years with an option to buy it at the end; (6) suppliers of aluminium who are paid at 90 days; (7) customer invoices sold to a specialised company that collects them and bears the risk if the customer does not pay.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify each source by nature, by origin and by term</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Nature: equity or liabilities [Slide 21]; origin: company resources or external resources [Slide 22]; term: long or short [Slides 29-35].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Equity, company resources: (1) self-financing (internal); (2) equity capital (external equity).</li><li>Liabilities, external, long term: (3) bonds, (4) long-term loan, (5) leasing.</li><li>Liabilities, external, short term: (6) accounts payable (trade credit), (7) factoring.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Instrument</th><th>Nature [Slide 21]</th><th>Origin [Slide 22]</th><th>Term</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Self-financing (retained earnings)</td><td>Equity</td><td>Company resources (internal equity)</td><td>Permanent</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Equity capital, rights issue</td><td>Equity</td><td>Company resources (external equity)</td><td>Permanent</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Bonds</td><td>Liabilities</td><td>External</td><td>Long term [Slide 29]</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Long-term bank loan</td><td>Liabilities</td><td>External</td><td>Long term [Slide 31]</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Leasing (use with purchase option)</td><td>Liabilities</td><td>External</td><td>Long term [Slide 33]</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Accounts payable (trade credit)</td><td>Liabilities</td><td>External</td><td>Short term [Slide 35]</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Factoring</td><td>Liabilities</td><td>External</td><td>Short term [Slide 35]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain the difference between a bond and a long-term loan, and between factoring and a trade discount</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A bond is a debt <strong>security</strong>; factoring transfers the risk of insolvency [Slides 29, 31, 35].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Bond: a loan in the form of a security; the issuer owes the holders principal and coupon at maturity.</li><li>Loan: an amount received and paid back at a certain time, with debt interest.</li><li>Factoring: the credit is sold and the factor assumes the insolvency risk.</li><li>Trade discount: the bank advances the amount of a bill before maturity after deducting expenses; the risk stays with the company.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>A <strong>bond</strong> is a <strong>debt security</strong>: a loan in the form of a security, in which the issuer is the borrower and the bondholder the lender; the issuer must repay the principal and pay the coupon at maturity [Slide 29]. With a <strong>long-term loan</strong>, the company receives an amount that must be paid back at a certain time and pays <strong>debt interest</strong> for it [Slide 31].</p>\n<p>With <strong>factoring</strong>, the company <strong>sells</strong> its credit to a factoring company, which collects it and <strong>assumes the risk of insolvency</strong> [Slide 35]. With a <strong>trade discount</strong>, the bank only <strong>advances</strong> the amount of a bill before it falls due, after deducting interest and commission [Slide 35]; if the customer does not pay, the company usually has to return the money (discount with recourse, extension).</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C22", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "self-financing", "unit": 3, "title": "Talon Avionics: pay a dividend or keep the profit?", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "self-financing", "dividends"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 23-26 and 37", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C22 - Talon Avionics Dividend or Self-Financing.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Talon Avionics S.A. earned a profit of 800,000 m.u. in 2025. Its retired founding shareholders, who live on the dividends, ask the board to distribute 600,000 m.u. The CEO wants to distribute only 160,000 m.u. and keep the rest to develop a new radar altimeter next year; otherwise she would have to ask a bank for a loan or issue new shares, which the founders dislike because they would lose part of their control.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the pay-out and self-financing ratios of each proposal</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Pay-out = dividends / profits; self-financing = retained earnings / profits [Slide 23].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Founders: pay-out 75%, self-financing 25%.</li><li>CEO: pay-out 20%, self-financing 80%.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Proposal</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Dividends</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Retained</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Pay-out</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Self-financing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Founders</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">200,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$600{,}000/800{,}000 = 75\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$200{,}000/800{,}000 = 25\\%$</td></tr><tr><td>CEO</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">160,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">640,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$160{,}000/800{,}000 = 20\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$640{,}000/800{,}000 = 80\\%$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain the advantages and drawbacks of self-financing compared with a bank loan or a rights issue, and give your recommendation</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Think about repayment, explicit vs implicit cost, control, and the fact that paying dividends is not compulsory [Slides 23-26, 37].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Self-financing: internal equity generated by the activity; no repayment, no explicit cost, no new owners; but it has an opportunity cost for shareholders and depends on profits.</li><li>Loan: explicit cost (interest), must be repaid, increases leverage and risk.</li><li>Rights issue: no repayment, but new shareholders dilute control.</li><li>Dividends are not compulsory; a balanced pay-out may be agreed.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p><strong>Self-financing</strong> (retained earnings) is internal equity generated by the company's normal activity [Slide 23]. It does not have to be repaid, carries no <strong>explicit</strong> cost and brings in no new owners, so the founders keep their control; its cost is <strong>implicit</strong>: the shareholders give up dividends today (an opportunity cost, [Slide 37]).</p>\n<p>The alternatives have other drawbacks: a <strong>bank loan</strong> carries an explicit cost (interest), must be repaid, and raises leverage and risk [Slides 31, 36]; a <strong>rights issue</strong> need not be repaid but brings in new shareholders who share future dividends [Slides 25-26] and voting power (extension).</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> since paying dividends is not compulsory [Slide 26] and the founders want to keep control, the CEO's high self-financing is the way to fund the project that needs no repayment, carries no explicit cost and keeps control in their hands. A compromise (for example a 30–40% pay-out) can still meet part of the founders' income needs if the project does not require all 640,000 m.u. (a suggestion beyond the course).</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C23", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "long-short-financing", "unit": 3, "title": "A flight school's simulator: leasing, a long-term loan or a credit line?", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "leasing", "bank-financing"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 31, 33 and 35", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C23 - Flight School Simulator Leasing Loan or Credit Line.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Cumulus Flight School S.L. needs a full-flight simulator that will be used for about eight years. A bank offers three options: (A) a long-term loan for the full price, repaid over eight years with interest; (B) a leasing contract: the school uses the simulator in exchange for monthly payments for eight years and can buy it at the end for a small amount; (C) a credit line with a limit equal to the price, renewable every year, on which interest is paid only on the amount drawn. Separately, the school expects short cash gaps every winter, when fewer students enrol.</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Describe each of the three instruments</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Long-term loans and bank credits [Slide 31]; leasing [Slide 33]; short-term bank financing [Slide 35].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Loan: an amount received and repaid at a certain time, paying debt interest.</li><li>Leasing: use of a fixed asset for periodic payments; the lessee has the right to buy at the end; finance or operating leasing.</li><li>Credit line: the bank makes a maximum available; interest only on the amount borrowed; higher rates.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<ul><li><strong>(A) Long-term loan:</strong> the school receives an amount that must be paid back at a certain time and pays for the service through the <strong>debt interest</strong> [Slide 31].</li><li><strong>(B) Leasing:</strong> the school obtains the <strong>use of a fixed asset</strong> in exchange for contractual, periodic payments; as lessee, it has the <strong>right to buy</strong> the asset at the end of the period. It can be <strong>finance</strong> or <strong>operating</strong> leasing [Slide 33].</li><li><strong>(C) Bank credit (credit line):</strong> the bank makes a <strong>maximum amount</strong> available; the school borrows what it needs up to that limit and pays interest <strong>only on the amount borrowed</strong>; interest rates are higher. Renewed yearly, it is short-term bank financing [Slides 31, 35].</li></ul>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Recommend how to finance the simulator and the winter cash gaps</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Match the term of the financing to the life of what it finances.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Simulator (8-year asset): long-term financing, loan or leasing; leasing also offers flexibility and the purchase option.</li><li>Winter gaps (short, recurring): the credit line, since interest is paid only on the amount drawn.</li><li>Financing a long-lived asset with a yearly-renewable credit line is risky and expensive.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The simulator will be used for eight years, so it should be financed with <strong>long-term</strong> resources: either the <strong>loan</strong> (A) or the <strong>leasing</strong> (B), whose eight-year payments match its useful life. Leasing is attractive for a flight school because it obtains the use of the asset [Slide 33] without a large initial outlay (reasoning applied to the case, beyond the slides) and keeps the <strong>option to buy</strong> it at the end. Financing it with the <strong>credit line</strong> (C) would be a mistake: the line must be renewed every year, so the bank could refuse (reasoning applied to the case, beyond the slides), and its interest rates are higher [Slide 31].</p>\n<p>The <strong>winter cash gaps</strong>, instead, are short and recurring, so the <strong>credit line</strong> is the right tool: the school draws only what it needs for a few weeks and pays interest <strong>only on the amount borrowed</strong> [Slide 31].</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "C24", "category": "short_theory_case", "archetype": "theory-case", "theme": "accrual-classification", "unit": 3, "title": "Radome Composites: revenues vs collections and current vs non-current items", "difficulty": 2, "points": 2.0, "time_minutes": 15, "tags": ["theory-case", "accrual-vs-cash", "balance-sheet"], "official": false, "source": "Practice case written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), based on Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 7-8 and 12-17", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank C24 - Radome Composites Accruals and Classification.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>The owner of Radome Composites S.L., which makes radar domes for aircraft noses, says: \"In December we delivered 20 domes to an airline that will pay in February, so they are not sales of this year. We received in November a 30,000 m.u. advance for domes to be delivered next March, so that is income of this year. The electricity of December will be paid in January, so it is a cost of next year. And the five-year bank loan is all a long-term debt, even the instalment due next June.\"</p>", "setup_html": null, "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Correct the owner's four statements using the accrual principle and the classification of the balance sheet</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Revenues are recorded when the sale is made, expenses when goods and services are received, whether paid or not [Slide 8]. Current items are settled within one year [Slides 14-16].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>December deliveries: revenue of this year; the amount owed is an account receivable (current asset).</li><li>November advance: not revenue yet; a liability (unearned revenue) until delivery in March.</li><li>December electricity: expense of this year; unpaid amount is a current liability.</li><li>Loan: the instalment due next June is a current liability; the rest is long-term.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Owner's statement</th><th>Correction</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>December deliveries are not sales of this year</td><td><strong>They are revenue of this year</strong>: the sale was made in December. The amount owed is an <strong>account receivable</strong>, a current asset</td><td>Revenues are recorded when the sale is made, paid or not [Slide 8]; current assets [Slide 14]</td></tr><tr><td>The November advance is income of this year</td><td><strong>It is not revenue yet</strong>: no domes have been delivered. It is a <strong>liability</strong> (unearned revenue) until March</td><td>Liabilities include unearned revenues [Slide 13]</td></tr><tr><td>December electricity is a cost of next year</td><td><strong>It is an expense of this year</strong>: the service was received in December. The unpaid amount is a <strong>current liability</strong></td><td>Expenses are recorded when the services are received [Slide 8]</td></tr><tr><td>The whole loan is long-term</td><td><strong>The instalment due next June is a current liability</strong>; only the instalments due after one year are long-term</td><td>Current liabilities are paid within the coming year [Slide 16]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain why the income statement and the balance sheet need these rules</p>", "hint_html": "<p>What does each statement report, and over what time [Slides 7, 12]?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>The income statement reports performance over a period: it must match the revenues and expenses of that period, not cash movements.</li><li>The balance sheet reports the financial position at a point in time: classifying by maturity shows liquidity.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>The <strong>income statement</strong> reports the <strong>performance</strong> of the business over a specific period [Slide 7]: it must contain the revenues earned and the expenses incurred in that period, whatever happens to the cash, otherwise the profit of a year would depend on when customers happen to pay. The <strong>balance sheet</strong> reports the <strong>financial position at a particular point in time</strong> [Slide 12]: splitting assets and liabilities into current and non-current shows whether the company can meet the debts due within the year with the resources that become cash within the year.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": null}, {"id": "P01", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Exercise 1: revenues vs collections, expenses vs payments, depreciation", "difficulty": 1, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 25, "tags": ["official-exercise", "accrual-vs-cash", "depreciation"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 1 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Exercise 1 - Accrual vs Cash Basis and Linear Depreciation)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P01 - T3 Exercise 1 Accrual vs Cash and Depreciation.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-financial-statements.html#exercise-1", "statement_html": "<p>A company started activity on 1 January of the present year. At 31 March it has the following goods, acquired on 31 December of the previous year:</p>\n<ul><li>factory building 1,000 m.u., duration 25 years;</li><li>office building 1,500 m.u., duration 50 years;</li><li>machinery 300 m.u., duration 10 years;</li><li>transport equipment 100 m.u., duration 5 years.</li></ul>\n<p>Information for the first quarter:</p>\n<ul><li>personnel salaries of the quarter 450 m.u., of which 75 m.u. are pending to be paid after 31/3;</li><li>materials purchased and used in production 700 m.u., of which 125 m.u. are pending to be paid after 31/3;</li><li>energy consumption estimated at 230 m.u., nothing paid to date;</li><li>sales invoiced to customers 1,930 m.u., of which 315 m.u. had not been collected at 31/3; the rest was collected in due time. In manufacturing all the units sold, all the purchased materials were consumed.</li></ul>\n<p>Ignore corporation taxes.</p>\n<p>a) At 31/3, find the income, expenses, collections (cash inflows), payments (cash outflows), investments and depreciation of the quarter (linear depreciation along the lifetime of the goods).</p>\n<p>b) How does the answer change if only half of the purchased materials had been consumed in manufacturing the units sold?</p>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Period:</strong> first quarter (1/01 to 31/03), $\\frac{3}{12} = \\frac{1}{4}$ of a year. The fixed assets were bought on 31/12 of the previous year, so they are used during the <strong>whole</strong> quarter.</p>\n<p>The exercise trains the distinction of [Slide 8]: a <strong>revenue</strong> is recorded when the sale is made, whether or not it is collected; an <strong>expense</strong> when the good or service is received or consumed, whether or not it is paid. Collections and payments are the cash side of the same operations.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Revenue / expense of Q1</th><th>Collection / payment in Q1</th><th>Pending at 31/3</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales</td><td>Revenue 1,930</td><td>Collection $1{,}930 - 315 = 1{,}615$</td><td>Receivable 315</td></tr><tr><td>Salaries</td><td>Expense 450</td><td>Payment $450 - 75 = 375$</td><td>Salaries payable 75</td></tr><tr><td>Materials (all consumed)</td><td>Expense 700</td><td>Payment $700 - 125 = 575$</td><td>Supplier payable 125</td></tr><tr><td>Energy</td><td>Expense 230</td><td>Payment 0</td><td>Energy payable 230</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed assets</td><td>Depreciation 30</td><td>0</td><td>Net value $2{,}900 - 30$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the depreciation of the quarter of each fixed asset</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Linear depreciation without residual value: cost / years of life, times 3/12 for a quarter.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Annual depreciation 120 m.u.; quarter 30 m.u.</li><li>Factory 10.0; office 7.5; machinery 7.5; transport 5.0 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Depreciation of the quarter</h4>\n<p>For each asset, with no residual value given:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{quarter}} = \\frac{\\text{Cost}}{\\text{Years of life}} \\times \\frac{3}{12}$$</div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Asset</th><th>Annual depreciation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Quarter</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Factory building</td><td>$1{,}000 / 25 = 40$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$40 / 4 = 10.0$</td></tr><tr><td>Office building</td><td>$1{,}500 / 50 = 30$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$30 / 4 = 7.5$</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery</td><td>$300 / 10 = 30$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$30 / 4 = 7.5$</td></tr><tr><td>Transport equipment</td><td>$100 / 5 = 20$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$20 / 4 = 5.0$</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>120</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>30.0</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 2.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the income, expenses, collections, payments and investments of the quarter</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Income and expenses follow the accrual principle; collections and payments only count what moved cash. The assets were bought before the quarter began.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income 1,930; expenses 1,410 m.u.; profit 520 m.u.</li><li>Collections 1,615; payments 950 m.u.; investments of the quarter 0</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Part (a)</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Income (revenues):</strong> the sales invoiced, <strong>1,930 m.u.</strong></li><li><strong>Expenses:</strong> $450 + 700 + 230 + 30 = $ <strong>1,410 m.u.</strong> (so the profit of the quarter is $1{,}930 - 1{,}410 = 520$ m.u.).</li><li><strong>Collections:</strong> $1{,}930 - 315 = $ <strong>1,615 m.u.</strong></li><li><strong>Payments:</strong> $(450 - 75) + (700 - 125) + 0 = 375 + 575 = $ <strong>950 m.u.</strong> Depreciation is not a payment.</li><li><strong>Investments of the quarter:</strong> <strong>0 m.u.</strong> The fixed assets (total $1{,}000 + 1{,}500 + 300 + 100 = 2{,}900$ m.u.) were acquired on 31/12 of the previous year, before the quarter began. If the question is read as \"the investment the company works with\", the answer is that 2,900 m.u.; state the reading you use in the exam.</li><li><strong>Depreciation:</strong> <strong>30 m.u.</strong></li></ul>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>How does the answer change if only half of the materials had been consumed?</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Only the material consumed is an expense; the rest is inventory. Did the payments change?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Expenses 1,060; profit 870 m.u.; inventory 350 m.u.</li><li>Collections and payments unchanged</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Part (b): only half of the materials consumed</h4>\n<p>Only the material <strong>consumed</strong> is an expense [Slide 8, Slide 10]: $700 / 2 = 350$ m.u. The other 350 m.u. stay in the warehouse as <strong>inventory</strong>, a current asset [Slide 14].</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Magnitude</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">(a)</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">(b)</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Income</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,930</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,930</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td>Expenses</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,410</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$450 + 350 + 230 + 30 = 1{,}060$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−350</td></tr><tr><td>Profit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">520</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">870</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+350</td></tr><tr><td>Collections</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,615</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,615</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td>Payments</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">950</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">950</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory at 31/3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">350</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+350</td></tr><tr><td>Depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Payments do not change: the company <strong>paid</strong> for what it <strong>bought</strong> (575 of the 700), regardless of how much it <strong>used</strong>.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Profit is not cash:</strong> in (a) the profit is 520 while the net cash generated is $1{,}615 - 950 = 665$. The difference, $665 - 520 = 145$, is explained item by item: $+30$ (depreciation, an expense without payment) $+75 + 125 + 230$ (expenses not yet paid) $- 315$ (revenue not yet collected) $= 145$.</li><li><strong>Part (b)</strong> raises the profit by 350 without any extra cash: unconsumed material is an asset, not an expense.</li><li><strong>Check of depreciation:</strong> the annual total 120 times $\\frac{1}{4}$ gives 30.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Financial Statements_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, pp. 1-2</div><p>Part (a) coincides with the key: income 1,930; collections 1,615; payments 950; expenses 1,410; depreciation 30; investments 0. In part (b) the key also states that revenue, collections and payments do not change and that the unused material appears as inventory.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Erratum in the official solution (part b)</div><p>The key gives total expenses of 1,124 for part (b), but its own items add to $450 + 350 + 230 + 30 = 1{,}060$. That is the value obtained above, and the only one consistent with a profit that rises by exactly the 350 of material that was not consumed.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P02", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Exercise 2: JUGA S.A. opening balance sheet with a patent contributed in kind", "difficulty": 1, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 20, "tags": ["official-exercise", "balance-sheet", "contributions-in-kind"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 2 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Exercise 2 - JUGA SA Opening Balance Sheet)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P02 - T3 Exercise 2 JUGA Opening Balance Sheet.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-financial-statements.html#exercise-2", "statement_html": "<p>The JUGA S.A. firm is started with a shareholders' contribution of 60,000 €. Of those, 30,000 € are paid in and deposited in a bank account, and the remaining 30,000 € correspond to the transfer of a patent. With the cash of the bank account, raw materials are purchased for 10,500 € and machinery for 15,000 €. Detail the balance sheet at the start of operations.</p>", "setup_html": "<ul><li>The capital contributed is <strong>60,000 €</strong>: 30,000 € in money and 30,000 € in kind (a patent).</li><li>The purchases are paid <strong>with the bank account</strong> (no debt is created).</li><li>It is the <strong>start</strong> of operations: nothing has been produced or sold yet, so there are no revenues or expenses and no income statement.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Assets</th><th>Liabilities</th><th>Equity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Money contributed</td><td>Bank +30,000</td><td></td><td>Capital +30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Patent contributed</td><td>Patent +30,000</td><td></td><td>Capital +30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Raw materials bought</td><td>Raw materials +10,500; bank −10,500</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Machinery bought</td><td>Machinery +15,000; bank −15,000</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each row keeps Assets = Liabilities + Equity [Slide 12]: the contributions increase both sides; the purchases only change the composition of the assets.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the bank balance and classify every item</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Only the cash part of the contribution reaches the bank; the purchases are paid from it. The patent is a non-current asset.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Bank 4,500 €</li><li>Patent and machinery non-current; raw materials and bank current; capital 60,000 € in equity</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Bank balance</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Bank} = 30{,}000 - 10{,}500 - 15{,}000 = 4{,}500 \\text{ €}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Classification</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Patent:</strong> non-current asset [Slide 15].</li><li><strong>Machinery:</strong> non-current asset (equipment) [Slide 15].</li><li><strong>Raw materials:</strong> inventory, a current asset [Slide 14]; not consumed yet, so not an expense.</li><li><strong>Bank:</strong> cash, a current asset [Slide 14].</li><li><strong>Share capital:</strong> contributed capital [Slide 17].</li></ul>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 2.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at the start of operations</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The contribution in kind (the patent) is capital exactly like the cash.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Total 60,000 € on both sides</li><li>Non-current assets 45,000 €; current assets 15,000 €</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Balance sheet at the start of operations</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Patent</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td><td>Share capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,000</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>60,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>45,000</strong></td><td><strong>Liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Raw materials</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,500</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td>Bank</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4,500</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>15,000</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>60,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>60,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balance:</strong> $45{,}000 + 15{,}000 = 60{,}000 = 60{,}000 + 0$.</li><li><strong>Typical mistake:</strong> keeping the bank at 30,000 € while also adding the machinery and the raw materials gives assets of 85,000 € against 60,000 € of equity. Spending cash on an asset never changes the total.</li><li><strong>Financial structure</strong> [Slide 36]: the company is financed 100% with equity ($L/E = 0$).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Financial Statements_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, p. 3</div><p>The balance sheet coincides with the key in every line: bank 4,500; patent 30,000; machinery 15,000; raw materials 10,500; share capital 60,000; total 60,000. The bank movements (+30,000, −15,000, −10,500) are the same.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P03", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "unordered-items", "theme": "unordered-items", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Exercise 3: Libby's Company balance sheet from a list of accounts", "difficulty": 1, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 20, "tags": ["official-exercise", "balance-sheet", "classification"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 3 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Exercise 3 - Libbys Company Balance Sheet Classification)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P03 - T3 Exercise 3 Libbys Balance Sheet Classification.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-financial-statements.html#exercise-3", "statement_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet of Libby's Company using the following figures (in euros):</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Account</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>Account</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Short-term (current) loan debts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,500</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,500</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term loan debts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td><td>Equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13,000</td></tr><tr><td>Money on its bank account</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td><td>Raw materials (purchased this year but not used yet)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,500</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500</td><td>Furniture</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,500</td></tr><tr><td>Paid-in capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td><td>Retained earnings from the year</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,500</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "setup_html": "<p>Every account is already a balance-sheet item: there are no revenues or expenses. The task is pure <strong>classification</strong> into the five blocks of [Slide 18] and a check that the totals agree.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify every account into its block of the balance sheet</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Ask of each account whether it is an asset, a liability or equity, and whether it is settled within one year.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Raw materials not used yet are inventory (current asset)</li><li>Retained earnings are equity, not a liability</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Account</th><th>Block</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment</td><td>Non-current asset</td><td>Long-lived resource [Slide 15]</td></tr><tr><td>Furniture</td><td>Non-current asset</td><td>Long-lived resource [Slide 15]</td></tr><tr><td>Raw materials (not used yet)</td><td>Current asset (inventory)</td><td>Will be used within the year [Slide 14]</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td>Current asset</td><td>Turned into cash within the year [Slide 14]</td></tr><tr><td>Bank account</td><td>Current asset (cash)</td><td>[Slide 14]</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td>Current asset (cash)</td><td>[Slide 14]</td></tr><tr><td>Paid-in capital</td><td>Equity: contributed capital</td><td>[Slide 17]</td></tr><tr><td>Retained earnings from the year</td><td>Equity: retained earnings</td><td>Earnings not paid as dividends [Slide 13]</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term loan debts</td><td>Long-term liability</td><td>Not paid within the year [Slide 16]</td></tr><tr><td>Short-term loan debts</td><td>Current liability</td><td>Paid within the year [Slide 16]</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts payable</td><td>Current liability</td><td>Owed to suppliers, short term [Slide 13, Slide 16]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the totals of each block</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Add the accounts of each block separately before building the statement.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Non-current assets 14,500; current assets 19,500 €</li><li>Equity 14,000; long-term liabilities 3,000; current liabilities 17,000 €</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Block totals</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Non-current assets} = 13{,}000 + 1{,}500 = 14{,}500$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Current assets} = 12{,}500 + 3{,}500 + 3{,}000 + 500 = 19{,}500$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Equity} = 12{,}000 + 2{,}000 = 14{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Long-term liabilities} = 3{,}000, \\qquad \\text{Current liabilities} = 15{,}500 + 1{,}500 = 17{,}000$$</div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Present the balance sheet</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Order: non-current before current assets; equity, long-term, then current liabilities.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Total 34,000 € on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Balance sheet</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13,000</td><td>Paid-in capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td>Furniture</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,500</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>14,500</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>14,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Raw materials</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,500</td><td>Long-term loan debts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,500</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Bank account</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td><td>Short-term loan debts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,500</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,500</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>19,500</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>17,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>34,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>34,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balance:</strong> $14{,}500 + 19{,}500 = 34{,}000 = 14{,}000 + 3{,}000 + 17{,}000$.</li><li><strong>Financial structure</strong> [Slide 36]: liabilities $L = 3{,}000 + 17{,}000 = 20{,}000$, equity $E = 14{,}000$, $E + L = 34{,}000$:</li></ul>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\%E = \\frac{14{,}000}{34{,}000} = 41.2\\%, \\qquad \\%L = \\frac{20{,}000}{34{,}000} = 58.8\\%, \\qquad \\frac{L}{E} = \\frac{20{,}000}{14{,}000} = 1.43$$</div>\n<p>The company has more debt than equity, and most of it (17,000 of 20,000) is due within the year: by [Slide 36], a high leverage indicates higher risk to shareholders.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> retained earnings are equity, not a liability; accounts receivable (owed <strong>to</strong> the company) are an asset, accounts payable (owed <strong>by</strong> the company) a liability.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Financial Statements_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, p. 4</div><p>The balance sheet coincides with the key in every block: current assets 19,500; non-current assets 14,500; current liabilities 17,000; non-current liabilities 3,000; owners' equity 14,000; total 34,000. The key asks only for the balance sheet, so the leverage figures in Phase 4 are an addition based on Slide 36.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P04", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "unordered-items", "theme": "unordered-items", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Exercise 4: Hawkins Partners income statement with inventory variation", "difficulty": 1, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 20, "tags": ["official-exercise", "income-statement", "inventory"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 4 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Exercise 4 - Hawkins Partners Income Statement and Inventories)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P04 - T3 Exercise 4 Hawkins Partners Income Statement.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-financial-statements.html#exercise-4", "statement_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for Hawkins Partners using the following figures (in euros), knowing that the applicable corporate tax rate is 30%:</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Initial inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td><td>6</td><td>Interests from the long-term debt</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,500</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Purchase of goods (sold 100%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">84,000</td><td>7</td><td>Wages and salaries</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,700</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Rental costs</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,000</td><td>8</td><td>Water, gas and electricity</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Property sold to another company (income)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,500</td><td>9</td><td>Sales</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">130,800</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Interests received by the company</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,200</td><td>10</td><td>Inventory at the end of the period</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "setup_html": "<p>The statement mixes the three kinds of income-statement lines of [Slide 9]: items of the primary operation (sales, goods, wages, rent, utilities), <strong>non-operating items</strong> (interests, the sale of property) [Slide 11], and the tax. It also gives the initial and final <strong>inventory</strong>, which adjust the cost of goods sold.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify every item into its line of the income statement</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Separate the cost of goods sold, the operating expenses and the non-operating items (interest, sale of property).</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Interest received, interest paid and the property sale are non-operating items</li><li>Inventories enter only through the cost of goods sold</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Line of the income statement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>9. Sales</td><td>Sales revenue</td></tr><tr><td>1, 2, 10. Inventories and purchases</td><td>Cost of goods sold</td></tr><tr><td>3, 7, 8. Rent, wages, utilities</td><td>Operating expenses [Slide 10]</td></tr><tr><td>4. Property sold (income)</td><td>Non-operating item: gain on the sale of property [Slide 11]</td></tr><tr><td>5. Interests received</td><td>Non-operating item: interest revenue [Slide 8, Slide 11]</td></tr><tr><td>6. Interests on long-term debt</td><td>Non-operating item: interest expense [Slide 11]</td></tr><tr><td>Tax 30%</td><td>Income taxes [Slide 11]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cost of goods sold</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Opening inventory + purchases − closing inventory.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cost of goods sold 84,100 €</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Cost of goods sold</h4>\n<p>The goods sold are those available (initial inventory + purchases) minus those still in stock at the end:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{COGS} = 600 + 84{,}000 - 500 = 84{,}100 \\text{ €}$$</div>\n<p>The inventory fell by $600 - 500 = 100$ €: those 100 € of goods were also sold, so they add to the purchases.</p>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 2.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Gross profit → income from operations → income before taxes → net income, with a 30% tax.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Gross profit 46,700 €; income before taxes 52,000 €</li><li>Net income 36,400 €</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Income statement</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">130,800</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">84,100</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td>$130{,}800 - 84{,}100$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>46,700</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Wages and salaries</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,700</td></tr><tr><td>− Rental costs</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Water, gas and electricity</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$46{,}700 - 14{,}900$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>31,800</strong></td></tr><tr><td>+ Gain on property sold</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,500</td></tr><tr><td>+ Interests received</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,200</td></tr><tr><td>− Interests on long-term debt</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,500</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$31{,}800 + 10{,}500 + 15{,}200 - 5{,}500$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>52,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 52{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,600</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$52{,}000 - 15{,}600$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>36,400</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Non-operating items add 20,200 €</strong> ($10{,}500 + 15{,}200 - 5{,}500$): almost 39% of the income before taxes does not come from the primary operation, which is why the slides separate them [Slide 11].</li><li><strong>The tax base</strong> is the income before taxes (52,000), not the income from operations [Slide 11].</li><li><strong>Reading of item 4:</strong> the statement labels the 10,500 € as income from the property sold; no book value of the property is given, so the whole amount is taken as the gain on the sale.</li><li><strong>Check:</strong> $130{,}800 + 10{,}500 + 15{,}200 = 156{,}500$ of revenues and gains; $84{,}100 + 14{,}900 + 5{,}500 = 104{,}500$ of expenses; $156{,}500 - 104{,}500 = 52{,}000$.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Financial Statements_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, p. 5</div><p>Cost of goods sold 84,100 (purchases 84,000 plus the inventory variation of 100), operating expenses 14,900, interests received 15,200, interests paid 5,500, income before taxes 52,000, tax 15,600 and net income 36,400 coincide with the key.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Discrepancy with the official solution (EBIT)</div><p>The key includes the 10,500 € of the property sold (labelled \"Income from rentals\") in the income from operations, so its EBIT is 42,300 €. Here the amount is shown below EBIT, as a non-operating gain, following the definition of Slide 11 (gains and losses on the sale of equipment and investments). Both layouts give the same income before taxes (52,000 €) and the same net income (36,400 €); only the EBIT subtotal changes: 31,800 € here and 42,300 € in the key. For an exam marked against this key, expect EBIT = 42,300 €.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P05", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Exercise 5: a large company's income statement with discounts, taxes and dividends", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 30, "tags": ["official-exercise", "income-statement", "dividends"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 5 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Exercise 5 - Corporate Income Statement, Taxes and Dividends)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P05 - T3 Exercise 5 Corporate Income Statement and Dividends.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-financial-statements.html#exercise-5", "statement_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement of a company with the following information for 2017:</p>\n<ul><li>Gross sales during the year reached 2,002 million €. The company's trade policy gives a 3% discount to its Type A customers, those whose annual purchases from the company exceed 120 million €. Type A customers account for 40% of sales. Assume total sales = gross sales − discount.</li><li>Purchases of raw materials amounted to 962 million €.</li><li>The workforce consists of 7,800 employees. The average salary is 30,000 € per year, and the average spending on Social Security is 30% of the salary.</li><li>Payments for other operating expenses amounted to 457 million €.</li><li>In recent years the company has invested a total of 1,200 million € in fixed assets with an average lifespan of 10 years (linear depreciation, no residual value).</li><li>The company obtained an international loan of 225 million € in 2015, at 6% interest along the whole year; the whole principal will be repaid in 2020.</li><li>The corporate tax rate is 30%. The company has agreed to a dividend of 50%.</li><li>Assume no change in stocks of raw materials or finished products during the year.</li></ul>", "setup_html": "<p>All amounts are expressed in <strong>millions of euros (M€)</strong>. Salaries are given per employee in €, so they must be converted: $1 \\text{ M€} = 10^6$ €.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>\"No change in stocks\" means initial inventory = final inventory, so $\\text{COGS} = \\text{Purchases}$.</li><li>The 120 M€ threshold only defines who is a Type A customer; the statement already says these customers make 40% of the sales, so the discount applies to that 40%.</li><li>The loan principal is not an expense: only its interest enters the income statement [Slide 11].</li><li>The dividend of 50% is applied to the <strong>net income</strong> [Slide 23].</li></ol>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Line</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gross sales − discount</td><td>Sales revenue (net)</td></tr><tr><td>Raw materials (no stock change)</td><td>Cost of goods sold</td></tr><tr><td>Salaries + Social Security</td><td>Operating expense</td></tr><tr><td>Other operating expenses</td><td>Operating expense</td></tr><tr><td>Depreciation of fixed assets</td><td>Operating expense [Slide 10]</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on the loan</td><td>Non-operating item [Slide 11]</td></tr><tr><td>Tax 30%</td><td>Income taxes</td></tr><tr><td>Dividend 50%</td><td>Distribution of net income (not an expense)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the net sales and the personnel expense</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The 3% discount applies only to Type A customers (40% of sales). Social Security is 30% on top of salaries.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Net sales 1,977.976 M€</li><li>Personnel 304.2 M€</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Net sales</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Type A sales} = 0.40 \\times 2{,}002 = 800.8 \\text{ M€}, \\qquad \\text{Discount} = 0.03 \\times 800.8 = 24.024 \\text{ M€}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Net sales} = 2{,}002 - 24.024 = 1{,}977.976 \\text{ M€}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Personnel expense</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Salaries} = 7{,}800 \\times 30{,}000 = 234{,}000{,}000 \\text{ €} = 234 \\text{ M€}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Social Security} = 0.30 \\times 234 = 70.2 \\text{ M€}, \\qquad \\text{Personnel} = 234 + 70.2 = 304.2 \\text{ M€}$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the depreciation and the interest of 2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Linear depreciation over 10 years with no residual value; the loan bears 6% over the whole year.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Depreciation 120 M€; interest 13.5 M€</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Depreciation and interest</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D = \\frac{1{,}200 - 0}{10} = 120 \\text{ M€/year}, \\qquad I = 225 \\times 0.06 = 13.5 \\text{ M€}$$</div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement of 2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>No change in stocks: purchases equal the cost of goods sold.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Gross profit 1,015.976 M€; income before taxes 121.276 M€</li><li>Net income 84.893 M€</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Income statement 2017</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">M€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Net sales</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,977.976</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold (raw materials)</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">962.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td>$1{,}977.976 - 962$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,015.976</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Personnel</td><td>Step 2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">304.200</td></tr><tr><td>− Other operating expenses</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">457.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$1{,}015.976 - 881.2$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>134.776</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>Step 3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.500</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$134.776 - 13.5$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>121.276</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 121.276$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">36.383</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$121.276 - 36.3828$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>84.893</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Distribute the net income</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A dividend of 50% of the net income; the rest is self-financing.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Dividends 42.447 M€; retained earnings 42.447 M€</li><li>Pay-out 50%, self-financing 50%</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>!Problem - T3 Exercise 5 - Corporate Income Statement, Taxes and Dividends#Step 5. Distribution of the net income [Slide 23]</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Applying the discount to all the sales</strong> would deduct $0.03 \\times 2{,}002 = 60.06$ M€ instead of 24.024: the 40% restriction matters.</li><li><strong>The loan repayment in 2020</strong> does not appear in 2017: repaying principal is a payment that is never an expense.</li><li><strong>Exact values:</strong> income taxes 36.3828 M€ and net income 84.8932 M€ (shown rounded to three decimals in the table).</li><li><strong>Dividends</strong> come after taxes and are not an expense: the income statement ends at the net income, and the 50/50 split is shown separately.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Financial Statements_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, p. 6</div><p>Every line coincides with the key: discount 24.024; net sales 1,977.976; cost of goods sold 962; personnel 304.2; other operating expenses 457; depreciation 120; EBIT 134.776; interest 13.5; income before taxes 121.276; tax 36.3828; net income 84.8932; dividends 42.4466 and retained earnings 42.4466 (all in M€).</p></div>"}, {"id": "P06", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Exercise 6: Company X full cycle and the effect of uncalled capital", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 30, "tags": ["official-exercise", "balance-sheet", "uncalled-capital", "cash"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 6 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Exercise 6 - Company X Full Cycle Balance Sheet and Uncalled Capital)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P06 - T3 Exercise 6 Company X Full Cycle and Uncalled Capital.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-financial-statements.html#exercise-6", "statement_html": "<p>Company X was incorporated on 1/01/2017 with a subscription of shares worth 100 m.u., fully paid in cash. On 1/01/2017 it bought, with cash: a) an area (land) for 20 m.u.; b) equipment with a life of 10 years (straight-line depreciation) and a purchase price of 20 m.u.; c) raw materials for 20 m.u.</p>\n<p>The company hires an employee for a total annual cost (salaries, Social Security, etc.) of 10 m.u. per year. This employee processes half of the raw material and sells it for a total of 50 m.u. Sales are the same every day of the year. The average collection period is 3 months.</p>\n<p>The single tax rate on the profits of the company is 30% (paid on 31/12/2017). The company follows a strict self-financing strategy.</p>\n<ol><li>Prepare the balance sheet at 31/12/2017, justifying and explaining the intermediate calculations.</li><li>Explain how the accounting statements would change if, instead of 100%, the shareholders had paid only 90% of the 100 m.u. subscribed.</li></ol>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Period:</strong> calendar year 2017. <strong>Closing:</strong> 31/12/2017.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>The employee's cost is paid during the year.</li><li>Land has no useful life given and is not depreciated; the equipment has no residual value given (zero).</li><li><strong>Strict self-financing</strong> [Slide 23]: the whole net income is retained (pay-out 0%).</li><li>The tax is paid on 31/12, so no tax liability remains.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Income statement 2017</th><th>Cash 2017</th><th>Open at 31/12/2017</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td>—</td><td>+100</td><td>Share capital 100</td></tr><tr><td>Land</td><td>—</td><td>−20</td><td>Land 20</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment</td><td>Depreciation 2</td><td>−20</td><td>Equipment $20 - 2$</td></tr><tr><td>Raw materials</td><td>COGS 10 (half consumed)</td><td>−20</td><td>Inventory 10</td></tr><tr><td>Employee</td><td>Personnel 10</td><td>−10</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Sales</td><td>Revenue 50</td><td>+37.5</td><td>Receivables 12.5</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>8.4</td><td>−8.4</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the depreciation, the cost of goods sold and the receivables at year end</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Only half of the raw material is processed and sold. With a 3-month collection period and even sales, 3/12 of the sales are uncollected.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Receivables 12.5 m.u.; inventory 10 m.u. left</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Depreciation and cost of goods sold</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D = \\frac{20 - 0}{10} = 2 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{COGS} = \\frac{20}{2} = 10 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Inventory} = 20 - 10 = 10 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Receivables from the collection period</h4>\n<p>Sales are spread evenly over the year and customers pay 3 months after buying. At 31/12 the sales of the last 3 months are still unpaid:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Receivables} = 50 \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 12.5 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Collected} = 50 - 12.5 = 37.5 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement of 2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Tax 30%, paid on 31/12.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Gross profit 40.0 m.u.; net income 19.6 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Income statement 2017</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50.0</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.0</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>40.0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Personnel</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.0</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.0</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations = income before taxes</strong></td><td>no interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>28.0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 28$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8.4</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$28 - 8.4$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>19.6</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>All 19.6 is retained (strict self-financing).</p>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash and prepare the balance sheet at 31/12/2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A strict self-financing strategy means no dividends.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash 59.1 m.u.</li><li>Total 119.6 m.u. on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Cash at 31/12/2017</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash} = 100 - 20 - 20 - 20 + 37.5 - 10 - 8.4 = 59.1 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Balance sheet at 31/12/2017</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20.0</td><td>Share capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100.0</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment ($20 - 2$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18.0</td><td>Retained earnings (net income 2017)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.6</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>38.0</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>119.6</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Inventory (raw materials)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.0</td><td><strong>Liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.5</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">59.1</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>81.6</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>119.6</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>119.6</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Explain the changes if the shareholders had paid only 90% of the capital</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The subscribed capital stays; the unpaid part is shown as a deduction. Does the income statement change?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income statement unchanged; cash 49.1 m.u.</li><li>Total 109.6 m.u. on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. What changes</h4>\n<p>The shareholders <strong>subscribed</strong> 100 m.u. but <strong>paid in</strong> only $0.90 \\times 100 = 90$ m.u. The remaining 10 m.u. is capital not yet paid (uncalled capital): the shareholders still owe it to the company.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Income statement:</strong> <strong>no change</strong>. Revenues and expenses do not depend on how the capital was paid; there is no interest involved. Net income stays 19.6.</li><li><strong>Cash:</strong> 10 m.u. less: $59.1 - 10 = 49.1$ m.u. All the purchases (60) can still be paid in cash, since the company receives 90.</li><li><strong>Equity:</strong> the contributed capital is what the stockholders actually provided [Slide 13, Slide 17], so the subscribed capital is shown minus the unpaid part: $100 - 10 = 90$.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>38.0</strong></td><td>Subscribed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100.0</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.0</td><td>− Uncalled capital (unpaid by shareholders)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(10.0)</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.5</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.6</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">49.1</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>109.6</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>71.6</strong></td><td><strong>Liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>0</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>109.6</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>109.6</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Both totals fall by exactly the 10 m.u. not paid.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balance:</strong> $38 + 81.6 = 119.6 = 100 + 19.6$ (requirement 1); $38 + 71.6 = 109.6 = 90 + 19.6$ (requirement 2).</li><li><strong>Equity bridge:</strong> equity = capital paid + net income retained: $100 + 19.6$ and $90 + 19.6$.</li><li><strong>Profit vs cash:</strong> after the purchases of 1/01, the cash generated during the year is $37.5 - 10 - 8.4 = 19.1$, while the profit is 19.6. Reconciliation: $19.6 + 2 + 10 - 12.5 = 19.1$, that is, profit + depreciation (expense, never a payment) + materials consumed (expense whose payment was made on 1/01 with the purchase) − sales not collected yet.</li><li><strong>Presentation note:</strong> some textbooks show the 10 m.u. owed by shareholders as an asset instead of deducting it from capital; then both totals stay at 119.6. The deduction used here is the one consistent with \"contributed capital\" in [Slide 17]. Both readings give the same net equity provided (109.6) and leave the income statement unchanged.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Financial Statements_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, pp. 7-8</div><p>Requirement 1 coincides with the key: sales 50; cost of goods sold 10; personnel 10; depreciation 2; EBIT 28; tax 8.4; net income 19.6; cash 59.1; receivables 12.5; inventory 10; land 20; equipment 18; total assets 119.6. The key does not solve requirement 2 (90 % paid in), so that part of the solution is not covered by it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P07", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Additional 1: CIRCONSA half-year statements", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 50, "tags": ["official-exercise", "half-year", "cash", "balance-sheet"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 1 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Extra 1 - Circonsa Balance Sheet and Income Statement)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P07 - T3 Extra 1 Circonsa Half-Year Statements.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-extra-exam-problems.html#problem-1", "statement_html": "<p>The financial director of CIRCONSA is working on its financial statements (balance sheet and income statement) as of 30 June 2015. It has the balance of the company as of 31 December 2014 and the following operations that took place from 1 January 2015 to 30 June 2015. The company sells a certain product.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>i.</strong> During the semester the company sold 1,200 units at 20 m.u./unit (every month the same amount, 200 u.). Customers pay 30 days after the sale. A very important customer (30% of the sales), who bought an equal amount each month, was given a 10% discount on its purchases.</li><li><strong>ii.</strong> During the semester 1,000 units were purchased (40% in the first quarter and 60% in the second) at 12 m.u./u. Suppliers are paid at 90 days.</li><li><strong>iii.</strong> At 31/12/2014 there is a stock of 200 units valued at 12 m.u./u.</li><li><strong>iv.</strong> During the first quarter of 2015 the taxes and the outstanding supplier balances of the previous year were paid.</li><li><strong>v.</strong> During the first quarter of 2015 the customer debt outstanding from the previous year was collected, except 300 m.u. from a customer who requested to defer payment until July 2016.</li><li><strong>vi.</strong> Personnel expenses were 350 m.u./month (plus 150 for Social Security) and general expenses 300 m.u./month. Personnel and Social Security are paid in the month in which they occur; overheads two months after.</li><li><strong>vii.</strong> On 1/04/2015 a renting contract (3 years) for computer equipment was signed with a specialised company, 400 m.u. per month, with payment at 120 days (payment at the end of each month).</li><li><strong>viii.</strong> On 1/04/2015 a patent was bought for 2,400 m.u., to be paid on 1/07/2015. It is amortised in 4 years.</li><li><strong>ix.</strong> On 1/02/2015 a credit of 2,000 m.u. was obtained, to be returned a year later with 12% annual interest; interest and principal are paid on 1/02/2016.</li><li><strong>x.</strong> At 31/12/2014 the company had only one machine, bought on 1/07/2014, amortised linearly with a residual value of 20%.</li><li><strong>xi.</strong> On 1/02/2015 a capital increase (fully paid, no issue premium) of 10,000 m.u. was made. The same day a new machine costing 12,000 m.u. was bought: 8,000 paid in cash and the remainder on 1/07/2015. It is amortised in 4 years with zero residual value.</li><li><strong>xii.</strong> Assume a 20% corporate tax, recorded in the balance at 30/06/2015 as \"taxes to pay\".</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Balance sheet at 31/12/2014 (m.u.):</strong> current assets 5,400 (cash 1,200; accounts receivable 1,800; inventory 2,400); non-current assets 450 (machinery 500; accumulated depreciation 50); current liabilities 800 (unpaid taxes 350; suppliers 450); owners' equity 5,050 (retained earnings 850; paid-in capital 4,200); total 5,850.</p>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> calculate the balance sheet and the profit and loss account of CIRCONSA at 30 June 2015.</p>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Period:</strong> 1/01/2015 to 30/06/2015, <strong>6 months</strong>. <strong>Closing instant:</strong> 30/06/2015. Every monthly figure is multiplied by the months it applies inside this window.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jan-Mar</td><td>Sales 200 u./month; purchases 400 u. (Q1); payment of 2014 taxes and suppliers; collection of 2014 receivables except 300</td></tr><tr><td>1/02</td><td>Credit 2,000 received; capital increase 10,000; machine 2 bought (8,000 paid)</td></tr><tr><td>1/04</td><td>Renting starts (400/month); patent bought (2,400, payable 1/07)</td></tr><tr><td>Apr-Jun</td><td>Sales 200 u./month; purchases 600 u. (Q2)</td></tr><tr><td>30/06</td><td><strong>Closing</strong></td></tr><tr><td>1/07</td><td>Payment of patent and of the 4,000 left on machine 2 (after the closing)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses</strong> (all read from the statement):</p>\n<ol><li>Sales are uniform (200 u./month) and collected 30 days later, so at 30/06 only <strong>June's sales</strong> are uncollected.</li><li>Q1 purchases are paid 90 days later, inside the semester; <strong>Q2 purchases</strong> are still unpaid at 30/06.</li><li>Overheads are paid two months later: January-April are paid, <strong>May and June</strong> are unpaid.</li><li>The renting is paid at 120 days: none of the April-June instalments has been paid at 30/06.</li><li>Simple interest, prorated by months. Tax: 20% of income before taxes, left unpaid.</li><li>Machine 1: its useful life is not given; it is deduced from the opening balance (Phase 3, Step 5).</li></ol>\n<p>The three questions of the method (income statement? cash? open balance item at 30/06?) applied to each operation:</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Op.</th><th>Income statement (6 months)</th><th>Cash in (+) / out (−)</th><th>Open at 30/06</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>i. Sales</td><td>Net sales 23,280</td><td>+19,400 (Jan-May)</td><td>Receivables 3,880 (June)</td></tr><tr><td>ii-iii. Purchases and stock</td><td>COGS 14,400</td><td>−4,800 (Q1)</td><td>Inventory 0; suppliers 7,200 (Q2)</td></tr><tr><td>iv. 2014 taxes and suppliers</td><td>—</td><td>−350 − 450</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>v. 2014 receivables</td><td>—</td><td>+1,500</td><td>Long-term receivable 300</td></tr><tr><td>vi. Personnel + SS</td><td>3,000</td><td>−3,000</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>vi. Overheads</td><td>1,800</td><td>−1,200 (Jan-Apr)</td><td>Overheads payable 600</td></tr><tr><td>vii. Renting</td><td>1,200</td><td>0</td><td>Renting payable 1,200</td></tr><tr><td>viii. Patent</td><td>Amortisation 150</td><td>0</td><td>Patent 2,400 − 150; patent payable 2,400</td></tr><tr><td>ix. Credit</td><td>Interest 100</td><td>+2,000</td><td>Credit 2,000; interest payable 100</td></tr><tr><td>x. Machine 1</td><td>Depreciation 50</td><td>0</td><td>Machine 500 − 100</td></tr><tr><td>xi. Capital increase</td><td>—</td><td>+10,000</td><td>Capital +10,000</td></tr><tr><td>xi. Machine 2</td><td>Depreciation 1,250</td><td>−8,000</td><td>Machine 12,000 − 1,250; debt 4,000</td></tr><tr><td>xii. Tax</td><td>266</td><td>0</td><td>Taxes payable 266</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each figure is derived in Phase 3.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Sales, purchases, stock and the items pending from 2014</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Draw the timeline month by month: what is collected at 30 days and paid at 90 days falls inside or outside the semester?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Receivables from June sales 3,880 m.u.</li><li>Second-quarter purchases (7,200) unpaid at 30/06</li><li>300 m.u. of 2014 receivables deferred to July 2016</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Sales and receivables (op. i)</h4>\n<p>Gross sales per month: $200 \\times 20 = 4{,}000$ m.u. The important customer buys 30% of them, so its discount per month is</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$0.30 \\times 4{,}000 \\times 0.10 = 120 \\text{ m.u./month}$$</div>\n<p>Net sales per month: $4{,}000 - 120 = 3{,}880$ m.u. Over the semester:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Net sales} = 6 \\times 3{,}880 = 23{,}280 \\text{ m.u.} \\quad (= 24{,}000 - 720)$$</div>\n<p>Collections at 30 days: the sales of January to May are collected by 30/06 ($5 \\times 3{,}880 = 19{,}400$); June's sales are still owed: <strong>receivables 3,880</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Purchases, stock and cost of goods sold (ops. ii, iii)</h4>\n<p>Units: initial stock 200 + purchases 1,000 = 1,200 available; 1,200 sold, so the ending stock is 0 units. All units cost 12 m.u., so</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{COGS} = \\text{Initial inventory} + \\text{Purchases} - \\text{Ending inventory} = 2{,}400 + 12{,}000 - 0 = 14{,}400 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Purchases: Q1 $= 0.40 \\times 1{,}000 \\times 12 = 4{,}800$, paid at 90 days (inside the semester); Q2 $= 0.60 \\times 1{,}000 \\times 12 = 7{,}200$, <strong>unpaid at 30/06</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Items from 2014 (ops. iv, v)</h4>\n<p>Paid in Q1: taxes 350 and suppliers 450 (cash −800; both liabilities disappear). Collected in Q1: $1{,}800 - 300 = 1{,}500$. The remaining 300 is due in July 2016, more than one year after 30/06/2015, so it becomes a <strong>non-current receivable</strong> [Slide 15].</p>\n<p>None of these affects the income statement: they settle revenues and expenses of 2014.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Personnel, overheads, renting, fixed assets and the credit</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Separate the expense of the semester from what is paid; depreciate each asset only for the months it was used.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Overheads payable 600; renting payable 1,200 m.u.</li><li>Machine 2 balance and patent due 1/07/2015: current liabilities</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Personnel, overheads and renting (ops. vi, vii)</h4>\n<ul><li>Personnel + Social Security: $6 \\times (350 + 150) = 3{,}000$, all paid.</li><li>Overheads: $6 \\times 300 = 1{,}800$ of expense; paid two months later, so January-April are paid ($4 \\times 300 = 1{,}200$) and May-June are owed: <strong>600 payable</strong>.</li><li>Renting: April, May, June, $3 \\times 400 = 1{,}200$ of expense; paid at 120 days, nothing paid by 30/06: <strong>1,200 payable</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Fixed assets: depreciation and amortisation (ops. viii, x, xi)</h4>\n<p><strong>Machine 1.</strong> Cost 500, residual value $0.20 \\times 500 = 100$, depreciable base $500 - 100 = 400$. The opening accumulated depreciation is 50 for the 6 months from 1/07/2014 to 31/12/2014, so the annual depreciation is $50 \\times \\frac{12}{6} = 100$ and the useful life is $\\frac{400}{100} = 4$ years. For the semester:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_1 = \\frac{500 - 100}{4} \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 50 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{accumulated} = 50 + 50 = 100$$</div>\n<p><strong>Machine 2.</strong> Bought 1/02, used 5 months (February-June), zero residual value:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_2 = \\frac{12{,}000 - 0}{4} \\times \\frac{5}{12} = 1{,}250 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Patent.</strong> Bought 1/04, used 3 months:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$A = \\frac{2{,}400}{4} \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 150 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Total depreciation and amortisation: $50 + 1{,}250 + 150 = 1{,}450$ m.u. None of it is a payment.</p>\n<p>Payments: machine 2, 8,000 paid in cash; the other 4,000 and the whole patent (2,400) are due on 1/07/2015, one day after the closing, so they are <strong>current liabilities</strong> at 30/06.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. Credit and interest (op. ix)</h4>\n<p>Received 1/02 (cash +2,000), returned on 1/02/2016, within one year of 30/06/2015: <strong>current liability</strong>. Interest from 1/02 to 30/06 (5 months), paid only in 2016:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I = 2{,}000 \\times 0.12 \\times \\frac{5}{12} = 100 \\text{ m.u.} \\quad \\text{(expense and interest payable)}$$</div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement of the semester</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The interest of the credit accrues even though it is paid in 2016.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income from operations 1,430 m.u.</li><li>Net income 1,064 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 7. Income statement for the semester</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Net sales</td><td>$24{,}000 - 720$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">23,280</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>Step 2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,400</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td>$23{,}280 - 14{,}400$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>8,880</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Personnel and Social Security</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Overheads</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,800</td></tr><tr><td>− Renting</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation and amortisation</td><td>$50 + 1{,}250 + 150$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,450</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$8{,}880 - 7{,}450$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,430</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>Step 6</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$1{,}430 - 100$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,330</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (20%)</td><td>$0.20 \\times 1{,}330$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">266</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$1{,}330 - 266$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,064</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash and prepare the balance sheet at 30/06/2015</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Cash = opening cash + collections − payments; every amount still open is a receivable or a payable.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash 16,300 m.u.</li><li>Total 33,880 m.u. on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 8. Cash at 30/06/2015</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Opening cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td><td>2014 taxes</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">350</td></tr><tr><td>2014 receivables collected</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,500</td><td>2014 suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">450</td></tr><tr><td>Sales Jan-May collected</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19,400</td><td>Q1 purchases</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4,800</td></tr><tr><td>Credit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000</td><td>Personnel + SS</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td></tr><tr><td>Capital increase</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td><td>Overheads Jan-Apr</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Machine 2 (cash part)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>34,100</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>17,800</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{30/06} = 34{,}100 - 17{,}800 = 16{,}300 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 9. Balance sheet at 30/06/2015</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Machinery (cost $500 + 12{,}000$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,500</td><td>Paid-in capital ($4{,}200 + 10{,}000$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,200</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation ($100 + 1{,}250$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(1,350)</td><td>Retained earnings (2014)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">850</td></tr><tr><td>Patent ($2{,}400 - 150$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,250</td><td>Net income of the semester</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,064</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term receivable (due July 2016)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>16,114</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>13,700</strong></td><td>Credit (due 1/02/2016)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td>Interest payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable (June)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,880</td><td>Suppliers (Q2 purchases)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7,200</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">16,300</td><td>Debt for machine 2 (due 1/07)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>20,180</strong></td><td>Debt for patent (due 1/07)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,400</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Overheads payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Renting payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">266</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>17,766</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>33,880</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>33,880</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>There are no long-term liabilities: every debt is due before 30/06/2016.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balance:</strong> $13{,}700 + 20{,}180 = 33{,}880 = 16{,}114 + 17{,}766$.</li><li><strong>Equity bridge:</strong> equity grows from 5,050 to 16,114, that is $+11{,}064 = 10{,}000$ (capital increase) $+ 1{,}064$ (net income).</li><li><strong>Profit is not cash:</strong> the semester earns 1,064 but cash grows by $16{,}300 - 1{,}200 = 15{,}100$, mostly because of the capital increase (10,000), the credit (2,000) and the many expenses still unpaid (suppliers, renting, overheads, interest, taxes). In the opposite direction, 8,000 of cash went into a machine whose expense this semester is only 1,250.</li><li><strong>Limiting case:</strong> if June's sales had been collected, cash would be 3,880 higher and receivables zero, but net income would not change: revenue is recognised when the sale is made [Slide 8].</li><li><strong>Exam traps:</strong> (1) the 1/07 debts are liabilities at 30/06, not payments; (2) the 300 deferred to July 2016 is non-current; (3) machine 2 is depreciated 5 months and the patent 3 months, not 6; (4) the life of machine 1 must be deduced from the opening balance.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Additional_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, pp. 6-8</div><p>The income statement and the cash account coincide with the key: net sales 23,280; cost of goods sold 14,400; personnel 3,000; general expenses 1,800; renting 1,200; EBITDA 2,880; depreciation and amortisation 1,450; EBIT 1,430; interest 100; income before taxes 1,330; tax 266; net income 1,064; collections 34,100; cash 16,300; total 33,880. The key writes the depreciation of machine 1 as $(500 - 100)/8$, that is, 8 half-years, the same 4-year life deduced above.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Discrepancy with the official solution (the 300 deferred to July 2016)</div><p>The key shows accounts receivable of 4,180, which includes the 300 due in July 2016, so it counts them as a current asset (current assets 20,480; non-current assets 13,400). Here they are a non-current receivable, because they fall due more than one year after 30/06/2015 (Slide 15), so receivables are 3,880 + 300 (current assets 20,180; non-current assets 13,700). Total assets, liabilities and equity are the same in both.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Erratum in the official solution</div><p>The key's payments column adds to 16,800, but its items add to $450 + 3{,}000 + 1{,}200 + 4{,}800 + 350 + 8{,}000 = 17{,}800$, which gives the cash of 16,300 that it does show. The capital increase in the equity block reads 1,000 instead of 10,000 (only 10,000 gives equity of 16,114), and the labels of the interest payable (100) and the tax payable (266) are swapped.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P08", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "year-on-year-cash", "theme": "year-on-year-cash", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Additional 2: INFOPYMES statements 2016-2017 and ROA/ROE", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 45, "tags": ["official-exercise", "roa", "roe", "cash", "balance-sheet"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 2 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Extra 2 - Infopymes Full Cycle Financial Statements and ROA ROE)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P08 - T3 Extra 2 Infopymes Statements and ROA ROE.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-extra-exam-problems.html#problem-2", "statement_html": "<p>Three entrepreneurs established INFOPYMES on 1/01/2016 to provide on-line information services for small and medium enterprises, start-ups and potential entrepreneurs.</p>\n<ul><li>The computer equipment, worth 100,000 €, was acquired on 1/01/2016 and will be paid to suppliers after three months, on 1/04/2016. Useful life 5 years (until 31/12/2020), linear depreciation, no residual value.</li><li>On-line consultations brought revenues of 300,000 € in 2016.</li><li>Rented premises: 1,000 €/month from 1/01/2016. Salaries and Social Security of the permanent employees: 5,000 €/month. Electricity, phone, etc.: 500 €/month.</li><li>Variable wages of the additional staff for the on-line consultations: 174,000 € in 2016.</li><li>The partners contributed an initial capital of 100,000 €. A bank granted a loan of 100,000 € at 10% yearly interest for 5 years (1/01/2016 to 31/12/2020). Each 31 December the company returns 20,000 € and pays the interest (10%) on the loan value at the beginning of each year.</li><li>Results are declared at 23:59 on 31 December (after all payments and receipts, including interest, wages, Social Security, etc.), and the corporation tax of 30% on the profits is paid immediately. Dividends of 40% of the profits after taxes are distributed, and the final balance is drawn at 24:00.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Required:</strong> (1) the income statement for 2016; (2) assuming the tax has been paid and the dividends distributed, the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2016; (3) the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2017, assuming no new investments in 2017 and that the retained earnings and the cash balance at the end of 2017 remained unchanged with respect to 2016; (4) the ROA and ROE ratios in 2017.</p>", "setup_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td>100,000 € (1/01/2016)</td></tr><tr><td>Loan</td><td>100,000 € at 10%; 20,000 € returned each 31/12; interest on the balance at the start of each year</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment</td><td>100,000 €, bought 1/01/2016, paid 1/04/2016, 5 years, no residual value</td></tr><tr><td>Revenues 2016</td><td>300,000 €</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed monthly costs</td><td>Rent 1,000; permanent staff 5,000; utilities 500 (€/month)</td></tr><tr><td>Variable wages 2016</td><td>174,000 €</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>30% of profits, paid at 23:59 on 31/12</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends</td><td>40% of profit after taxes, paid at 23:59 on 31/12</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>The statement says the results are declared \"after having made all payments and receipts\", so the 300,000 € of revenues are <strong>collected</strong> and all 2016 costs are <strong>paid</strong> within 2016.</li><li>The balance is drawn at 24:00, <strong>after</strong> paying tax, interest, the 20,000 € instalment and dividends: none of them remains as a liability.</li><li>For 2017 the statement gives no operating data; we assume the <strong>same revenues and operating costs as in 2016</strong>, which is the only reading that lets requirement 4 be computed. With retained earnings unchanged, the whole 2017 profit must have been paid out as dividends (shown in Phase 3).</li></ol>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Income statement 2016</th><th>Cash 2016</th><th>Open at 24:00 on 31/12/2016</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td>—</td><td>+100,000</td><td>Capital 100,000</td></tr><tr><td>Loan</td><td>—</td><td>+100,000</td><td>Loan 80,000 (after the instalment)</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment</td><td>Depreciation 20,000</td><td>−100,000 (on 1/04)</td><td>Equipment 100,000 − 20,000</td></tr><tr><td>Revenues</td><td>300,000</td><td>+300,000</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Rent, staff, utilities, variable wages</td><td>252,000</td><td>−252,000</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Interest</td><td>10,000</td><td>−10,000</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Instalment</td><td>—</td><td>−20,000</td><td>(reduces the loan)</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>5,400</td><td>−5,400</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends</td><td>— (distribution)</td><td>−5,040</td><td>Retained earnings 7,560</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The equipment is bought on credit on 1/01 and paid on 1/04: between those dates it is a debt to the supplier, but at 31/12 that debt no longer exists.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for 2016</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Interest is computed on the loan balance at the beginning of the year; the equipment is depreciated over 5 years.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Depreciation 20,000; interest 10,000 €</li><li>Income from operations 28,000; net income 12,600 €</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Depreciation and interest 2016</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D = \\frac{100{,}000 - 0}{5} \\times \\frac{12}{12} = 20{,}000 \\text{ €/year}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{2016} = 100{,}000 \\times 0.10 = 10{,}000 \\text{ €} \\quad \\text{(loan value at the beginning of 2016)}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Income statement 2016 (requirement 1)</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Revenues (on-line consultations)</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Rent</td><td>$12 \\times 1{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Permanent staff and Social Security</td><td>$12 \\times 5{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Electricity, phone, etc.</td><td>$12 \\times 500$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Variable wages</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">174,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$300{,}000 - 272{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>28,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$28{,}000 - 10{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>18,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 18{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,400</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$18{,}000 - 5{,}400$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>12,600</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Distribution [Slide 23]: dividends $= 0.40 \\times 12{,}600 = 5{,}040$ €; retained earnings (self-financing) $= 12{,}600 - 5{,}040 = 7{,}560$ €.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash and prepare the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2016</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Tax, interest, the 20,000 instalment and the dividend are all paid before 24:00. Split the remaining loan by maturity.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash 107,560 €</li><li>Total 187,560 € on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Cash at 24:00 on 31/12/2016</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td><td>Equipment (1/04)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td></tr><tr><td>Loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td><td>Rent + staff + utilities</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">78,000</td></tr><tr><td>Revenues collected</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td><td>Variable wages</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">174,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Loan instalment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Tax</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,400</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividends</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,040</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>500,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>392,440</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{2016} = 500{,}000 - 392{,}440 = 107{,}560 \\text{ €}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2016 (requirement 2)</h4>\n<p>The loan after the first instalment is $100{,}000 - 20{,}000 = 80{,}000$ €. The instalment due on 31/12/2017 (20,000 €) is within one year, so it is a <strong>current liability</strong>; the other 60,000 € are long-term [Slide 16].</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(20,000)</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7,560</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>80,000</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>107,560</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">107,560</td><td>Long-term bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>107,560</strong></td><td>Short-term bank loan (due 31/12/2017)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>187,560</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>187,560</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>No new investment: one more year of depreciation and one more instalment; cash and retained earnings unchanged.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Total 167,560 € on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2017 (requirement 3)</h4>\n<p>Item by item:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Equipment:</strong> no new investment; two years of depreciation, $2 \\times 20{,}000 = 40{,}000$; net value $100{,}000 - 40{,}000 = 60{,}000$ €.</li><li><strong>Loan:</strong> second instalment paid, $80{,}000 - 20{,}000 = 60{,}000$ €: 20,000 € current (due 31/12/2018) and 40,000 € long-term.</li><li><strong>Cash and retained earnings:</strong> unchanged by statement: 107,560 € and 7,560 €.</li><li><strong>Capital:</strong> unchanged, 100,000 €.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">€</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(40,000)</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7,560</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>60,000</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>107,560</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">107,560</td><td>Long-term bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>107,560</strong></td><td>Short-term bank loan (due 31/12/2018)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>167,560</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>167,560</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Is the statement consistent?</strong> With the 2016 operations repeated (hypothesis 3), 2017 gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{2017} = 80{,}000 \\times 0.10 = 8{,}000, \\quad \\text{IBT} = 28{,}000 - 8{,}000 = 20{,}000, \\quad T = 0.30 \\times 20{,}000 = 6{,}000, \\quad \\text{NI} = 14{,}000$$</div>\n<p>Retained earnings unchanged means dividends $= 14{,}000$ € (a 100% pay-out). Cash then moves by $+300{,}000 - 252{,}000 - 8{,}000 - 20{,}000 - 6{,}000 - 14{,}000 = 0$, so the cash is indeed unchanged: the two conditions of the statement are compatible.</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the ROA and the ROE of 2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The course decks do not define them: state the definitions you use (extension).</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>ROA 16.71%; ROE 13.02% (income from operations / total assets; net income / equity)</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. ROA and ROE in 2017 (requirement 4)</h4>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Definitions not in the Topic 3 slides</div><p>The Topic 3 deck in <code>sources/</code> does not define ROA and ROE, but the problem asks for them. The standard definitions used here are $\\text{ROA} = \\dfrac{\\text{Income from operations}}{\\text{Total assets}}$ (the return of the economic structure) and $\\text{ROE} = \\dfrac{\\text{Net income}}{\\text{Equity}}$ (the return to the owners). Check them against your class notes: if your lecturer defines ROA with net income instead, the value is $14{,}000 / 167{,}560 = 8.36\\%$.</p></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROA}_{2017} = \\frac{28{,}000}{167{,}560} = 0.1671 = 16.71\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROE}_{2017} = \\frac{14{,}000}{107{,}560} = 0.1302 = 13.02\\%$$</div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balances:</strong> $80{,}000 + 107{,}560 = 187{,}560 = 107{,}560 + 60{,}000 + 20{,}000$ (2016) and $60{,}000 + 107{,}560 = 167{,}560 = 107{,}560 + 40{,}000 + 20{,}000$ (2017).</li><li><strong>What changes between the two years:</strong> both sides shrink by 20,000 €: the asset loses 20,000 € of value through depreciation, and the loan falls by the 20,000 € instalment, which in cash terms was financed by the cash generated (depreciation is an expense that is not a payment).</li><li><strong>Leverage</strong> [Slide 36]: $L/E = 80{,}000/107{,}560 = 0.74$ in 2016 and $60{,}000/107{,}560 = 0.56$ in 2017; the company becomes less indebted each year.</li><li><strong>ROE &lt; ROA here:</strong> the return to the owners is lower than the return of the assets because interest and taxes are deducted from the numerator; the equity base being smaller than total assets is not enough to compensate.</li><li><strong>Exam traps:</strong> (1) the loan instalment is a payment, never an expense; (2) at 24:00 no tax or dividend liability remains; (3) the current part of the loan (next instalment) must be separated from the long-term part.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Additional_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, pp. 12-14</div><p>Requirements 1 and 2 coincide with the key in every figure: EBIT 28,000; interest 10,000; income before taxes 18,000; tax 5,400; net income 12,600; dividends 5,040; retained earnings 7,560; cash 107,560; equipment net 80,000; total 187,560; bank loan 20,000 current and 60,000 long term; equity 107,560.</p>\n<p>The key does not solve requirement 3 (balance sheet at 31/12/2017) nor requirement 4 (ROA and ROE), so Steps 5 and 6 above are not covered by it.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P09", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Additional 3: WIFINET accounting cycle and two ways of paying a dividend", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 50, "tags": ["official-exercise", "dividends", "balance-sheet", "losses"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 3 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Extra 3 - Wifinet Internet Provider Accounting Cycle and Dividends)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P09 - T3 Extra 3 Wifinet Accounting Cycle and Dividends.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-extra-exam-problems.html#problem-3", "statement_html": "<p>Wifinet S.L. was incorporated on 1/01/2016 with a registered capital of 6,003 m.u., to provide wireless Internet on the Alicante coast.</p>\n<p><strong>Fiscal year 2016.</strong> It took a bank loan of 2,000 m.u. at 5% annual interest (payable at the end of each year), the principal being repayable in 2019. It invested 5,371 m.u. in technical equipment with a life of 20 years; at the end of 2016 it recorded an accumulated depreciation of 26 m.u. for those assets. It also invested 206 m.u. in licence fees by 31/12/2016, to use certain technologies for 5 years. It spent 636 m.u. on financial shares of other companies. Most of 2016 was devoted to building the network and business started only in the last two months. At the end of 2016 the balance sheet reflected a <strong>loss of 340 m.u.</strong> and <strong>cash in hand and bank of 1,476 m.u.</strong></p>\n<p>In 2017 the results were positive, but the company decided not to distribute dividends and to retain all its profits to reduce the loss of the previous year.</p>\n<p><strong>Fiscal year 2017.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Internet and VOIP: 5,000 customers on average, with an average revenue of 0.032 m.u./month per client in subscription fees. Additionally, 46 m.u. of on-line sales of other products. At the end of 2017 customers owed 164 m.u.</li><li>Stocks were exhausted at the end of 2016. Purchases of materials to be installed in customers' homes: 680 m.u., of which 560 m.u. paid immediately and the rest deferred to the following year. The 2017 sales consumed half of the purchased goods.</li><li>Staff costs: 559 m.u., paid in full in 2017. Supplies and outside services: 312 m.u., paid.</li><li>Other administrative expenses: 300 m.u., 60% of them deferred to the following year.</li><li>Corporation tax: 30%, payable in the first quarter of 2018.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Required:</strong> (1) the income statement for 2017; (2) the balance sheet at 31/12/2017; (3) which balance-sheet items change, and by how much, if a dividend of 20% of net profit is paid at 24:00 on 31/12/2017; (4) the same if the dividend of 20% is decided before the end of 2017 and paid on 30/06/2018.</p>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Period:</strong> calendar year 2017. <strong>Closing:</strong> 31/12/2017. The 2016 data serve only to build the <strong>opening balance</strong> of 2017.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>The licences were bought at the very end of 2016, so they start to be amortised in 2017 (no 2016 amortisation is given).</li><li>The equipment was depreciated only 26 m.u. in 2016 (part of the year); in 2017 it is used the whole year.</li><li>Interest is paid at the end of each year, so no interest payable remains; the loan is due in 2019, more than one year after 31/12/2017: <strong>long-term</strong> [Slide 16].</li><li>The financial shares are a long-term investment (non-current asset) [Slide 15]; they produce no revenue in the data.</li></ol>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Opening balance at 31/12/2016 (rebuilt from the data)</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Technical equipment ($5{,}371 - 26$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,345</td><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,003</td></tr><tr><td>Licences</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">206</td><td>Loss of 2016</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(340)</td></tr><tr><td>Financial shares</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">636</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5,663</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,476</td><td>Bank loan (due 2019)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>7,663</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>7,663</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>It balances ($7{,}663 = 5{,}663 + 2{,}000$), which confirms that no other 2016 item is missing.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Operations of 2017</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Income statement 2017</th><th>Cash 2017</th><th>Open at 31/12/2017</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Subscriptions + on-line sales</td><td>Revenue 1,966</td><td>+1,802</td><td>Receivables 164</td></tr><tr><td>Purchases of materials (680)</td><td>COGS 340</td><td>−560</td><td>Inventory 340; suppliers 120</td></tr><tr><td>Staff</td><td>559</td><td>−559</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Supplies and outside services</td><td>312</td><td>−312</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Administrative expenses</td><td>300</td><td>−120</td><td>Other creditors 180</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment depreciation</td><td>268.55</td><td>0</td><td>Accumulated depreciation 294.55</td></tr><tr><td>Licence amortisation</td><td>41.20</td><td>0</td><td>Licences 164.80</td></tr><tr><td>Interest</td><td>100</td><td>−100</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>13.575</td><td>0</td><td>Taxes payable 13.575</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for 2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compute revenues, consumption, operating expenses, depreciation and interest first; check whether earlier losses reduce the tax.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income from operations 145.25 m.u.</li><li>Net income 31.675 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Revenues and receivables</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Subscriptions} = 5{,}000 \\text{ clients} \\times 0.032 \\tfrac{\\text{m.u.}}{\\text{client} \\cdot \\text{month}} \\times 12 \\text{ months} = 1{,}920 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Revenues} = 1{,}920 + 46 = 1{,}966 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Collected} = 1{,}966 - 164 = 1{,}802 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Purchases, consumption and inventory</h4>\n<p>Opening stock 0 (\"stocks were exhausted at the end of 2016\"); purchases 680; half consumed:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{COGS} = 0 + 680 - 340 = 340 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Ending inventory} = 340 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Payment: 560 paid; $680 - 560 = 120$ owed to suppliers.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Operating expenses</h4>\n<ul><li>Staff 559 and supplies and outside services 312, all paid.</li><li>Administrative expenses 300: 60% deferred, $0.60 \\times 300 = 180$ owed; $300 - 180 = 120$ paid.</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Depreciation and amortisation</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{equipment}} = \\frac{5{,}371}{20} = 268.55 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad A_{\\text{licences}} = \\frac{206}{5} = 41.20 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Accumulated depreciation of the equipment: $26 + 268.55 = 294.55$; net value $5{,}371 - 294.55 = 5{,}076.45$. Licences: $206 - 41.20 = 164.80$.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Interest</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I = 2{,}000 \\times 0.05 = 100 \\text{ m.u.} \\quad \\text{(paid at the end of 2017)}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. Income statement 2017 (requirement 1)</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Revenues</td><td>$1{,}920 + 46$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,966.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold (materials consumed)</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">340.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,626.000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Staff</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">559.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Supplies and outside services</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">312.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Administrative expenses</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation and amortisation</td><td>$268.55 + 41.20$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">309.750</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$1{,}626 - 1{,}480.75$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>145.250</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$145.25 - 100$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>45.250</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 45.25$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.575</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$45.25 - 13.575$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>31.675</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash and prepare the balance sheet at 31/12/2017</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Start from the opening cash of 1,476 m.u.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash 1,627 m.u.</li><li>Total 8,008.25 m.u. on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 7. Cash at 31/12/2017</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash} = 1{,}476 + 1{,}802 - 560 - 559 - 312 - 120 - 100 = 1{,}627 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 8. Balance sheet at 31/12/2017 (requirement 2)</h4>\n<p>All the profit is retained, so it stays in equity and reduces the accumulated loss: $-340 + 31.675 = -308.325$.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Technical equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,371.000</td><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,003.000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(294.550)</td><td>Loss of 2016</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(340.000)</td></tr><tr><td>Licences (net)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">164.800</td><td>Net income 2017</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">31.675</td></tr><tr><td>Financial shares</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">636.000</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5,694.675</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>5,877.250</strong></td><td>Bank loan (due 2019)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000.000</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">340.000</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2,000.000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">164.000</td><td>Suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120.000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,627.000</td><td>Other creditors (administration)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180.000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2,131.000</strong></td><td>Taxes payable (Q1 2018)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.575</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>313.575</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>8,008.250</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>8,008.250</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Which items change if a 20% dividend is paid at 24:00 on 31/12/2017?</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Cash leaves the company and the profit kept in equity falls by the same amount.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Dividend 6.335 m.u.: cash and equity −6.335; total 8,001.915 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 9. Dividend paid at 24:00 on 31/12/2017 (requirement 3)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividend} = 0.20 \\times 31.675 = 6.335 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Paying it at 24:00 means cash leaves the company and the profit kept in equity falls by the same amount:</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Before</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,627.000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−6.335</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,620.665</td></tr><tr><td>Equity (net income retained)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,694.675</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−6.335</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,688.340</td></tr><tr><td>Total assets = total liabilities and equity</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8,008.250</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−6.335</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8,001.915</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>And if the dividend is decided in 2017 but paid on 30/06/2018?</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The dividend is owed but not paid: what appears among the liabilities?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Equity −6.335; dividends payable +6.335 (current); total unchanged at 8,008.25 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 10. Dividend decided in 2017, paid on 30/06/2018 (requirement 4)</h4>\n<p>The dividend is already owed to the shareholders at 31/12/2017 but no cash has left: equity falls and a <strong>current liability</strong> (dividends payable, due within the year) appears.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Before</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equity</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,694.675</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−6.335</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,688.340</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends payable (current liability)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+6.335</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.335</td></tr><tr><td>Current liabilities</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">313.575</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+6.335</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">319.910</td></tr><tr><td>Cash and total assets</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">unchanged</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8,008.250</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balance:</strong> $5{,}877.25 + 2{,}131 = 8{,}008.25 = 5{,}694.675 + 2{,}000 + 313.575$; requirement 3: $8{,}001.915 = 5{,}688.340 + 2{,}313.575$; requirement 4: $8{,}008.25 = 5{,}688.34 + 2{,}000 + 319.91$.</li><li><strong>Requirement 3 vs 4:</strong> both reduce equity by the same 6.335; the difference is only on the other side: in 3 an <strong>asset</strong> (cash) falls, in 4 a <strong>liability</strong> rises. A dividend is never an expense, so net income is 31.675 in all three cases.</li><li><strong>Why the company chose not to pay:</strong> after 2017 the equity is still below the capital (accumulated loss $-308.325$); retaining the profit is self-financing [Slide 23] that rebuilds the equity.</li><li><strong>Exam traps:</strong> (1) the 2016 data are only the opening balance; (2) inventory is half of the purchases; (3) the licences bought at the end of 2016 are amortised from 2017; (4) the loan due in 2019 is long-term.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Additional_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, pp. 16-18</div><p>Requirements 1 and 2 coincide with the key: revenues 1,966; cost of goods sold 340; operating expenses 1,511; EBITDA 455; depreciation and amortisation 309.75; EBIT 145.25; interest 100; income before taxes 45.25; tax 13.575; net income 31.675; cash 1,627; total assets 8,008.25; equity 5,694.675. The key rounds to one decimal (309.8; 145.3; 45.3; 13.6; 31.7), which only shifts the last digit.</p>\n<p>The key does not answer requirements 3 and 4 (20 % dividend): it only shows \"Dividends (@0%)\", so Steps 9 and 10 above are not covered by it.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Erratum in the official solution</div><p>The key gives total liabilities of 2,325.935, but its own items add to $120 + 180 + 13.575 + 2{,}000 = 2{,}313.575$ (2,313.6 rounded), which together with equity of 5,694.7 gives the total of 8,008.3 that it does show. The accumulated depreciation mentions \"2009\"; the 26 m.u. belong to 2016.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P10", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "T3 Additional 4: CEMED statements and a 5% prompt-payment discount", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 50, "tags": ["official-exercise", "supplier-discount", "balance-sheet", "cash"], "official": true, "source": "T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 4 (official exercise, solved in Problem - T3 Extra 4 - Cemed Mediterranean Pottery Manufacturing and Supplier Discount)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P10 - T3 Extra 4 Cemed Statements and Supplier Discount.md", "page": "subjects/business-management/problemas/topic-3-extra-exam-problems.html#problem-4", "statement_html": "<p>CERÁMICAS DEL MEDITERRÁNEO (CEMED) manufactures and distributes ceramic tiles for construction in Valencia.</p>\n<ul><li>CEMED was created on 1/01/2016 by three partners, each contributing 7 m.u., fully in cash.</li><li>On 1/02/2016 it bought a piece of land worth 10 m.u., paying 50% in cash and leaving 50% to be paid one year later. On the same date it bought the factory building, paying 6 m.u. in cash and financing this amount with a mortgage at 5% annual interest for ten years. The factory is amortised over 20 years, linearly, with zero residual value. Each 31 December the company returns 10% of the mortgage principal plus the interest accrued in the year.</li><li>On 1/04/2016 it obtained an additional bank loan of 5 m.u. at 6% annual interest, returning 20% of the principal each semester for the next 2½ years (first repayment on 1/10/2016, second on 1/04/2017, and so on). Interest is paid semiannually, calculated on the amount actually due in that semester, the day after the end of the semester.</li><li>With this loan, on the same date, it bought machinery for 3 m.u. with 60 days payment delay; useful life 10 years, straight-line, zero residual value.</li><li>On 1/07/2016 it acquired 150 tons of raw material at 0.03 m.u./ton. Half of it was used in producing the products sold. 60% of the total cost is paid to the supplier at 90 days; the other 40% remains unpaid at the end of the year.</li><li>Sales revenue: 50 m.u., of which only 70% had been collected at 31/12/2016.</li><li>Personnel plus Social Security: 0.8 m.u., paid in full in 2016.</li><li>Corporation tax: 30%, settled by calendar year and paid on 15/01/2017.</li><li>The company keeps 40% of annual net profits as reserves; the dividends are paid on 31/12/2016.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Required:</strong> (1) the income statement and the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2016, explaining the intermediate calculations; (2) the changes in the income statement and the balance sheet if the company had negotiated a 5% discount on the total price of the raw materials in exchange for paying in cash the 60% agreed at 90 days. In which case is the profit higher, and why?</p>", "setup_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Cash</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1/01</td><td>Capital $3 \\times 7 = 21$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+21</td></tr><tr><td>1/02</td><td>Land 10: 50% paid, 50% due 1/02/2017</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−5</td></tr><tr><td>1/02</td><td>Building 6, financed by a mortgage of 6</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+6 −6</td></tr><tr><td>1/04</td><td>Bank loan 5 at 6%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+5</td></tr><tr><td>1/04</td><td>Machinery 3 bought, paid 60 days later (31/05)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−3 (on 31/05)</td></tr><tr><td>1/07</td><td>Raw material $150 \\times 0.03 = 4.5$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">—</td></tr><tr><td>29/09</td><td>60% of the raw material paid (90 days)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−2.7</td></tr><tr><td>1/10</td><td>Loan: first instalment (20% of 5) and interest of the first semester</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−1 −0.15</td></tr><tr><td>31/12</td><td>Mortgage: 10% of principal and interest of the year</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−0.6 −0.275</td></tr><tr><td>31/12</td><td>Dividends (60% of net income)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−19.3053</td></tr><tr><td>15/01/2017</td><td>Tax paid (after the closing)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>The building is used from 1/02 (11 months) and the machinery from 1/04 (9 months). Land is not depreciated.</li><li>The mortgage interest of 2016 runs from 1/02 to 31/12 (11 months) on the 6 m.u. outstanding.</li><li>Loan semesters: 1/04-30/09 (principal 5) and 1/10-31/03 (principal 4). The interest of the second semester is paid on 1/04/2017, so the part from 1/10 to 31/12 is an <strong>accrued, unpaid</strong> expense of 2016.</li><li>Results to four decimals; exact fractions were used and the last digit is rounded.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Income statement 2016</th><th>Cash 2016</th><th>Open at 31/12/2016</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td>—</td><td>+21</td><td>Capital 21</td></tr><tr><td>Land</td><td>—</td><td>−5</td><td>Land 10; land payable 5 (current)</td></tr><tr><td>Building / mortgage</td><td>Depreciation 0.275; interest 0.275</td><td>+6 −6 −0.6 −0.275</td><td>Building $6 - 0.275$; mortgage 5.4 (0.6 current, 4.8 long-term)</td></tr><tr><td>Bank loan</td><td>Interest $0.15 + 0.06$</td><td>+5 −1 −0.15</td><td>Loan 4 (2 current, 2 long-term); interest payable 0.06</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery</td><td>Depreciation 0.225</td><td>−3</td><td>Machinery $3 - 0.225$</td></tr><tr><td>Raw material</td><td>COGS 2.25</td><td>−2.7</td><td>Inventory 2.25; suppliers 1.8</td></tr><tr><td>Sales</td><td>Revenue 50</td><td>+35</td><td>Receivables 15</td></tr><tr><td>Personnel + SS</td><td>0.8</td><td>−0.8</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>13.7895</td><td>0</td><td>Taxes payable 13.7895</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends</td><td>— (distribution)</td><td>−19.3053</td><td>Reserves 12.8702</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for 2016</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Work out depreciation, the mortgage and loan interest, the raw material consumed and the sales before building the statement.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income from operations 46.45 m.u.</li><li>Net income 32.1755 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Depreciation</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{building}} = \\frac{6 - 0}{20} \\times \\frac{11}{12} = 0.3 \\times 0.9167 = 0.2750 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{machinery}} = \\frac{3 - 0}{10} \\times \\frac{9}{12} = 0.3 \\times 0.75 = 0.2250 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Total depreciation: $0.275 + 0.225 = 0.5$ m.u.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Mortgage</h4>\n<p>Interest of 2016 (1/02 to 31/12, principal 6):</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{\\text{mortgage}} = 6 \\times 0.05 \\times \\frac{11}{12} = 0.2750 \\text{ m.u.} \\quad \\text{(paid on 31/12)}$$</div>\n<p>Principal returned on 31/12: $0.10 \\times 6 = 0.6$; outstanding $6 - 0.6 = 5.4$. The next 0.6, due 31/12/2017, is current; the remaining $5.4 - 0.6 = 4.8$ is long-term.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Bank loan</h4>\n<p>Each instalment is $0.20 \\times 5 = 1$ m.u. (five instalments: 1/10/2016, 1/04/2017, 1/10/2017, 1/04/2018, 1/10/2018).</p>\n<ul><li>First semester (1/04-30/09, principal 5): $I_1 = 5 \\times 0.06 \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 0.15$, paid on 1/10/2016 together with the first instalment.</li><li>Second semester (1/10/2016-31/03/2017, principal 4): the 2016 part is October-December, $I_2 = 4 \\times 0.06 \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 0.06$, <strong>accrued but unpaid</strong> (it will be paid on 1/04/2017).</li></ul>\n<p>Interest of the loan in 2016: $0.15 + 0.06 = 0.21$. Principal outstanding at 31/12: $5 - 1 = 4$, of which the instalments of 1/04/2017 and 1/10/2017 (2) are current and those of 2018 (2) are long-term.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Raw material, cost of goods sold and suppliers</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Purchase} = 150 \\times 0.03 = 4.5 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{COGS} = \\frac{4.5}{2} = 2.25, \\qquad \\text{Inventory} = 2.25$$</div>\n<p>Paid at 90 days: $0.60 \\times 4.5 = 2.7$; unpaid: $0.40 \\times 4.5 = 1.8$ (suppliers, current).</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 5. Sales</h4>\n<p>Collected $0.70 \\times 50 = 35$; receivables $50 - 35 = 15$.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 6. Income statement 2016</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50.0000</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>Step 4</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.2500</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>47.7500</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Personnel and Social Security</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.8000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.5000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$47.75 - 1.3$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>46.4500</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest (mortgage 0.275; loan 0.21)</td><td>Steps 2-3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.4850</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$46.45 - 0.485$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>45.9650</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 45.965$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.7895</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$45.965 - 13.7895$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>32.1755</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Distribution: reserves $= 0.40 \\times 32.1755 = 12.8702$; dividends $= 0.60 \\times 32.1755 = 19.3053$, paid on 31/12.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash and prepare the balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2016</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Split the land debt and the loan by maturity.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash 28.1697 m.u.</li><li>Total 63.9197 m.u. on both sides</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 7. Cash at 24:00 on 31/12/2016</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">21.0000</td><td>Land (50%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Mortgage</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.0000</td><td>Building</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.0000</td><td>Machinery</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Sales collected</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">35.0000</td><td>Raw material (60%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.7000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Personnel</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.8000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Mortgage principal + interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.8750</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Loan instalment + interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.1500</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividends</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.3053</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>67.0000</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>38.8303</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash} = 67 - 38.8303 = 28.1697 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 8. Balance sheet at 24:00 on 31/12/2016</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.0000</td><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">21.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Building ($6 - 0.275$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.7250</td><td>Reserves (40% of net income)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.8702</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery ($3 - 0.225$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.7750</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>33.8702</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>18.5000</strong></td><td>Mortgage, long-term part</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4.8000</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory (raw material)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.2500</td><td>Bank loan, long-term part</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.0000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15.0000</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>6.8000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">28.1697</td><td>Land payable (1/02/2017)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.0000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>45.4197</strong></td><td>Mortgage, current part</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.6000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Bank loan, current part</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.0000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Interest payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.0600</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.8000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Taxes payable (15/01/2017)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.7895</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>23.2495</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>63.9197</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>63.9197</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Changes if the company had obtained a 5% discount for paying 60% in cash; in which case is the profit higher?</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A lower purchase price lowers the cost of the material consumed; the unconsumed part stays in inventory at the lower price.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Net income 32.2543 m.u. (+0.0788, the after-tax saving)</li><li>Total 63.8950 m.u.; cash 28.2575 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 9. New purchase price and payments</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Price} = 4.5 \\times (1 - 0.05) = 4.275 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<ul><li>Paid in cash on 1/07: $0.60 \\times 4.275 = 2.565$ (instead of 2.7 at 90 days).</li><li>Unpaid at 31/12: $0.40 \\times 4.275 = 1.71$ (instead of 1.8).</li><li>Consumed (half): $\\text{COGS} = 4.275 / 2 = 2.1375$; inventory 2.1375 (both instead of 2.25).</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 10. New income statement</h4>\n<p>Only the cost of goods sold changes before taxes: $\\Delta\\text{COGS} = 2.1375 - 2.25 = -0.1125$.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Requirement 1</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Requirement 2</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cost of goods sold</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.2500</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.1375</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−0.1125</td></tr><tr><td>Income before taxes</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">45.9650</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">46.0775</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.1125</td></tr><tr><td>Income taxes (30%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.7895</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13.8233</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.0338</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Net income</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>32.1755</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>32.2543</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>+0.0788</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Dividends (60%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.3053</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19.3526</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.0473</td></tr><tr><td>Reserves (40%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.8702</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.9017</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.0315</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The net change is the after-tax saving: $0.1125 \\times (1 - 0.30) = 0.07875$.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 11. New balance sheet items</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−0.1125</td><td>Half of the material is still in stock, valued at the lower price</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.0878</td><td>Pays 2.565 instead of 2.7 (+0.135) but pays 0.0473 more dividends</td></tr><tr><td>Suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−0.0900</td><td>40% of a lower price (1.71 instead of 1.8)</td></tr><tr><td>Taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.0338</td><td>Higher profit</td></tr><tr><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+0.0315</td><td>40% of the higher net income</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−0.0248</strong></td><td>$-0.1125 + 0.08775$</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−0.0248</strong></td><td>$-0.09 + 0.03375 + 0.0315$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>New totals: $63.9197 - 0.0248 = 63.8950$ on both sides (cash 28.2575).</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Balance:</strong> $18.5 + 45.4197 = 63.9197 = 33.8702 + 6.8 + 23.2495$; requirement 2: both sides fall by 0.02475.</li><li><strong>In which case is the profit higher?</strong> With the <strong>discount</strong>. The discount lowers the cost of the material; the price paid for it is giving up 90 days of <strong>trade credit</strong> [Slide 35], and trade credit has no explicit interest in this problem, so losing it costs nothing in the income statement: it only advances a payment that in both cases falls inside 2016.</li><li><strong>Why only half of the saving reaches the profit:</strong> only half of the material was consumed. The other half of the saving is in the lower value of the inventory and will reach the income statement when that stock is used.</li><li><strong>Debt structure</strong> [Slide 36]: liabilities $6.8 + 23.2495 = 30.0495$, equity 33.8702, so $L/E = 0.89$.</li><li><strong>Exam traps:</strong> (1) the mortgage finances the building: cash +6 and −6 on the same day; (2) the loan interest of October-December is accrued but unpaid; (3) the current part of each loan is what is due before 31/12/2017; (4) the land is not depreciated; (5) taxes are payable on 15/01/2017, so they are a liability, while dividends were paid.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Official solution, <code>T3_Additional_SOLUTIONS.pdf</code>, pp. 19-25</div><p>Requirement 1 coincides with the key: cost of goods sold 2.25; personnel 0.8; depreciation 0.5; EBIT 46.45; interest 0.485; income before taxes 45.965; tax 13.79; net income 32.18; dividends 19.31; reserves 12.87; cash 28.17; total 63.92. Requirement 2 also coincides: cost of goods sold 2.14 (2.1375, rounded in the key); net income 32.25; dividends 19.35; cash 28.26; suppliers 1.71; total 63.89. The key's figures show net income rising from 32.18 to 32.25, but the key states no conclusion to the question \"in which case are profits higher?\"; the reasoning in Phase 4 is ours.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Discrepancy with the official solution (current part of the debt)</div><p>The key leaves the whole bank loan (4.0) and the whole mortgage (5.4) as long-term debt, so its current liabilities are 20.65 and its long-term liabilities 9.4. Here the instalments due within twelve months of 31/12/2016 are shown as current: 2.0 of the loan (1/04/2017 and 1/10/2017) and 0.6 of the mortgage (31/12/2017), following the definition of Slide 16. Current liabilities are therefore 23.25 and long-term liabilities 6.8; total liabilities (30.05), equity and total assets are the same.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Differences in the wording of the key</div><p>The key's statement dates the company in 2010, while the official sheet in <code>sources/</code> says 2016, which is the year used here. In the key's part 2 the interest formula writes 0.85 where 0.80 is meant (the loan balance of 4 over 5); its value, 0.06, is unchanged.</p></div>"}, {"id": "P11", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "unordered-items", "theme": "unordered-items", "unit": 3, "title": "AeroFix Composites: statements from an unordered list of balances", "difficulty": 1, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 35, "tags": ["extra-practice", "income-statement", "balance-sheet", "working-capital"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), in the style of the worked example of Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slide 19", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P11 - AeroFix Composites Unordered Account Balances.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>AeroFix Composites S.L. repairs carbon-fibre fairings for regional aircraft. At 31/12/2025, after closing the year, its accountant hands you the following list of balances in alphabetical order (figures in monetary units, m.u.). The corporate income tax rate is 30%; the tax of 2025 has not been paid yet, and no dividends are distributed this year.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Account</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Accounts payable (suppliers of resin and fabric)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable (airline customers)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">26,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accumulated depreciation of equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">24,000</td></tr><tr><td>Bank loan, instalment due in 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td></tr><tr><td>Bank loan, instalments due after 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">23,000</td></tr><tr><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cost of goods sold (materials used in repairs)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">92,000</td></tr><tr><td>Depreciation expense of 2025</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8,000</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment (autoclave, curing ovens) at cost</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120,000</td></tr><tr><td>Gain on the sale of an old van</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest expense</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4,000</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory of resin and carbon fabric</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">21,000</td></tr><tr><td>Rent expense of the workshop</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td>Retained earnings at 1/1/2025</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,000</td></tr><tr><td>Salaries expense</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">46,000</td></tr><tr><td>Sales revenue (repair services)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">190,000</td></tr><tr><td>Utilities expense</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "setup_html": "<p>The list mixes <strong>flows</strong> of the year (revenues and expenses, which go to the income statement) and <strong>stocks</strong> at 31/12 (assets, liabilities and equity, which go to the balance sheet). The income statement is prepared first, because its net income is needed to close the equity of the balance sheet [Slide 7].</p>\n<p><strong>Conventions.</strong> Extended format of the income statement [Slides 9-11]; assets split into non-current and current, liabilities into long-term and current [Slides 14-16]; equity = contributed capital + retained earnings [Slide 17].</p>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> Working capital $= \\text{Current assets} - \\text{Current liabilities}$ and current ratio $= \\text{Current assets} / \\text{Current liabilities}$ measure short-term liquidity. The debt-to-equity ratio $L/E$ is course material [Slide 36].</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify every item of the list as income statement or balance sheet, and give its class</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Ask of each item: is it a flow measured over 2025, or an amount that exists at the instant 31/12/2025? For balance-sheet items, ask whether it turns into cash (or is paid) within one year.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>8 income-statement items: sales, cost of goods sold, salaries, rent, utilities, depreciation, interest, gain on the van.</li><li>10 balance-sheet items; the bank loan splits into 10,000 current and 40,000 long-term.</li><li>The tax of 2025 is not in the list: it must be computed, and it appears twice (expense and current liability).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Statement</th><th>Class</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td>Income statement</td><td>Revenue</td><td>Earned when the repair is done [Slide 8]</td></tr><tr><td>Cost of goods sold</td><td>Income statement</td><td>Expense</td><td>Materials used in the repairs sold [Slide 10]</td></tr><tr><td>Salaries, rent, utilities, depreciation</td><td>Income statement</td><td>Operating expenses</td><td>Usual costs of running the business [Slide 10]</td></tr><tr><td>Interest expense</td><td>Income statement</td><td>Non-operating item</td><td>Financing cost, not the primary operation [Slide 11]</td></tr><tr><td>Gain on the sale of the van</td><td>Income statement</td><td>Non-operating item</td><td>Gain on the sale of equipment [Slide 11]</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment, accumulated depreciation</td><td>Balance sheet</td><td>Non-current asset (net)</td><td>Used for more than one year [Slide 15]</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory, accounts receivable, cash</td><td>Balance sheet</td><td>Current assets</td><td>Used or turned into cash within one year [Slide 14]</td></tr><tr><td>Contributed capital, retained earnings</td><td>Balance sheet</td><td>Equity</td><td>Owners' financing [Slide 17]</td></tr><tr><td>Bank loan due after 2026</td><td>Balance sheet</td><td>Long-term liability</td><td>Not paid within the year [Slide 16]</td></tr><tr><td>Bank loan due in 2026, accounts payable</td><td>Balance sheet</td><td>Current liabilities</td><td>Paid within the coming year [Slide 16]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Accumulated depreciation is not a liability: it is subtracted from the cost of the equipment to give its net book value.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Follow the cascade gross profit → income from operations → income before taxes → net income. The gain on the van and the interest are both non-operating items, with opposite signs.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Gross profit 98,000 m.u.; income from operations 26,000 m.u.</li><li>Income before taxes 24,000 m.u.; income taxes 7,200 m.u.</li><li>Net income 16,800 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Operating expenses</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Operating expenses} = 46{,}000 + 12{,}000 + 6{,}000 + 8{,}000 = 72{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Non-operating items</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Non-operating items} = +2{,}000 - 4{,}000 = -2{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Income statement</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">190,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">92,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td>$190{,}000 - 92{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>98,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Operating expenses</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">72,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$98{,}000 - 72{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>26,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>± Non-operating items</td><td>Step 2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−2,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$26{,}000 - 2{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>24,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 24{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7,200</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$24{,}000 - 7{,}200$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>16,800</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 31/12/2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Two figures are not in the list and come from part (b): the tax payable and the retained earnings at the close of the year. With no dividends, the whole net income is retained [Slide 23].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Non-current assets 96,000 m.u.; current assets 70,000 m.u.</li><li>Equity 90,800 m.u. (retained earnings 30,800 m.u.).</li><li>Current liabilities 35,200 m.u.; total 166,000 m.u. on both sides.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. The two computed figures</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Retained earnings}_{31/12} = 14{,}000 + 16{,}800 - 0 = 30{,}800 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Income taxes payable} = 7{,}200 \\text{ m.u.} \\quad \\text{(due in 2026, current liability)}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Balance sheet</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120,000</td><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(24,000)</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,800</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>96,000</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>90,800</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">21,000</td><td>Bank loan due after 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">26,000</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>40,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">23,000</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Bank loan due in 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Income taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7,200</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>70,000</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>35,200</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>166,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>166,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Check: $96{,}000 + 70{,}000 = 166{,}000$ and $90{,}800 + 40{,}000 + 35{,}200 = 166{,}000$ [Slide 18].</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the working capital, the current ratio and the debt-to-equity ratio, and comment on liquidity and solvency</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Working capital and the current ratio use only the current blocks; the debt-to-equity ratio uses all the liabilities, current and long-term.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Working capital 34,800 m.u.; current ratio 1.99 (extension).</li><li>Total liabilities 75,200 m.u.; debt-to-equity 0.83; equity 54.7% of the financing.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Liquidity (extension)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Working capital} = 70{,}000 - 35{,}200 = 34{,}800 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Current ratio} = \\frac{70{,}000}{35{,}200} = 1.99$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Solvency [Slide 36]</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$L = 40{,}000 + 35{,}200 = 75{,}200 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\frac{L}{E} = \\frac{75{,}200}{90{,}800} = 0.83, \\qquad \\%\\,\\text{Equity} = \\frac{90{,}800}{166{,}000} = 54.7\\%$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Comment</h4>\n<p>The current assets cover the debts due within one year almost twice, so the company has no short-term liquidity problem. More than half of the financing comes from the owners, and the debt-to-equity ratio below 1 indicates moderate financial leverage: the risk for shareholders is limited [Slide 36].</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> every line is in m.u.; ratios are dimensionless.</li><li><strong>Why the income statement comes first:</strong> the balance sheet only closes once the net income of the year is added to retained earnings; with the tax payable, these are the two figures that link the statements.</li><li><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> treating accumulated depreciation as a liability, putting the whole bank loan in long-term liabilities, and forgetting the tax payable.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P12", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "unordered-items", "theme": "unordered-items", "unit": 3, "title": "SkyParts Distribution: a mixed list with items that are not accounts", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 40, "tags": ["extra-practice", "income-statement", "balance-sheet", "dividends", "self-financing"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), in the style of Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 8, 13 and 19", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P12 - SkyParts Distribution Mixed List with Non-Accounting Items.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>SkyParts Distribution S.A. sells certified spare parts to aircraft maintenance shops. A trainee has typed into one list everything they found in the files at 31/12/2025 (m.u.). Some lines are not accounting items at all. The tax rate is 30% and the tax of 2025 is paid in 2026. On 31/12/2025 the board declared a dividend equal to 30% of the net income of 2025, to be paid on 15/01/2026.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">240,000</td></tr><tr><td>Order backlog signed with customers for deliveries in 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td></tr><tr><td>Warehouse at cost</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accumulated depreciation of the warehouse</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Market value of the warehouse according to an appraiser</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">150,000</td></tr><tr><td>Delivery vans at cost</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accumulated depreciation of the vans</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td>Private car of the majority shareholder</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cost of goods sold</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">150,000</td></tr><tr><td>Salaries</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">38,000</td></tr><tr><td>Advertising</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,000</td></tr><tr><td>Depreciation of 2025 (warehouse and vans)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest expense</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest revenue from a short-term deposit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,000</td></tr><tr><td>Short-term deposit (matures in March 2026)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,000</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory of spare parts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">35,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,000</td></tr><tr><td>Advance received from a customer for a delivery in February 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">22,000</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term bank loan (repayable in 2029)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td></tr><tr><td>Retained earnings at 1/1/2025</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td>Number of employees</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "setup_html": "<p>Only economic events that have already happened and that can be measured in money enter the statements. Revenues are recorded <strong>when the sale is made</strong> [Slide 8]; assets are the economic resources <strong>owned by the company</strong> [Slide 13]. The tax and the dividend payable are not in the list: they are computed.</p>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> Current ratio $= \\text{Current assets}/\\text{Current liabilities}$. The pay-out and self-financing ratios are course material [Slide 23].</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Identify the items that must be left out of both statements and justify each one</p>", "hint_html": "<p>For each suspicious line ask three questions: has a sale or purchase already taken place? Does the company own the resource? Is the figure the amount the company actually recorded?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Four lines are left out: order backlog, market value of the warehouse, the shareholder's private car, number of employees.</li><li>The customer advance is <strong>not</strong> left out: it is a current liability.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Decision</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Order backlog 80,000</td><td>Left out</td><td>No sale has been made yet; revenue is recorded when the sale is made [Slide 8]</td></tr><tr><td>Market value of the warehouse 150,000</td><td>Left out</td><td>The warehouse stays at cost less accumulated depreciation, as in every course example; recording appraisals is not done (historical-cost principle, extension)</td></tr><tr><td>Private car of the shareholder 20,000</td><td>Left out</td><td>It belongs to the shareholder, not to the company; the corporation is a separate legal entity from its owners [Topic 1, Slide 24]</td></tr><tr><td>Number of employees 12</td><td>Left out</td><td>Not a monetary amount</td></tr><tr><td>Advance from a customer 5,000</td><td><strong>Kept</strong> as current liability</td><td>The company owes a delivery in February 2026 (unearned revenue) [Slide 13]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The interest revenue and the interest expense are both non-operating items [Slide 11]. The 2025 depreciation is the flow; the accumulated depreciation is a stock.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Gross profit 90,000; income from operations 38,000 m.u.</li><li>Income before taxes 36,000; taxes 10,800; net income 25,200 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">240,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">150,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td>$240{,}000 - 150{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>90,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Operating expenses</td><td>$38{,}000 + 5{,}000 + 9{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">52,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$90{,}000 - 52{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>38,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>± Non-operating items</td><td>$+1{,}000 - 3{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−2,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$38{,}000 - 2{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>36,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 36{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,800</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$36{,}000 - 10{,}800$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>25,200</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 31/12/2025, after the dividend has been declared</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A dividend declared but not yet paid is a debt to the shareholders due within a few days: it leaves retained earnings and becomes a current liability.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Dividend payable $0.30 \\times 25{,}200 = 7{,}560$ m.u.; retained earnings at 31/12 = 37,640 m.u.</li><li>Non-current assets 98,000; current assets 95,000; current liabilities 45,360 m.u.</li><li>Total 193,000 m.u. on both sides.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Distribution of the net income [Slide 23]</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividends} = 0.30 \\times 25{,}200 = 7{,}560 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Retained} = 25{,}200 - 7{,}560 = 17{,}640 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Retained earnings}_{31/12} = 20{,}000 + 17{,}640 = 37{,}640 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Balance sheet</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Warehouse (100,000 − 30,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">70,000</td><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td></tr><tr><td>Delivery vans (40,000 − 12,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">28,000</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">37,640</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>98,000</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>117,640</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">35,000</td><td>Long-term bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>30,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Short-term deposit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,000</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">22,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,000</td><td>Customer advance</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividend payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7,560</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Income taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,800</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>95,000</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>45,360</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>193,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>193,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The short-term deposit matures within one year, so it is a current asset (a short-term investment [Slide 14]).</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the pay-out and self-financing ratios and the current ratio, and comment</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The pay-out ratio relates dividends to profits; the self-financing ratio relates retained earnings to profits. Both add up to 100%.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Pay-out 30%; self-financing 70%.</li><li>Current ratio 2.09 (extension).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Distribution ratios [Slide 23]</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Pay-out} = \\frac{7{,}560}{25{,}200} = 30\\%, \\qquad \\text{Self-financing} = \\frac{17{,}640}{25{,}200} = 70\\%$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Current ratio (extension)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Current ratio} = \\frac{95{,}000}{45{,}360} = 2.09$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Comment</h4>\n<p>SkyParts keeps 70% of its profit inside the company, an internal equity source that needs no repayment and has no explicit cost [Slides 23, 37]. Its current assets cover the short-term debts about twice, so paying the dividend in January does not threaten its liquidity.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> all amounts in m.u.; the three ratios are dimensionless.</li><li><strong>Key idea:</strong> the statements record transactions of the company, at the amounts recorded. Expectations (the backlog), opinions (the appraisal) and the owners' private assets stay out.</li><li><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> adding the backlog to sales, revaluing the warehouse, and leaving the customer advance out because \"no money is owed\".</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P13", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "unordered-items", "theme": "unordered-items", "unit": 3, "title": "Helix Rotor Components: depreciation and cost of goods sold from raw data", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 40, "tags": ["extra-practice", "depreciation", "inventory", "income-statement", "balance-sheet"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), in the style of T3_Financial Statements_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercises 1 and 4", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P13 - Helix Rotor Components Depreciation and Inventory Variation.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Helix Rotor Components S.A. machines titanium hubs for helicopter rotors. Its list of balances at 31/12/2025 is incomplete: the depreciation and the cost of goods sold were never computed. You have the following data (m.u.); the tax rate is 25%.</p>\n<p><strong>Fixed assets</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Land, bought on 1/1/2020: 50,000 (not depreciated).</li><li>Building, bought on 1/1/2020 for 200,000; useful life 40 years; no residual value; linear depreciation.</li><li>CNC machinery, bought on 1/4/2024 for 60,000; useful life 5 years; residual value 10% of cost; linear depreciation.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Inventory of titanium bars:</strong> 18,000 on 1/1/2025; purchases during 2025: 110,000; physical count on 31/12/2025: 24,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Other items of 2025:</strong> sales 210,000; salaries 52,000; utilities 7,000; insurance 3,000. The bank loan of 80,000 carries 5% interest per year (paid on 31/12/2025); 16,000 of it is repayable in 2026 and the rest later.</p>\n<p><strong>Other balances at 31/12/2025:</strong> accounts receivable 19,000; accounts payable 14,000; contributed capital 200,000; retained earnings at 1/1/2025: 12,000; cash 18,840.</p>\n<p><strong>Distribution:</strong> 40% of the net income is paid as a dividend in cash on 31/12/2025, before the balance is drawn; the tax is paid in 2026.</p>", "setup_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Asset</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Cost</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Residual</th><th>Life</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Depreciable base</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Building</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">200,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td>40 years</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">200,000</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,000</td><td>5 years</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">54,000</td></tr><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">—</td><td>not depreciated</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Linear depreciation spreads the depreciable base evenly over the useful life, in proportion to the months of use in each year. The cost of goods sold of a trading or manufacturing firm is the inventory consumed: opening inventory plus purchases minus closing inventory.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the depreciation of 2025 and the accumulated depreciation at 31/12/2025 of the building and the machinery</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The machinery was used for only 9 months in 2024. The residual value is not depreciated. Accumulated depreciation sums every year since purchase.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Building: 5,000 per year; accumulated 30,000 m.u. (6 years).</li><li>Machinery: 10,800 per year; accumulated 8,100 + 10,800 = 18,900 m.u.</li><li>Depreciation expense of 2025: 15,800 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Building</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{building}} = \\frac{200{,}000 - 0}{40} = 5{,}000 \\text{ m.u./year}$$</div>\n<p>From 1/1/2020 to 31/12/2025 there are 6 full years: accumulated depreciation $= 6 \\times 5{,}000 = 30{,}000$ m.u.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Machinery</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{machinery}} = \\frac{60{,}000 - 0.10 \\times 60{,}000}{5} = \\frac{54{,}000}{5} = 10{,}800 \\text{ m.u./year}$$</div>\n<p>In 2024 it was used from 1/4 to 31/12, that is 9 months:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{2024} = 10{,}800 \\times \\frac{9}{12} = 8{,}100 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad D_{2025} = 10{,}800 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Accumulated depreciation}_{31/12/2025} = 8{,}100 + 10{,}800 = 18{,}900 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Expense of the year</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{2025} = 5{,}000 + 10{,}800 = 15{,}800 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cost of goods sold and prepare the income statement for 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>What was available to sell in 2025 is the opening inventory plus the purchases; what was not sold is still on the shelf at 31/12.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cost of goods sold 104,000 m.u.; gross profit 106,000 m.u.</li><li>Income from operations 28,200; income before taxes 24,200 m.u.</li><li>Net income 18,150 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Cost of goods sold</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{COGS} = 18{,}000 + 110{,}000 - 24{,}000 = 104{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Interest</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I = 0.05 \\times 80{,}000 = 4{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Income statement</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">210,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">104,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td>$210{,}000 - 104{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>106,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Salaries</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">52,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Utilities and insurance</td><td>$7{,}000 + 3{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Part (a)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,800</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$106{,}000 - (52{,}000 + 10{,}000 + 15{,}800) = 106{,}000 - 77{,}800$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>28,200</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>Step 2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$28{,}200 - 4{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>24,200</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (25%)</td><td>$0.25 \\times 24{,}200$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,050</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$24{,}200 - 6{,}050$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>18,150</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 31/12/2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The dividend is already paid in cash, so it is no longer a liability; only 60% of the net income reaches retained earnings. Split the loan by maturity.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Dividend 7,260; retained earnings at 31/12 = 22,890 m.u.</li><li>Non-current assets 261,100; current assets 61,840 m.u.</li><li>Total 322,940 m.u. on both sides.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Retained earnings [Slide 23]</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividend} = 0.40 \\times 18{,}150 = 7{,}260, \\qquad \\text{Retained earnings}_{31/12} = 12{,}000 + 18{,}150 - 7{,}260 = 22{,}890 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Balance sheet</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Land</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50,000</td><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">200,000</td></tr><tr><td>Building (200,000 − 30,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">170,000</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">22,890</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery (60,000 − 18,900)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">41,100</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>222,890</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>261,100</strong></td><td>Bank loan due after 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">64,000</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">24,000</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>64,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">19,000</td><td>Bank loan due in 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">16,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18,840</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Income taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,050</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>61,840</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>36,050</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>322,940</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>322,940</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the working capital and the debt-to-equity ratio and comment</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Working capital is an extension of the course: current assets minus current liabilities.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Working capital 25,790 m.u. (extension).</li><li>Debt-to-equity $100{,}050 / 222{,}890 = 0.45$.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Working capital} = 61{,}840 - 36{,}050 = 25{,}790 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\frac{L}{E} = \\frac{64{,}000 + 36{,}050}{222{,}890} = \\frac{100{,}050}{222{,}890} = 0.45$$</div>\n<p>The company finances itself mostly with equity (69% of the total) and keeps a positive working capital, so it is both liquid and solvent. Low leverage means low risk for shareholders, but also that the profitability of equity is not amplified by debt [Slide 36].</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> depreciation in m.u./year times years (or months/12) gives m.u.</li><li><strong>Sensitivity:</strong> forgetting the residual value would raise the machinery's yearly depreciation to 12,000 m.u. and lower the net income by $0.75 \\times 1{,}200 = 900$ m.u.</li><li><strong>Land is never depreciated:</strong> it has no limited useful life.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P14", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "unordered-items", "theme": "unordered-items", "unit": 3, "title": "Nimbus Air Charter: the missing cash figure and a liquidity diagnosis", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 45, "tags": ["extra-practice", "balance-sheet", "working-capital", "leverage", "short-term-financing"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), in the style of Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 19, 31, 35 and 36", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P14 - Nimbus Air Charter Missing Cash and Liquidity Diagnosis.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Nimbus Air Charter S.A. operates three turboprops for executive flights. A file corruption deleted the cash balance at 31/12/2025; everything else survived (m.u.). The tax rate is 30% (payable in 2026). On 31/12/2025 the board declared a dividend of 50% of the net income, to be paid in January 2026.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Charter revenue of 2025</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">520,000</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">160,000</td></tr><tr><td>Crew salaries and Social Security</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">140,000</td></tr><tr><td>Maintenance</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">45,000</td></tr><tr><td>Airport and navigation fees</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">38,000</td></tr><tr><td>Depreciation of aircraft</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">72,000</td></tr><tr><td>Amortization of flight-planning software</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on the long-term loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">21,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest on the bank credit line</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000</td></tr><tr><td>Aircraft at cost</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">900,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accumulated depreciation of aircraft</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td></tr><tr><td>Software at cost</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accumulated amortization of software</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel and spare parts in stock</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">25,000</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term loan (total outstanding; 70,000 repayable in 2026)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">350,000</td></tr><tr><td>Bank credit line: limit 100,000, drawn at 31/12</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts payable to fuel suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">48,000</td></tr><tr><td>Flights paid in advance by customers, to be flown in 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">250,000</td></tr><tr><td>Retained earnings at 1/1/2025</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "setup_html": "<p>The balance sheet must satisfy $\\text{Assets} = \\text{Liabilities} + \\text{Equity}$ [Slide 12], so a single missing figure can always be recovered as the balancing item once everything else is placed. The bank credit line is short-term bank financing: the bank makes a maximum available and interest is paid only on the amount drawn [Slides 31, 35].</p>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> Working capital $= \\text{CA} - \\text{CL}$; current ratio $= \\text{CA}/\\text{CL}$.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Nimbus sells a service, so there is no cost of goods sold line: all its operating costs go to operating expenses. Both interests are non-operating items.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Operating expenses 461,000; income from operations 59,000 m.u.</li><li>Income before taxes 35,000; taxes 10,500; net income 24,500 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Charter revenue</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">520,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Fuel, crew, maintenance, fees</td><td>$160{,}000 + 140{,}000 + 45{,}000 + 38{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">383,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation and amortization</td><td>$72{,}000 + 6{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">78,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$520{,}000 - (383{,}000 + 78{,}000) = 520{,}000 - 461{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>59,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>$21{,}000 + 3{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">24,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$59{,}000 - 24{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>35,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 35{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,500</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$35{,}000 - 10{,}500$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>24,500</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Recover the cash balance at 31/12/2025 and present the balance sheet</p>", "hint_html": "<p>First close the right-hand side completely (equity after the dividend, long-term and current liabilities). Then subtract every known asset from that total.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Dividend payable 12,250; equity 270,250 m.u.</li><li>Current liabilities 230,750; long-term liabilities 280,000 m.u.</li><li>Total 781,000 m.u.; cash = 124,000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Equity [Slide 23]</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividend} = 0.50 \\times 24{,}500 = 12{,}250, \\qquad \\text{Retained earnings}_{31/12} = 8{,}000 + 24{,}500 - 12{,}250 = 20{,}250$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$E = 250{,}000 + 20{,}250 = 270{,}250 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Liabilities by maturity [Slide 16]</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Long-term loan, part after 2026} = 350{,}000 - 70{,}000 = 280{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{CL} = 70{,}000 + 60{,}000 + 48{,}000 + 30{,}000 + 10{,}500 + 12{,}250 = 230{,}750 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Total liabilities and equity} = 270{,}250 + 280{,}000 + 230{,}750 = 781{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Cash as the balancing item</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Known assets} = (900{,}000 - 300{,}000) + (30{,}000 - 10{,}000) + 12{,}000 + 25{,}000 = 657{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash} = 781{,}000 - 657{,}000 = 124{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 4. Balance sheet</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Aircraft (900,000 − 300,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600,000</td><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">250,000</td></tr><tr><td>Software (30,000 − 10,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,250</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>620,000</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>270,250</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Fuel and spare parts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td><td>Long-term loan (after 2026)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">280,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">25,000</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>280,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">124,000</td><td>Loan instalment due in 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">70,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Bank credit line drawn</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">48,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Flights paid in advance</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Income taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,500</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividend payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,250</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>161,000</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>230,750</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>781,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>781,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the working capital, the current ratio and the debt-to-equity ratio</p>", "hint_html": "<p>A negative working capital means that part of the long-lived assets is financed with debts due within the year.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Working capital −69,750 m.u.; current ratio 0.70 (extension).</li><li>Debt-to-equity 1.89; equity 34.6% of the financing.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Working capital} = 161{,}000 - 230{,}750 = -69{,}750 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Current ratio} = \\frac{161{,}000}{230{,}750} = 0.70$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\frac{L}{E} = \\frac{280{,}000 + 230{,}750}{270{,}250} = \\frac{510{,}750}{270{,}250} = 1.89, \\qquad \\%\\,\\text{Equity} = \\frac{270{,}250}{781{,}000} = 34.6\\%$$</div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Diagnose the financial position and propose two measures from the course's financing sources</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Look at which side of the balance sheet the problem lies on (maturity of debts, not profitability), then pick instruments from the map of financing sources [Slides 21-22, 35].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Profitable but illiquid: debts due within a year exceed current assets.</li><li>Measures: lengthen the debt (long-term loan or leasing instead of the credit line), retain profits (no dividend), or bring in equity; factoring of receivables helps only marginally (25,000).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Nimbus is <strong>profitable</strong> (net income 24,500 m.u.) but its <strong>short-term liquidity is weak</strong>: the current liabilities exceed the current assets by 69,750 m.u., and the leverage (1.89) is high, which increases the risk for shareholders [Slide 36]. Part of the aircraft fleet is effectively financed with short-term money (the credit line and the debts due in 2026).</p>\n<p>Measures consistent with the course:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Replace the credit line with long-term financing</strong> (a long-term loan, or leasing for the next aircraft [Slides 31, 33]), so that long-lived assets are financed with long-lived resources.</li><li><strong>Increase self-financing</strong> by cancelling or reducing the dividend: the 12,250 m.u. would stay in the company with no explicit cost [Slides 23-24].</li><li><strong>A capital increase</strong> (rights issue) would reduce leverage directly [Slide 26]. Factoring the receivables [Slide 35] brings cash forward but only 25,000 m.u. are available, so it cannot solve the gap alone.</li></ol>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> all in m.u.; ratios dimensionless.</li><li><strong>Profit is not liquidity:</strong> a firm can earn a profit and still struggle to pay its short-term debts; the balance sheet shows maturities, the income statement does not.</li><li><strong>Why the advance from customers is a liability:</strong> Nimbus owes flights, not money, but it is an obligation to be settled within the year [Slide 13].</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P15", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "reverse-engineering-ratios", "theme": "reverse-engineering-ratios", "unit": 3, "title": "Vector Avionics: closing the gaps of a balance sheet with three ratios", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 35, "tags": ["extra-practice", "balance-sheet", "ratios", "working-capital", "leverage"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), using Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 18, 23 and 36", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P15 - Vector Avionics Balance Sheet Gaps from Ratios.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Vector Avionics S.A. designs flight-control computers. A bank analyst received its balance sheet at 31/12/2025 with four figures blanked out ($X$, $W$, $Y$, $Z$), together with three ratios computed by the company's auditor (m.u.).</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment (net)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">280,000</td><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">200,000</td></tr><tr><td>Patents (net)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$Z$</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">70,000</td><td>Long-term bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$Y$</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">65,000</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$X$</td><td>Short-term bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$W$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Ratios at 31/12/2025:</p>\n<ul><li>working capital $= 60{,}000$ m.u.;</li><li>current ratio $= 1.5$;</li><li>debt-to-equity ratio $L/E = 1.0$.</li></ul>\n<p>In 2025 the net income was 30,000 m.u. and the company paid 40% of it as dividends.</p>", "setup_html": "<p>The unknowns sit in different blocks of the balance sheet, so each ratio unlocks one block:</p>\n<ul><li>working capital and current ratio involve only current assets (CA) and current liabilities (CL);</li><li>the balance-sheet identity [Slide 18] gives total assets, hence total liabilities plus equity;</li><li>the debt-to-equity ratio [Slide 36] splits that total between $L$ and $E$.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> Working capital $= \\text{CA} - \\text{CL}$; current ratio $= \\text{CA}/\\text{CL}$; $\\text{ROE} = \\text{Net income}/\\text{Equity}$.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the current assets, the current liabilities, the cash $X$ and the short-term loan $W$</p>", "hint_html": "<p>You have two equations in CA and CL: $\\text{CA} - \\text{CL} = 60{,}000$ and $\\text{CA} = 1.5\\,\\text{CL}$. Substitute the second into the first.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>CL = 120,000 m.u.; CA = 180,000 m.u.</li><li>$X = 45{,}000$ m.u.; $W = 40{,}000$ m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Solve the two liquidity equations</h4>\n<p>Substituting $\\text{CA} = 1.5\\,\\text{CL}$ into $\\text{CA} - \\text{CL} = 60{,}000$:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$1.5\\,\\text{CL} - \\text{CL} = 0.5\\,\\text{CL} = 60{,}000 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{CL} = \\frac{60{,}000}{0.5} = 120{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{CA} = 1.5 \\times 120{,}000 = 180{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. The unknown items</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$X = \\text{CA} - \\text{Inventory} - \\text{Receivables} = 180{,}000 - 70{,}000 - 65{,}000 = 45{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$W = \\text{CL} - \\text{Accounts payable} = 120{,}000 - 80{,}000 = 40{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Find total assets, equity, total liabilities, the long-term loan $Y$ and retained earnings $Z$</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Total assets follow from the asset side, now complete. With $L/E = 1$, liabilities and equity are equal halves of the total.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Total assets 500,000 m.u.</li><li>$E = L = 250{,}000$ m.u.</li><li>$Y = 130{,}000$ m.u.; $Z = 50{,}000$ m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Total assets</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Non-current assets} = 280{,}000 + 40{,}000 = 320{,}000, \\qquad \\text{TA} = 320{,}000 + 180{,}000 = 500{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Split by the debt-to-equity ratio</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$L + E = 500{,}000, \\qquad \\frac{L}{E} = 1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; L = E \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 2E = 500{,}000 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; E = L = 250{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. The two unknown items</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$Y = L - \\text{CL} = 250{,}000 - 120{,}000 = 130{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$Z = E - \\text{Contributed capital} = 250{,}000 - 200{,}000 = 50{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Present the complete balance sheet</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Order the items by the course layout: non-current before current assets; equity, then long-term, then current liabilities [Slide 18].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>500,000 m.u. on both sides; equity 50%.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment (net)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">280,000</td><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">200,000</td></tr><tr><td>Patents (net)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>320,000</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>250,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">70,000</td><td>Long-term bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">130,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">65,000</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>130,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">45,000</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Short-term bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>180,000</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>120,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>500,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>500,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the retained earnings at 1/1/2025 and the ROE of 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Retained earnings grow each year by the part of the net income that is not distributed [Slide 23].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Retained in 2025: 18,000 m.u.; retained earnings at 1/1/2025: 32,000 m.u.</li><li>ROE = 12% (extension).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Retained in 2025} = (1 - 0.40) \\times 30{,}000 = 18{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$Z_{1/1/2025} = Z_{31/12/2025} - 18{,}000 = 50{,}000 - 18{,}000 = 32{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROE} = \\frac{30{,}000}{250{,}000} = 12\\% \\quad \\text{(extension, on year-end equity)}$$</div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Consistency check:</strong> $\\text{CA}/\\text{CL} = 180{,}000/120{,}000 = 1.5$ and $\\text{CA} - \\text{CL} = 60{,}000$ ✓; $L/E = 250{,}000/250{,}000 = 1$ ✓.</li><li><strong>Why three ratios are enough:</strong> each ratio is one equation; together with the balance identity they determine four unknowns because two of them (CA, CL) are intermediate totals.</li><li><strong>Reading the result:</strong> a leverage of 1 means each m.u. of owners' money is matched by one m.u. of debt; the bank analyst will check that the current assets comfortably cover the current debts (they do, 1.5 times).</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P16", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "reverse-engineering-ratios", "theme": "reverse-engineering-ratios", "unit": 3, "title": "Stratos Ground Systems: rebuilding the income statement from margins", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 40, "tags": ["extra-practice", "income-statement", "ratios", "roa", "roe"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), using Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 9-11", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P16 - Stratos Ground Systems Income Statement from Margins.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Stratos Ground Systems S.L. builds antenna stations for satellite operators. In its 2025 annual report only percentages and a few amounts are published (m.u.):</p>\n<ul><li>gross margin: gross profit is 40% of sales;</li><li>operating expenses (salaries, rent, depreciation, etc.): 150,000;</li><li>the only debt that bears interest is a bank loan of 200,000 at 5% per year;</li><li>corporate income tax: 25% of the income before taxes;</li><li>net margin: net income is 6% of sales;</li><li>ROE: 10%; ROA: 8% (both on year-end figures, see Setup);</li><li>inventory: 40,000 at 1/1/2025 and 55,000 at 31/12/2025.</li></ul>", "setup_html": "<p>Write every line of the extended income statement [Slides 9-11] as a function of the unknown sales $S$. The net margin then gives one equation in $S$.</p>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> Net margin $= \\text{Net income}/\\text{Sales}$; $\\text{ROA} = \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{Total assets}$; $\\text{ROE} = \\text{Net income}/\\text{Equity}$. These are the definitions used in the solved Infopymes problem of the portal. Total assets and equity are taken at year end (31/12/2025).</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the sales of 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Gross profit $= 0.40\\,S$; income from operations $= 0.40\\,S - 150{,}000$; subtract the interest, apply the 25% tax, and set the result equal to $0.06\\,S$.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Interest 10,000 m.u.</li><li>Equation $0.75\\,(0.40\\,S - 160{,}000) = 0.06\\,S$.</li><li>Sales $S = 500{,}000$ m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Interest</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I = 0.05 \\times 200{,}000 = 10{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Lines as functions of $S$</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Gross profit} = 0.40\\,S, \\qquad \\text{Income from operations} = 0.40\\,S - 150{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Income before taxes} = 0.40\\,S - 150{,}000 - 10{,}000 = 0.40\\,S - 160{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Net income} = (1 - 0.25)(0.40\\,S - 160{,}000) = 0.75\\,(0.40\\,S - 160{,}000)$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Net margin equation</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$0.75\\,(0.40\\,S - 160{,}000) = 0.06\\,S$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$0.30\\,S - 120{,}000 = 0.06\\,S \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 0.24\\,S = 120{,}000 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; S = \\frac{120{,}000}{0.24} = 500{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Present the complete income statement</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Substitute $S = 500{,}000$ into each line of Step 2 and verify the net margin.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cost of goods sold 300,000; income from operations 50,000 m.u.</li><li>Net income 30,000 m.u. ($= 6\\%$ of 500,000 ✓).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td>Part (a)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>$0.60 \\times 500{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td>$0.40 \\times 500{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>200,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Operating expenses</td><td>given</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">150,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$200{,}000 - 150{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>50,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>$0.05 \\times 200{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$50{,}000 - 10{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>40,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (25%)</td><td>$0.25 \\times 40{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$40{,}000 - 10{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>30,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Check: $30{,}000 / 500{,}000 = 6\\%$ ✓.</p>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Use ROA and ROE to find total assets, equity, liabilities and the debt-to-equity ratio</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Each ratio has one known numerator from part (b): invert it to obtain its denominator.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Total assets 625,000; equity 300,000; liabilities 325,000 m.u.</li><li>$L/E = 1.08$; equity 48% of the financing.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{TA} = \\frac{\\text{Income from operations}}{\\text{ROA}} = \\frac{50{,}000}{0.08} = 625{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad E = \\frac{\\text{Net income}}{\\text{ROE}} = \\frac{30{,}000}{0.10} = 300{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$L = \\text{TA} - E = 625{,}000 - 300{,}000 = 325{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\frac{L}{E} = \\frac{325{,}000}{300{,}000} = 1.08, \\qquad \\%\\,E = \\frac{300{,}000}{625{,}000} = 48\\%$$</div>\n<p>Of the 325,000 m.u. of liabilities, only 200,000 bear interest; the other 125,000 are interest-free debts such as suppliers and taxes payable.</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the purchases of goods in 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Turn the relation $\\text{COGS} = \\text{Opening inventory} + \\text{Purchases} - \\text{Closing inventory}$ around.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Purchases 315,000 m.u.: more than the cost of goods sold because the inventory grew by 15,000.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Purchases} = \\text{COGS} + \\text{Closing inventory} - \\text{Opening inventory} = 300{,}000 + 55{,}000 - 40{,}000 = 315{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> margins and returns are dimensionless; dividing m.u. by a dimensionless ratio returns m.u.</li><li><strong>ROE above ROA here:</strong> $10\\% &gt; 8\\%$ because the debt finances part of the assets; the owners earn on the whole asset base while contributing only 48% of the money (financial leverage, [Slide 36]; the quantitative relation is an extension).</li><li><strong>Typical mistake:</strong> applying the tax rate to the income from operations instead of the income before taxes.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P17", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "reverse-engineering-ratios", "theme": "reverse-engineering-ratios", "unit": 3, "title": "Lumen Optics Space: both statements from the financial structure and the ROA", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 40, "tags": ["extra-practice", "financial-structure", "roa", "roe", "leverage"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), using Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 9-11 and 36", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P17 - Lumen Optics Space Statements from Structure and ROA.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Lumen Optics Space S.A. polishes mirrors for Earth-observation satellites. Its investor presentation for 2025 gives only the following information (m.u.):</p>\n<ul><li>total assets at 31/12/2025: 800,000;</li><li>equity finances 60% of the assets;</li><li>of the liabilities, 120,000 are interest-free (suppliers and taxes payable); the rest is a bank loan at 6% per year;</li><li>ROA (income from operations / total assets): 9%;</li><li>operating margin (income from operations / sales): 12%;</li><li>the cost of goods sold is 55% of sales;</li><li>tax rate: 30%.</li></ul>", "setup_html": "<p>The structure ratios of [Slide 36] fix the right-hand side of the balance sheet; the bank loan then fixes the interest. The ROA gives the income from operations, and from there the income statement can be completed both downward (to net income) and upward (to sales).</p>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> $\\text{ROA} = \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{TA}$; $\\text{ROE} = \\text{Net income}/E$; operating margin $= \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{Sales}$.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Find equity, liabilities, the bank loan and its interest</p>", "hint_html": "<p>$\\%\\,\\text{Equity} = E/(E+L)$ and $E + L = \\text{TA}$ [Slides 18, 36].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>$E = 480{,}000$; $L = 320{,}000$; bank loan 200,000 m.u.</li><li>Interest 12,000 m.u.; $L/E = 0.67$.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$E = 0.60 \\times 800{,}000 = 480{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad L = 800{,}000 - 480{,}000 = 320{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Bank loan} = 320{,}000 - 120{,}000 = 200{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad I = 0.06 \\times 200{,}000 = 12{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\frac{L}{E} = \\frac{320{,}000}{480{,}000} = 0.67$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Complete the income statement from the income from operations down to the net income</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Invert the ROA to get the income from operations, then subtract the interest and the tax.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income from operations 72,000; income before taxes 60,000 m.u.</li><li>Taxes 18,000; net income 42,000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Income from operations} = 0.09 \\times 800{,}000 = 72{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Income from operations</strong></td><td>$0.09 \\times 800{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>72,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>Part (a)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$72{,}000 - 12{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>60,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 60{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$60{,}000 - 18{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>42,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the ROE and compare it with the ROA</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compare the return on the assets with the cost of the bank loan: when the assets earn more than the debt costs, the surplus goes to the owners.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>ROE = 8.75% (extension).</li><li>ROE &lt; ROA here, although the assets earn more (9%) than the debt costs (6%): taxes reduce the owners' return.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROE} = \\frac{42{,}000}{480{,}000} = 8.75\\%$$</div>\n<p>The ROA (9%) is measured <strong>before</strong> interest and taxes, while the ROE (8.75%) is measured <strong>after</strong> both. The assets earn 9% and the bank loan costs only 6%, so debt raises the owners' return before taxes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\frac{\\text{Income before taxes}}{E} = \\frac{60{,}000}{480{,}000} = 12.5\\% &gt; 9\\%$$</div>\n<p>The 30% tax then brings it down to $0.70 \\times 12.5\\% = 8.75\\%$. Leverage helps the owners here; it is the tax, not the debt, that pushes the ROE below the ROA [Slide 36; the after-tax comparison is an extension].</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the sales, the cost of goods sold, the gross profit and the operating expenses</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Invert the operating margin to obtain the sales. The operating expenses are the gap between gross profit and income from operations.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Sales 600,000; cost of goods sold 330,000; gross profit 270,000 m.u.</li><li>Operating expenses 198,000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$S = \\frac{72{,}000}{0.12} = 600{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{COGS} = 0.55 \\times 600{,}000 = 330{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Gross profit} = 600{,}000 - 330{,}000 = 270{,}000, \\qquad \\text{Operating expenses} = 270{,}000 - 72{,}000 = 198{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">330,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>270,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Operating expenses</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">198,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>72,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>60,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>42,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> every ratio dimensionless; amounts in m.u.</li><li><strong>Order of reasoning:</strong> structure → interest → bottom of the income statement; ROA and margin → top of the income statement. Each published ratio is one equation.</li><li><strong>Net margin:</strong> $42{,}000 / 600{,}000 = 7\\%$ of sales reaches the owners.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P18", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "reverse-engineering-ratios", "theme": "reverse-engineering-ratios", "unit": 3, "title": "Corvid Drone Works: reconstructing equity from the pay-out and a rights issue", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 35, "tags": ["extra-practice", "equity", "self-financing", "rights-issue", "leverage"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), using Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 17, 23-26 and 36", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P18 - Corvid Drone Works Equity Reconstruction and Rights Issue.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Corvid Drone Works S.A. makes inspection drones for wind farms and airports. The equity section of its balance sheet is known only at 31/12/2024: contributed capital 150,000 m.u. (shares of nominal value 10 m.u.) and retained earnings 40,000 m.u. For 2025 the following is known (m.u.):</p>\n<ul><li>the pay-out ratio of 2025 was 35%, and the dividend was paid in cash in 2025;</li><li>retained earnings increased by 26,000 during 2025;</li><li>during 2025 the company issued new shares at their nominal value (no premium), fully paid in cash;</li><li>at 31/12/2025 total liabilities were 200,000 (long-term loan 120,000; accounts payable 67,000; taxes payable 13,000) and the debt-to-equity ratio was 0.8;</li><li>assets at 31/12/2025: non-current assets 300,000; inventory 60,000; accounts receivable 55,000; cash $X$.</li></ul>", "setup_html": "<p>Retained earnings change only by the undistributed profit [Slides 17, 23]:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\Delta\\,\\text{Retained earnings} = \\text{Net income} - \\text{Dividends} = (1 - \\text{pay-out}) \\times \\text{Net income}$$</div>\n<p>Contributed capital changes only through rights issues (capital increases) [Slides 25-26]. The debt-to-equity ratio [Slide 36] fixes the total equity at year end.</p>\n<p><strong>Extension (not defined in the course decks).</strong> $\\text{ROE} = \\text{Net income}/E$; current ratio $= \\text{CA}/\\text{CL}$.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the net income and the dividend of 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The 26,000 m.u. retained are the self-financing share of the profit, that is $1 - 0.35 = 65\\%$ of it.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Net income 40,000 m.u.; dividend 14,000 m.u.</li><li>Self-financing ratio 65%.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$0.65 \\times \\text{NI} = 26{,}000 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{NI} = \\frac{26{,}000}{0.65} = 40{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividend} = 0.35 \\times 40{,}000 = 14{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{check: } 40{,}000 - 14{,}000 = 26{,}000 \\;✓$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Find equity at 31/12/2025, the contributed capital and the number of new shares issued</p>", "hint_html": "<p>$L/E = 0.8$ with $L = 200{,}000$ gives $E$. Subtract the retained earnings at the close of 2025 to obtain the capital.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>$E = 250{,}000$; retained earnings 66,000; capital 184,000 m.u.</li><li>Rights issue 34,000 m.u. = 3,400 new shares.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$E = \\frac{L}{0.8} = \\frac{200{,}000}{0.8} = 250{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Retained earnings}_{2025} = 40{,}000 + 26{,}000 = 66{,}000, \\qquad \\text{Capital}_{2025} = 250{,}000 - 66{,}000 = 184{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Rights issue} = 184{,}000 - 150{,}000 = 34{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{New shares} = \\frac{34{,}000}{10} = 3{,}400$$</div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the cash $X$ and present the balance sheet at 31/12/2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Total assets equal $E + L$. Cash is the only missing asset.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Total 450,000 m.u.; $X = 35{,}000$ m.u.</li><li>Current ratio 1.88 (extension).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{TA} = 250{,}000 + 200{,}000 = 450{,}000, \\qquad X = 450{,}000 - 300{,}000 - 60{,}000 - 55{,}000 = 35{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>300,000</strong></td><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">184,000</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">66,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">55,000</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>250,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">35,000</td><td>Long-term loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>120,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">67,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>150,000</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>80,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>450,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>450,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Current ratio} = \\frac{150{,}000}{67{,}000 + 13{,}000} = \\frac{150{,}000}{80{,}000} = 1.88$$</div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the ROE of 2025 and compare the two ways in which equity grew</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Equity grew by internal equity (self-financing) and by external equity (the rights issue). Compare their sizes and their features in the course.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Equity grew from 190,000 to 250,000 (+60,000): 26,000 self-financing + 34,000 rights issue.</li><li>ROE = 16% (extension).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROE} = \\frac{40{,}000}{250{,}000} = 16\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Amount (m.u.)</th><th>Feature in the course</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-financing (retained earnings)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">26,000</td><td>Internal; generated by the activity; no new owners [Slides 23-24]</td></tr><tr><td>Rights issue</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">34,000</td><td>External equity; never repaid; new shareholders share future dividends [Slides 25-26]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>More than half of the growth of equity came from new shareholders. That allowed the company to reach a leverage of 0.8 without borrowing more, but it also spreads future dividends over 3,400 more shares.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> shares = m.u. / (m.u. per share); ratios dimensionless.</li><li><strong>Two equations, two unknowns:</strong> the pay-out fixes the profit split; the leverage fixes the size of equity. The rights issue is the residual.</li><li><strong>Typical mistake:</strong> dividing the 26,000 by the pay-out (35%) instead of by the self-financing ratio (65%).</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P19", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "Aerolift Drones: the first year told as a story", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 45, "tags": ["extra-practice", "income-statement", "balance-sheet", "cash", "depreciation"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), in the style of T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercises 1 and 2", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P19 - Aerolift Drones First Year of Operations.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>Two aerospace engineers founded <strong>Aerolift Drones S.L.</strong> on 1 January 2025 to assemble cargo drones for mountain rescue teams. Their consultant summarises the first year as follows (m.u.).</p>\n<p>The partners contributed 120,000 in cash on 1 January, and the same day a bank lent the company 100,000 at 6% per year for five years: on each 31 December the company repays 20,000 and pays the interest on the balance outstanding at the start of that year. On 1 March they bought the assembly line for 90,000; they paid 60,000 on delivery and agreed to pay the remaining 30,000 on 1 March 2026. The line will last six years, after which it can be sold for 18,000, and it is depreciated linearly.</p>\n<p>During the year they bought 1,000 drone kits at 150 each, the same number every month, paying the suppliers 90 days after each purchase. They sold 900 drones at 400 each, also evenly over the twelve months; the rescue teams pay 60 days after each sale. The 100 kits not yet assembled were still in the warehouse on 31 December.</p>\n<p>Salaries cost 8,000 per month, paid in the same month; Social Security adds 2,500 per month, paid in the following month. The workshop rent is 2,000 per month, paid monthly. The corporate tax is 25% of the profit and will be paid in 2026. No dividends are distributed.</p>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Timeline.</strong> Every flow is spread evenly over the months, so \"paid 90 days later\" means that the purchases of October, November and December are unpaid at 31/12; \"collected 60 days later\" leaves November and December sales uncollected.</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kits</td><td>1,000 at 150 = 150,000 (12,500 per month), paid at 90 days</td></tr><tr><td>Sales</td><td>900 at 400 = 360,000 (30,000 per month), collected at 60 days</td></tr><tr><td>Assembly line</td><td>90,000 on 1/3; 60,000 paid, 30,000 due 1/3/2026; 6 years; residual 18,000</td></tr><tr><td>Loan</td><td>100,000 at 6%; 20,000 repaid each 31/12</td></tr><tr><td>Staff</td><td>8,000/month salaries (paid same month) + 2,500/month Social Security (paid next month)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Accrual principle.</strong> Revenues are recorded when the sale is made and expenses when the goods and services are received, whether paid or not [Slide 8]. Cash only records what was actually collected or paid.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Only the 900 kits used in the drones sold are an expense of 2025; the other 100 are inventory. The assembly line worked for 10 months.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cost of goods sold 135,000; depreciation 10,000 m.u.</li><li>Income from operations 65,000; interest 6,000 m.u.</li><li>Net income 44,250 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Cost of goods sold and depreciation</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{COGS} = 900 \\times 150 = 135{,}000 \\text{ m.u.} \\quad (= 150{,}000 - 15{,}000 \\text{ closing inventory})$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D = \\frac{90{,}000 - 18{,}000}{6} \\times \\frac{10}{12} = 12{,}000 \\times \\frac{10}{12} = 10{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Interest</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{2025} = 0.06 \\times 100{,}000 = 6{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Income statement</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td>$900 \\times 400$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">360,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">135,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td>$360{,}000 - 135{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>225,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Salaries and Social Security</td><td>$12 \\times (8{,}000 + 2{,}500)$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">126,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Rent</td><td>$12 \\times 2{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">24,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$225{,}000 - (126{,}000 + 24{,}000 + 10{,}000) = 225{,}000 - 160{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>65,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>Step 2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$65{,}000 - 6{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>59,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (25%)</td><td>$0.25 \\times 59{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,750</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$59{,}000 - 14{,}750$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>44,250</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash balance at 31/12/2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Use the course's cash account: initial cash + collections − payments. List what is still unpaid or uncollected at 31/12 first; everything else moved cash.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Collections from customers 300,000; payments to suppliers 112,500 m.u.</li><li>Social Security paid 27,500 (11 months) m.u.</li><li>Cash at 31/12/2025: 174,000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. What is still open at 31/12</h4>\n<ul><li>Customers: November and December sales, $2 \\times 30{,}000 = 60{,}000$ uncollected → collected $360{,}000 - 60{,}000 = 300{,}000$.</li><li>Suppliers of kits: October to December, $3 \\times 12{,}500 = 37{,}500$ unpaid → paid $150{,}000 - 37{,}500 = 112{,}500$.</li><li>Social Security of December, 2,500 unpaid → paid $11 \\times 2{,}500 = 27{,}500$.</li><li>Assembly line: 30,000 unpaid → paid 60,000.</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Cash account (initial cash + inflows − outflows)</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120,000</td><td>Assembly line</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td>Bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td><td>Suppliers of kits</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">112,500</td></tr><tr><td>Collections from customers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td><td>Salaries</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">96,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Social Security</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">27,500</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Rent</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">24,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Loan repayment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>520,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>346,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{31/12} = 0 + 520{,}000 - 346{,}000 = 174{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 31/12/2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Every amount \"still open\" in part (b) is either a receivable or a payable. Split the loan: the instalment of 31/12/2026 is current.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Non-current assets 80,000; current assets 249,000 m.u.</li><li>Equity 164,250; current liabilities 104,750 m.u.</li><li>Total 329,000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Assembly line</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">90,000</td><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(10,000)</td><td>Retained earnings (profit of 2025)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">44,250</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>80,000</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>164,250</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Inventory (100 kits × 150)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,000</td><td>Bank loan due after 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>60,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">174,000</td><td>Bank loan due 31/12/2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Supplier of the assembly line</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Suppliers of kits</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">37,500</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Social Security payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,500</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Income taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,750</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>249,000</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>104,750</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>329,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>329,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Give a short diagnosis of the financial health of Aerolift</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compute the working capital (extension) and the debt-to-equity ratio, then ask whether the cash balance is well used.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Working capital 144,250 m.u. (extension); $L/E = 164{,}750/164{,}250 = 1.00$.</li><li>Idle cash of 174,000 m.u. while paying 6% on the loan.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Working capital} = 249{,}000 - 104{,}750 = 144{,}250 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\frac{L}{E} = \\frac{60{,}000 + 104{,}750}{164{,}250} = 1.00$$</div>\n<p>The first year is profitable (net margin $44{,}250/360{,}000 = 12.3\\%$) and liquid: the current assets more than double the current liabilities. Leverage is balanced (one m.u. of debt per m.u. of equity). The weak point is the large idle cash balance: 174,000 m.u. earn nothing while the loan costs 6%. The company could repay debt early or invest in a second assembly line.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Profit vs cash:</strong> net income 44,250 m.u., but cash grew by 174,000 m.u. because it also received the capital and the loan, and because part of its expenses (kits, tax, Social Security) is still unpaid.</li><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> units × m.u./unit = m.u.; m.u./year × months/12 = m.u.</li><li><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> expensing all 1,000 kits, depreciating 12 months, and forgetting that the residual value is not depreciated.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P20", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "Kestrel MRO: a half-year with renting, a credit line and accrued interest", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 50, "tags": ["extra-practice", "half-year", "credit-line", "renting", "cash", "balance-sheet"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), in the style of T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf, Exercise 1 (CIRCONSA)", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P20 - Kestrel MRO Half-Year with Renting and a Credit Line.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p><strong>Kestrel MRO S.L.</strong> maintains the light aircraft of three flying schools. Its balance sheet at 31/12/2025 was (m.u.):</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tools and equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">70,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(16,000)</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td>Spare parts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td><td>Long-term loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,000</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">22,000</td><td>Taxes payable (2025)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>110,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>110,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The manager must present the statements at 30/06/2026. During the first half of 2026:</p>\n<ol><li>Kestrel billed 30,000 per month for maintenance; the schools pay 30 days after each invoice. The receivables of 2025 were collected in January, except 2,000 from a school that has agreed to pay in September 2026.</li><li>Spare parts were bought for 8,000 per month, paid at 60 days; the suppliers' balance of 2025 was paid in January. The physical count of 30/06 shows 12,000 of spare parts in stock.</li><li>The mechanics' salaries are 12,000 per month, paid monthly.</li><li>From 1 March, Kestrel rents an engine test bench (renting contract) for 1,500 per month, paid at the end of each month.</li><li>On 1 April a bank opened a credit line of 40,000; Kestrel drew 20,000 that day. Interest is 6% per year on the amount drawn and is paid at the end of each quarter.</li><li>The tax of 2025 was paid in January.</li><li>On 1 May Kestrel bought a borescope diagnostic kit for 24,000 in cash, with a useful life of 5 years and no residual value. The old equipment is depreciated at 8,000 per year.</li><li>The long-term loan bears 4% per year, paid every 31 December.</li><li>The tax rate is 25%; the tax on the half-year profit will be paid later.</li></ol>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Time window:</strong> 1/1/2026 to 30/06/2026, six months. Expenses are recognised when incurred, even if not paid (the half-year interest of the long-term loan has accrued but is paid only in December) [Slide 8].</p>\n<p><strong>Renting</strong> works like an operating lease [Slide 33] with no purchase option used: the monthly fee is an operating expense and no asset is recorded, as in the CIRCONSA exercise.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify every operation by its effect on the income statement, on cash and on the balance sheet at 30/06</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Build the three-column table used in the method note of the portal: income statement, cash, and what is still open at 30/06.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>June invoices (30,000) and the deferred 2,000 stay as receivables.</li><li>Parts of May and June (16,000) stay as payables; parts consumed 46,000.</li><li>Accrued interest on the long-term loan: 300, current liability.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Income statement H1</th><th>Cash H1</th><th>Open at 30/06/2026</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Maintenance invoices</td><td>Revenue 180,000</td><td>+150,000 (Jan–May) +12,000 (2025 balance)</td><td>Receivables 30,000 + 2,000</td></tr><tr><td>2. Spare parts</td><td>Consumption 46,000</td><td>−32,000 (Jan–Apr) −9,000 (2025 balance)</td><td>Payables 16,000; stock 12,000</td></tr><tr><td>3. Salaries</td><td>72,000</td><td>−72,000</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>4. Test bench renting</td><td>6,000 (4 months)</td><td>−6,000</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>5. Credit line</td><td>Interest 300</td><td>+20,000 −300</td><td>Credit line 20,000</td></tr><tr><td>6. Tax 2025</td><td>—</td><td>−4,000</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>7. Borescope kit</td><td>Depreciation 800</td><td>−24,000</td><td>Equipment +24,000</td></tr><tr><td>Old equipment</td><td>Depreciation 4,000</td><td>—</td><td>Accumulated depreciation</td></tr><tr><td>8. Long-term loan</td><td>Interest 300</td><td>—</td><td>Interest payable 300</td></tr><tr><td>9. Tax H1</td><td>25% of the profit</td><td>—</td><td>Taxes payable</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement of the half-year</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Parts consumed = opening stock + purchases − closing stock. The kit works for two months; the credit line for three months.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Parts consumed 46,000; depreciation 4,800 m.u.</li><li>Income from operations 51,200; interest 600 m.u.</li><li>Net income 37,950 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Computations</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Parts consumed} = 10{,}000 + 6 \\times 8{,}000 - 12{,}000 = 46{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D = 8{,}000 \\times \\frac{6}{12} + \\frac{24{,}000}{5} \\times \\frac{2}{12} = 4{,}000 + 800 = 4{,}800 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{\\text{credit line}} = 20{,}000 \\times 0.06 \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 300, \\qquad I_{\\text{loan}} = 15{,}000 \\times 0.04 \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 300 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Income statement 1/1–30/6/2026</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Maintenance revenue</td><td>$6 \\times 30{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Spare parts consumed</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">46,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Salaries</td><td>$6 \\times 12{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">72,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Renting of the test bench</td><td>$4 \\times 1{,}500$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4,800</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$180{,}000 - (46{,}000 + 72{,}000 + 6{,}000 + 4{,}800) = 180{,}000 - 128{,}800$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>51,200</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>$300 + 300$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td>$51{,}200 - 600$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>50,600</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (25%)</td><td>$0.25 \\times 50{,}600$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,650</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td>$50{,}600 - 12{,}650$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>37,950</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash balance at 30/06/2026</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Start from the 22,000 of the opening balance sheet. Each column of the cash row in part (a) is one line of the cash account.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Inflows 182,000; outflows 147,300 m.u.</li><li>Cash at 30/06/2026: 56,700 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025 receivables collected</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td><td>2025 suppliers paid</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9,000</td></tr><tr><td>Invoices January–May</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">150,000</td><td>Parts January–April</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">32,000</td></tr><tr><td>Credit line drawn</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td><td>Salaries</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">72,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Renting</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Credit-line interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Tax 2025</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Borescope kit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">24,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>182,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>147,300</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{30/06} = 22{,}000 + 182{,}000 - 147{,}300 = 56{,}700 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 30/06/2026</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The half-year profit is not distributed; add it to equity as the result of the period. Everything open in part (a) appears here.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Non-current assets 83,200; current assets 100,700 m.u.</li><li>Equity 119,950; current liabilities 48,950 m.u.</li><li>Total 183,900 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tools and equipment (80,000 + 24,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">104,000</td><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">70,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation (16,000 + 4,800)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(20,800)</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>83,200</strong></td><td>Profit of the half-year</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">37,950</td></tr><tr><td>Spare parts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>119,950</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable (30,000 + 2,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">32,000</td><td>Long-term loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>15,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">56,700</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">16,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Bank credit line</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Interest payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,650</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>100,700</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>48,950</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>183,900</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>183,900</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The receivable deferred to September 2026 is still within one year, so it stays current.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Bank credit vs loan:</strong> Kestrel pays interest only on the 20,000 drawn, not on the 40,000 limit [Slide 31].</li><li><strong>Accrual check:</strong> the 300 m.u. of interest on the long-term loan reduce the profit of the half-year although no money has left the company yet.</li><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> m.u./year × months/12 = m.u. for depreciation and interest.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P21", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "Orbitron Satellite Components: bonds, a rights issue and factoring in one year", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 50, "tags": ["extra-practice", "bonds", "rights-issue", "factoring", "cash", "balance-sheet"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), using Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 21-22, 25-29 and 35", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P21 - Orbitron Satellite Components Bonds Rights Issue and Factoring.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p><strong>Orbitron Satellite Components S.A.</strong> builds reaction wheels for small satellites. On 1/1/2025 its balance sheet showed (m.u.): plant at cost 400,000 with accumulated depreciation 100,000; inventory 30,000; accounts receivable 50,000; cash 40,000; contributed capital 250,000 (shares of nominal value 10); retained earnings 60,000; long-term bank loan 70,000; accounts payable 40,000.</p>\n<p>The finance director tells the story of 2025 like this:</p>\n<p>\"We sold for 600,000, all on credit. We collected the 50,000 pending from 2024 and 500,000 of this year's sales. To get cash before Christmas, on 1 December we sold 40,000 of our receivables to a factoring company, which paid us at once after keeping a 3% fee. We bought materials for 320,000 on credit; we paid the 40,000 owed from 2024 and 280,000 of this year's purchases. At year end the warehouse holds 35,000 of materials.</p>\n<p>Salaries were 140,000 and other operating expenses 25,000, all paid. The plant is depreciated at 10% of its cost per year. On 1 July we bought a thermal-vacuum test chamber for 180,000 in cash; it will last 10 years with no residual value. To pay for it, the same day we issued bonds for 200,000 at par, maturing in 2030, with a 5% coupon paid every 30 June. On 1 October we also issued 5,000 new shares at nominal value, fully paid in cash.</p>\n<p>The bank loan costs 5% per year on the balance at the start of the year; on 31 December we paid the interest and repaid 14,000, and we will repay another 14,000 at the end of 2026. The tax rate is 30% and the tax is paid next year. The board paid a dividend of 40% of the net income on 31 December.\"</p>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Financing operations of the year</strong> and their place in the map of the financial structure [Slides 21-22]: bonds and the bank loan are long-term external liabilities [Slides 29, 31]; the rights issue is external equity [Slides 25-26]; retained earnings are internal equity (self-financing) [Slide 23]; factoring is short-term external financing [Slide 35].</p>\n<p><strong>Factoring</strong> (without recourse, as described in the course): the receivable is sold, so it leaves the balance sheet; the factor's fee is a financial (non-operating) expense.</p>\n<p><strong>Accrued coupon:</strong> the bonds have been outstanding for 6 months at 31/12, so half a year of coupon has accrued but will be paid on 30/06/2026.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify the financing operations of 2025 by nature and by origin</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Nature: equity or liabilities. Origin: company's own resources or external resources [Slides 21-22].</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Bonds 200,000 and bank loan: liabilities, external, long-term.</li><li>Rights issue 50,000: equity, external. Retained earnings: equity, internal.</li><li>Factoring: short-term external financing that brings cash forward; no liability is recorded (the receivable is sold).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Nature</th><th>Origin</th><th>Term</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bond issue, 200,000</td><td>Liability</td><td>External</td><td>Long term (2030)</td></tr><tr><td>Bank loan (remaining)</td><td>Liability</td><td>External</td><td>Long and short term</td></tr><tr><td>Rights issue, 5,000 shares</td><td>Equity</td><td>External</td><td>Permanent (never repaid)</td></tr><tr><td>Retained earnings of 2025</td><td>Equity</td><td>Internal (self-financing)</td><td>Permanent</td></tr><tr><td>Factoring of 40,000</td><td>Short-term external resource</td><td>External</td><td>Short term</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts payable</td><td>Liability</td><td>External (trade credit)</td><td>Short term</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Three non-operating expenses: bank interest, the accrued bond coupon (6 months) and the factoring fee. The test chamber is depreciated for 6 months.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cost of goods sold 315,000; depreciation 49,000 m.u.</li><li>Income from operations 71,000; non-operating expenses 9,700 m.u.</li><li>Net income 42,910 m.u.; dividend 17,164 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Computations</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{COGS} = 30{,}000 + 320{,}000 - 35{,}000 = 315{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D = 0.10 \\times 400{,}000 + \\frac{180{,}000}{10} \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 40{,}000 + 9{,}000 = 49{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I_{\\text{bank}} = 0.05 \\times 70{,}000 = 3{,}500, \\qquad I_{\\text{bonds}} = 0.05 \\times 200{,}000 \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 5{,}000, \\qquad \\text{Fee} = 0.03 \\times 40{,}000 = 1{,}200$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Income statement</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">600,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">315,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>285,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Salaries</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">140,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Other operating expenses</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">25,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">49,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$285{,}000 - 214{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>71,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Bank interest, bond coupon, factoring fee</td><td>$3{,}500 + 5{,}000 + 1{,}200$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9,700</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>61,300</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 61{,}300$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18,390</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>42,910</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividend} = 0.40 \\times 42{,}910 = 17{,}164, \\qquad \\text{Retained} = 42{,}910 - 17{,}164 = 25{,}746 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash balance at 31/12/2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The factor pays the receivable minus its fee. The coupon and the tax have not been paid yet; the dividend has.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Factoring inflow 38,800 m.u.</li><li>Inflows 838,800; outflows 699,664 m.u.</li><li>Cash at 31/12/2025: 179,136 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Customers (50,000 + 500,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">550,000</td><td>Suppliers (40,000 + 280,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">320,000</td></tr><tr><td>Factoring ($40{,}000 - 1{,}200$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">38,800</td><td>Salaries</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">140,000</td></tr><tr><td>Bond issue</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">200,000</td><td>Other operating expenses</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">25,000</td></tr><tr><td>Rights issue ($5{,}000 \\times 10$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50,000</td><td>Test chamber</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Bank interest</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,500</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Loan repayment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividend</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">17,164</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>838,800</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>699,664</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{31/12} = 40{,}000 + 838{,}800 - 699{,}664 = 179{,}136 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 31/12/2025 and compute the financial structure ratios</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Which of this year's sales are neither collected nor sold to the factor? Split the remaining bank loan by the date of each instalment.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Receivables 60,000; non-current assets 431,000 m.u.</li><li>Equity 385,746; long-term liabilities 242,000; current liabilities 77,390 m.u.</li><li>Total 705,136 m.u.; %equity 54.7%; $L/E = 0.83$.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Balance sheet</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plant and test chamber (400,000 + 180,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">580,000</td><td>Contributed capital (250,000 + 50,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation (100,000 + 49,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(149,000)</td><td>Retained earnings (60,000 + 25,746)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">85,746</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>431,000</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>385,746</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">35,000</td><td>Bonds (2030)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">200,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td><td>Bank loan due after 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">42,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">179,136</td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>242,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Bank loan due in 2026</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Coupon payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Income taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18,390</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>274,136</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>77,390</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>705,136</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>705,136</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Structure ratios [Slide 36]</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$L = 242{,}000 + 77{,}390 = 319{,}390, \\qquad \\%\\,E = \\frac{385{,}746}{705{,}136} = 54.7\\%, \\qquad \\frac{L}{E} = \\frac{319{,}390}{385{,}746} = 0.83$$</div>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Long-term asset, long-term money:</strong> the 180,000 test chamber was financed with 200,000 of bonds maturing in 2030, matching the life of the investment.</li><li><strong>Factoring cost:</strong> bringing 40,000 forward by about a month cost 1,200 m.u.; it is an expensive source but it shifts the risk of non-payment to the factor [Slide 35].</li><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> all amounts in m.u.; coupon = rate × nominal × time fraction.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P22", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "narrative-case", "theme": "narrative-case", "unit": 3, "title": "Albatross Avionics: a discounted bill of exchange and the sale of a used van", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 50, "tags": ["extra-practice", "trade-discount", "non-operating-items", "cash", "balance-sheet"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), using Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 11 and 35, in the style of T3_Additional_EXERCISES.pdf", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P22 - Albatross Avionics Discounted Bill and Sale of a Van.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p><strong>Albatross Avionics S.L.</strong> distributes certified radio altimeters to aircraft workshops. Its balance sheet at 31/12/2024 was (m.u.): warehouse equipment 60,000 (bought 1/1/2021, 10 years of life, no residual value) with accumulated depreciation 24,000; a delivery van bought for 20,000 with accumulated depreciation 12,000 (it is depreciated at 4,000 per year); inventory of 80 altimeters at 500 each; accounts receivable 30,000; cash 25,000; contributed capital 100,000; retained earnings 9,000; accounts payable 30,000.</p>\n<p>In 2025 Albatross bought 400 altimeters at 500 each. It paid the 30,000 owed from 2024 and all the purchases of the year except 25,000, still pending at 31 December. It sold 380 altimeters at 800 each. It collected the 30,000 pending from 2024 and 244,000 from the 2025 sales. For the rest of its December sales, a large workshop signed a bill of exchange of 60,000 maturing on 31/3/2026; on 1 December Albatross took the bill to its bank, which advanced the money after deducting 1,200 of interest and commission. If the workshop does not pay at maturity, the bank will claim the money from Albatross.</p>\n<p>On 30 June Albatross sold the old van for 11,000 in cash. Staff costs were 50,000 and transport and insurance 12,000, all paid. The tax rate is 30% (paid in 2026), and a dividend of 50% of the net income was paid on 31 December.</p>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Trade discount</strong> [Slide 35]: the bank advances the amount of a bill before it falls due, after deducting interest and commission. Because the bank keeps the right to claim from Albatross if the customer does not pay, the bill stays among Albatross's receivables and the bank's advance is a short-term debt until 31/3/2026; the 1,200 are a financial expense. For simplicity, as in the course exercises, the whole 1,200 is charged to 2025 instead of being spread over the four months of the advance.</p>\n<p><strong>Sale of a fixed asset</strong> [Slide 11]: depreciate it up to the date of sale; the gain (or loss) is the price minus the book value, and it is a non-operating item.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the gain on the sale of the van and explain where it goes in the income statement</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Book value at sale = cost − accumulated depreciation <strong>at 30/06/2025</strong>, not at 1/1/2025.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Depreciation of the van in 2025: 2,000 m.u.; accumulated at sale 14,000 m.u.</li><li>Book value 6,000 m.u.; gain 5,000 m.u., non-operating.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$D_{\\text{van, 2025}} = 4{,}000 \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 2{,}000, \\qquad \\text{Accumulated at sale} = 12{,}000 + 2{,}000 = 14{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Book value} = 20{,}000 - 14{,}000 = 6{,}000, \\qquad \\text{Gain} = 11{,}000 - 6{,}000 = 5{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Albatross sells avionics, not vans: the gain does not come from its primary operations, so it is a non-operating item placed after the income from operations [Slide 11]. The 2,000 of depreciation are an operating expense (the van was used for the business until June).</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement for 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Inventory moves in units: $80 + 400 - 380 = 100$ altimeters remain, all at 500.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cost of goods sold 190,000; depreciation 8,000 m.u.</li><li>Income from operations 44,000; non-operating +3,800 m.u.</li><li>Net income 33,460 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Cost of goods sold and depreciation</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{COGS} = 380 \\times 500 = 190{,}000 = 40{,}000 + 200{,}000 - 50{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$D = \\frac{60{,}000}{10} + 2{,}000 = 6{,}000 + 2{,}000 = 8{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Income statement</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td>$380 \\times 800$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">304,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Cost of goods sold</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">190,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Gross profit</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>114,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Staff</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Transport and insurance</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td>$114{,}000 - 70{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>44,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>+ Gain on the sale of the van</td><td>Part (a)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Discount interest and commission</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>47,800</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td>$0.30 \\times 47{,}800$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,340</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>33,460</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the cash at 31/12/2025 and prepare the balance sheet</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Which receivable is still open at year end? The bank's advance is recorded at the amount owed to the bank, not at the cash received.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Cash 85,070 m.u.</li><li>Current assets 195,070; current liabilities 99,340 m.u.</li><li>Total 225,070 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Cash account</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividend} = 0.50 \\times 33{,}460 = 16{,}730 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Receivables of 2024</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td><td>Payables of 2024</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td></tr><tr><td>Collections of 2025 sales</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">244,000</td><td>Purchases of 2025 ($200{,}000 - 25{,}000$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">175,000</td></tr><tr><td>Bank advance on the bill ($60{,}000 - 1{,}200$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">58,800</td><td>Staff</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50,000</td></tr><tr><td>Sale of the van</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">11,000</td><td>Transport and insurance</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividend</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">16,730</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>343,800</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>283,730</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{31/12} = 25{,}000 + 343{,}800 - 283{,}730 = 85{,}070 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Balance sheet</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Warehouse equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation (24,000 + 6,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(30,000)</td><td>Retained earnings (9,000 + 16,730)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">25,730</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>30,000</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>125,730</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Inventory (100 × 500)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50,000</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">25,000</td></tr><tr><td>Bill receivable (discounted)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td><td>Bank debt for the discounted bill</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">85,070</td><td>Income taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">14,340</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>195,070</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>99,340</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>225,070</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>225,070</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The van has left the balance sheet: its cost (20,000) and its accumulated depreciation (14,000) are both removed.</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Compare the trade discount with factoring the same receivable</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Who bears the risk that the workshop does not pay? What happens to the receivable in each case [Slide 35]?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Trade discount: risk stays with Albatross; receivable and bank debt both stay until maturity.</li><li>Factoring: the factor assumes the insolvency risk; the receivable leaves the balance sheet and there is no bank debt.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>With the <strong>trade discount</strong>, Albatross gets the cash early but keeps the risk: if the workshop fails to pay on 31/3/2026, the bank will charge Albatross. That is why both the bill and the 60,000 debt appear until maturity. With <strong>factoring</strong>, the company sells the credit to the factoring company, which collects it and <strong>assumes the risk of insolvency</strong> [Slide 35]: the receivable would disappear from the balance sheet, no bank debt would be recorded, and the total assets would be 60,000 m.u. smaller. Factoring usually costs more because it includes that risk.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Liquidity:</strong> working capital $= 195{,}070 - 99{,}340 = 95{,}730$ m.u. (extension); about 60,000 of the current assets will cancel against the bank debt in March.</li><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> units × m.u./unit = m.u.</li><li><strong>Typical mistakes:</strong> computing the van's book value at 1/1/2025, and treating the bank advance as revenue.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P23", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "year-on-year-cash", "theme": "year-on-year-cash", "unit": 3, "title": "Pegasus Aerostructures: a plant expansion seen through two balance sheets", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 45, "tags": ["extra-practice", "cash-flow", "working-capital", "roa", "roe", "leverage"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise); the three-section cash-flow statement is an extension of Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P23 - Pegasus Aerostructures Plant Expansion and Cash Flows.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>In January 2025 <strong>Pegasus Aerostructures S.A.</strong>, a maker of wing ribs, doubled its plant capacity with a 300,000 m.u. investment financed by a new long-term bank loan of 300,000 m.u. drawn on 1/1/2025. Its statements are (m.u.):</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Balance sheet at 31/12</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2024</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2025</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plant at cost</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">800,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(150,000)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(220,000)</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">90,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">70,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">110,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">73,000</td></tr><tr><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td></tr><tr><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">110,000</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term bank loans</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">330,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">70,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">95,000</td></tr><tr><td>Taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Income statement</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2024</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2025</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales revenue</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">820,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">900,000</td></tr><tr><td>Income from operations</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">109,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">90,000</td></tr><tr><td>Depreciation included above</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">70,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest expense</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18,000</td></tr><tr><td>Tax rate</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">25%</td></tr><tr><td>Net income</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">70,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">54,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In 2025 the old loan was partly repaid, and dividends were paid in cash. No plant was sold.</p>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Extension: cash-flow statement, indirect method.</strong> The change in cash of the year splits into three flows:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\Delta\\text{Cash} = \\underbrace{\\text{CFO}}_{\\text{operations}} + \\underbrace{\\text{CFI}}_{\\text{investing}} + \\underbrace{\\text{CFF}}_{\\text{financing}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{CFO} = \\text{Net income} + \\text{Depreciation} - \\Delta\\text{Inventory} - \\Delta\\text{Receivables} + \\Delta\\text{Payables} + \\Delta\\text{Taxes payable}$$</div>\n<p>Depreciation is added back because it is an expense that did not use cash. An increase in an operating asset absorbs cash; an increase in an operating liability provides it.</p>\n<p><strong>Extension:</strong> working capital $= \\text{CA} - \\text{CL}$; $\\text{ROA} = \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{TA}$; $\\text{ROE} = \\text{Net income}/E$ (year-end figures). The debt-to-equity ratio is course material [Slide 36].</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the totals of both balance sheets and the working capital of each year</p>", "hint_html": "<p>All the liabilities in this company except the bank loans are current.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Total assets 530,000 (2024) and 853,000 (2025) m.u.</li><li>Working capital 80,000 → 160,000 m.u. (+80,000).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Block</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2024</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2025</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Non-current assets (net plant)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">350,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">580,000</td></tr><tr><td>Current assets</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">273,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>530,000</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>853,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Equity</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">380,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">410,000</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term liabilities</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">50,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">330,000</td></tr><tr><td>Current liabilities</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">113,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>530,000</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>853,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{WC}_{2024} = 180{,}000 - 100{,}000 = 80{,}000, \\qquad \\text{WC}_{2025} = 273{,}000 - 113{,}000 = 160{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the cash-flow statement of 2025 in three sections</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Find first the two flows the statement does not give: the repayment of the old loan (from the change in total bank loans) and the dividend (from the change in retained earnings).</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>CFO = 67,000 m.u.</li><li>CFI = −300,000 m.u.</li><li>Repayment 20,000; dividend 24,000; CFF = +256,000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Hidden flows</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Loans: } 50{,}000 + 300{,}000 - \\text{Repayment} = 330{,}000 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{Repayment} = 20{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Retained earnings: } 80{,}000 + 54{,}000 - \\text{Dividend} = 110{,}000 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{Dividend} = 24{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Plant: } 800{,}000 - 500{,}000 = 300{,}000 \\text{ invested;} \\quad 220{,}000 - 150{,}000 = 70{,}000 = \\text{depreciation} \\;✓$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Cash-flow statement 2025 (extension)</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Net income</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">54,000</td></tr><tr><td>+ Depreciation</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">70,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Increase in inventory</td><td>$90{,}000 - 60{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−30,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Increase in receivables</td><td>$110{,}000 - 70{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−40,000</td></tr><tr><td>+ Increase in payables</td><td>$95{,}000 - 70{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+25,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Decrease in taxes payable</td><td>$18{,}000 - 30{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−12,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Cash flow from operations (CFO)</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>67,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Investment in plant</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−300,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Cash flow from investing (CFI)</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−300,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>+ New bank loan</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+300,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Repayment of the old loan</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−20,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Dividends paid</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−24,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Cash flow from financing (CFF)</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>+256,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Check the result against the balance sheets</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The three flows must add up to the change in the cash line.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>$67{,}000 - 300{,}000 + 256{,}000 = 23{,}000 = 73{,}000 - 50{,}000$ ✓.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{CFO} + \\text{CFI} + \\text{CFF} = 67{,}000 - 300{,}000 + 256{,}000 = 23{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\Delta\\text{Cash} = 73{,}000 - 50{,}000 = 23{,}000 \\text{ m.u.} \\;✓$$</div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute ROA, ROE and debt-to-equity for both years and assess the impact of the expansion</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The expansion doubled the asset base in January, but its returns take time to appear. Compare also how the financial structure changed.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>ROA 20.6% → 10.6%; ROE 18.4% → 13.2% (extension).</li><li>$L/E$ 0.39 → 1.08.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ratio</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2024</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2025</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ROA $= \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{TA}$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$109{,}000/530{,}000 = 20.6\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$90{,}000/853{,}000 = 10.6\\%$</td></tr><tr><td>ROE $= \\text{NI}/E$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$70{,}000/380{,}000 = 18.4\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$54{,}000/410{,}000 = 13.2\\%$</td></tr><tr><td>$L/E$ [Slide 36]</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$150{,}000/380{,}000 = 0.39$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$443{,}000/410{,}000 = 1.08$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In its first year the expansion has <strong>not yet paid off</strong>: sales grew by only 9.8% while the assets grew by 61%, so the return on assets fell sharply and the higher depreciation (+40%), the doubled interest and the lower tax rate only partly offsetting them pushed the net income down. The financial structure moved from low to balanced leverage, which raises the risk for shareholders [Slide 36]. The operations still generate cash (CFO 67,000) and working capital doubled, so the company is not in liquidity trouble; the question for 2026 is whether sales will fill the new capacity.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Why CFO ≠ net income:</strong> depreciation (+70,000) and the growth of inventory and receivables (−70,000) cancel exactly; the higher payables (+25,000) and the lower tax payable (−12,000) leave CFO 13,000 above the net income.</li><li><strong>Investment financed long-term:</strong> CFI ≈ −CFF, so the plant was paid with long-term money, not with the cash from operations.</li><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> every flow and change in m.u.; ratios dimensionless.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P24", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "year-on-year-cash", "theme": "year-on-year-cash", "unit": 3, "title": "Halcyon Regional Airline: what a 15% fall in sales does to profit and cash", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 45, "tags": ["extra-practice", "cash-flow", "margins", "roe", "losses"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise); margins, ROE and the three-section cash-flow statement are extensions of Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P24 - Halcyon Regional Airline Falling Sales Year on Year.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p><strong>Halcyon Regional Airline S.A.</strong> flies island routes with turboprops. In 2025 a new ferry service took many of its passengers. Its statements are (m.u.):</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Income statement</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2024</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2025</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Passenger revenue</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,020,000</td></tr><tr><td>Operating expenses except depreciation (fuel, crews, fees, maintenance, leases)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,000,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">930,000</td></tr><tr><td>Depreciation of aircraft</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest expense</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">22,000</td></tr><tr><td>Tax rate</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Balance sheet at 31/12</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2024</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2025</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Aircraft (net)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">800,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">720,000</td></tr><tr><td>Spare parts</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">45,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable (travel agencies)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">75,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">103,000</td></tr><tr><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">400,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">400,000</td></tr><tr><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">150,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">?</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">280,000</td></tr><tr><td>Short-term bank credit</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td></tr><tr><td>Taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In 2025 Halcyon paid a dividend of 35,000 m.u. out of the 2024 profit. No aircraft were bought or sold.</p>", "setup_html": "<p>A company with a loss pays no corporate tax that year; the loss can be offset against future profits (as in the WIFINET exercise of the course), which is not needed here.</p>\n<p><strong>Extensions.</strong> Operating margin $= \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{Sales}$; net margin $= \\text{NI}/\\text{Sales}$; $\\text{ROE} = \\text{NI}/E$; working capital $= \\text{CA} - \\text{CL}$; indirect cash-flow statement as in Bank P23.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare both income statements, compute the operating and net margins, and explain why profit fell much more than sales</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Which costs moved with the passengers and which did not? Depreciation, crews on fixed contracts and leases do not shrink when sales fall.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Income from operations 120,000 → 10,000 m.u.; net income 70,000 → −12,000 m.u.</li><li>Operating margin 10.0% → 1.0%; net margin 5.8% → −1.2%.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2024</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2025</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Passenger revenue</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,200,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,020,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Operating expenses except depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,000,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">930,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income from operations</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>120,000</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>10,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">22,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>100,000</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−12,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (30%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>70,000</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−12,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Operating margin: } \\frac{120{,}000}{1{,}200{,}000} = 10.0\\% \\;\\to\\; \\frac{10{,}000}{1{,}020{,}000} = 1.0\\%; \\qquad \\text{Net margin: } 5.8\\% \\;\\to\\; -1.2\\%$$</div>\n<p>Sales fell by $180{,}000/1{,}200{,}000 = 15\\%$, but operating expenses fell by only $70{,}000/1{,}000{,}000 = 7\\%$ and depreciation not at all: much of an airline's cost is fixed in the short run. Income from operations therefore fell by $110{,}000/120{,}000 = 92\\%$.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the retained earnings of 2025 and prepare the cash-flow statement of 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Retained earnings: start from 150,000, subtract the loss and the dividend. In the cash-flow statement, the loss is the starting line and the drop in taxes payable absorbs cash.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Retained earnings 2025: 103,000 m.u.</li><li>CFO = −2,000; CFI = 0; CFF = +5,000 m.u.</li><li>Δcash = +3,000 m.u. ✓</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Retained earnings</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{RE}_{2025} = 150{,}000 - 12{,}000 - 35{,}000 = 103{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>Check of the 2025 balance: assets $720{,}000 + 45{,}000 + 75{,}000 + 103{,}000 = 943{,}000$; liabilities and equity $400{,}000 + 103{,}000 + 280{,}000 + 60{,}000 + 100{,}000 + 0 = 943{,}000$ ✓.</p>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Cash-flow statement 2025 (extension)</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Net income (loss)</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−12,000</td></tr><tr><td>+ Depreciation</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Increase in spare parts</td><td>$45{,}000 - 40{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−5,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Increase in receivables</td><td>$75{,}000 - 60{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−15,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Decrease in payables</td><td>$100{,}000 - 120{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−20,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Decrease in taxes payable</td><td>$0 - 30{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−30,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= CFO</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−2,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>= CFI</strong> (no aircraft bought or sold)</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Repayment of the long-term loan</td><td>$280{,}000 - 300{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−20,000</td></tr><tr><td>+ Short-term bank credit drawn</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+60,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Dividend paid</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−35,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= CFF</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>+5,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Change in cash</strong></td><td>$-2{,}000 + 0 + 5{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>+3,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>$\\Delta\\text{Cash} = 103{,}000 - 100{,}000 = 3{,}000$ m.u. ✓</p>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the working capital, the ROE and the debt-to-equity ratio of both years</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Do not be misled by the working capital: ask whether the new short-term credit can change it, and what does.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Working capital 50,000 → 63,000 m.u. (+13,000, explained by the long-term side, not by the credit).</li><li>ROE 12.7% → −2.4%; $L/E$ 0.82 → 0.87.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ratio</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2024</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2025</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Working capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$200{,}000 - 150{,}000 = 50{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$223{,}000 - 160{,}000 = 63{,}000$</td></tr><tr><td>ROE</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$70{,}000/550{,}000 = 12.7\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$-12{,}000/503{,}000 = -2.4\\%$</td></tr><tr><td>$L/E$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$450{,}000/550{,}000 = 0.82$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$440{,}000/503{,}000 = 0.87$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The new short-term credit does <strong>not</strong> explain the rise: it adds 60,000 to cash (current assets) and 60,000 to current liabilities, so it leaves the working capital unchanged. The rise comes from the long-term side of the balance sheet:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\Delta\\text{WC} = \\Delta E + \\Delta\\text{Long-term liabilities} - \\Delta\\text{Non-current assets} = (-47{,}000) + (-20{,}000) - (-80{,}000) = +13{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<p>The aircraft lost 80,000 of value through depreciation that was not reinvested, more than the loss, the dividend and the loan repayment together. What the credit does change is the quality of the liquidity: without it, cash would be only 43,000.</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Diagnose the situation and propose two measures based on the course</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Operations no longer generate cash. Which financing sources of the course do not add fixed charges, and which decisions protect cash?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Diagnosis: fixed-cost structure + negative CFO; the dividend and the loan repayment were paid with borrowed short-term money.</li><li>Measures: suspend dividends (self-financing), refinance at long term or via sale and leasing of an aircraft, adjust capacity (variable-cost measures); a rights issue lowers leverage.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>Halcyon has gone from a profitable year to a small loss because its costs are largely fixed. Its operations consumed 2,000 m.u. of cash, and the 35,000 dividend plus the 20,000 loan repayment were in practice paid with <strong>short-term bank credit</strong>, which is the most fragile source [Slide 35].</p>\n<p>Measures consistent with the course:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Suspend the dividend</strong> until profits recover, so that profits (when they return) stay in the company as self-financing [Slides 23-24].</li><li><strong>Replace the short-term credit with long-term resources</strong>, a long-term loan or leasing arrangements for the aircraft [Slides 31, 33], or a <strong>rights issue</strong> that reduces leverage [Slide 26].</li><li>On the operating side, turn fixed costs into variable ones (for example operating leasing of aircraft and seasonal crews) so that costs follow revenue.</li></ol>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Operating leverage (extension):</strong> a 15% fall in sales caused a 92% fall in income from operations because most costs are fixed.</li><li><strong>Working capital is not liquidity:</strong> it rose by 13,000 m.u. while the operations consumed cash and the cash balance depended on short-term borrowing.</li><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> all figures in m.u.; margins and ROE dimensionless.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P25", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "year-on-year-cash", "theme": "year-on-year-cash", "unit": 3, "title": "Cirrus Test Labs: the closing balance sheet from a list of changes", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 40, "tags": ["extra-practice", "balance-sheet", "cash-flow", "depreciation", "self-financing"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise); the three-section cash-flow statement is an extension of Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P25 - Cirrus Test Labs Closing Balance from a List of Changes.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p><strong>Cirrus Test Labs S.L.</strong> runs vibration and climatic tests for space equipment. Its balance sheet at 31/12/2024 was (m.u.): equipment at cost 300,000 with accumulated depreciation 90,000; consumables 20,000; accounts receivable 45,000; cash 35,000; contributed capital 200,000; retained earnings 40,000; long-term loan 40,000; accounts payable 30,000.</p>\n<p>Its income statement for 2025 shows: test revenue 400,000; consumables used 80,000; staff 210,000; other operating expenses 30,000; depreciation 30,000; interest 2,000; tax rate 25% (payable in 2026).</p>\n<p>During 2025 the following also happened:</p>\n<ol><li>A new shaker table was bought for 60,000: 40,000 paid in 2025, 20,000 to be paid in March 2026.</li><li>An old climatic chamber, bought for 50,000 and fully depreciated, was scrapped; nothing was received for it.</li><li>Accounts receivable rose by 15,000; the stock of consumables fell by 4,000; accounts payable rose by 6,000.</li><li>10,000 of the long-term loan were repaid.</li><li>A dividend of 40% of the net income of 2025 was paid in cash on 31 December.</li><li>A capital increase of 30,000 was subscribed and paid in cash.</li></ol>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Roll-forward of fixed assets:</strong> closing cost = opening cost + purchases − retirements; closing accumulated depreciation = opening + depreciation of the year − depreciation of the retired assets. A fully depreciated asset that is scrapped leaves both lines by the same amount, so no gain or loss arises.</p>\n<p><strong>Extension:</strong> indirect cash-flow statement (see Bank P23).</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the net income and roll forward the equipment and its accumulated depreciation</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The scrapped chamber is removed from the cost <strong>and</strong> from the accumulated depreciation.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Net income 36,000 m.u.</li><li>Equipment at cost 310,000; accumulated depreciation 70,000; net 240,000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Net income</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Income from operations} = 400{,}000 - 80{,}000 - 210{,}000 - 30{,}000 - 30{,}000 = 50{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{IBT} = 50{,}000 - 2{,}000 = 48{,}000, \\qquad \\text{Tax} = 0.25 \\times 48{,}000 = 12{,}000, \\qquad \\text{NI} = 36{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Equipment</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Cost</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Accumulated depreciation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Opening, 31/12/2024</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">90,000</td></tr><tr><td>+ Shaker table</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">—</td></tr><tr><td>+ Depreciation of 2025</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">—</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Scrapped chamber</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−50,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−50,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Closing, 31/12/2025</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>310,000</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>70,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Net equipment} = 310{,}000 - 70{,}000 = 240{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the balance sheet at 31/12/2025, finding the cash as the balancing item</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Apply each change to its line. The unpaid 20,000 for the shaker table and the tax of 2025 are new current liabilities.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Dividend 14,400; retained earnings 61,600; equity 291,600 m.u.</li><li>Current liabilities 68,000; total 389,600 m.u.; cash 73,600 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Equity</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividend} = 0.40 \\times 36{,}000 = 14{,}400, \\qquad \\text{RE} = 40{,}000 + 36{,}000 - 14{,}400 = 61{,}600$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$E = (200{,}000 + 30{,}000) + 61{,}600 = 291{,}600 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Liabilities and cash</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$L = (40{,}000 - 10{,}000) + (30{,}000 + 6{,}000) + 20{,}000 + 12{,}000 = 30{,}000 + 68{,}000 = 98{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{TA} = 291{,}600 + 98{,}000 = 389{,}600, \\qquad \\text{Cash} = 389{,}600 - 240{,}000 - 16{,}000 - 60{,}000 = 73{,}600 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Balance sheet</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assets</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Liabilities and equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">310,000</td><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">230,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Accumulated depreciation</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">(70,000)</td><td>Retained earnings</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">61,600</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>240,000</strong></td><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>291,600</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Consumables ($20{,}000 - 4{,}000$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">16,000</td><td>Long-term loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td><strong>Long-term liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>30,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable ($45{,}000 + 15{,}000$)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">36,000</td></tr><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">73,600</td><td>Supplier of the shaker table</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Income taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Current assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>149,600</strong></td><td><strong>Current liabilities</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>68,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>389,600</strong></td><td><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>389,600</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the cash-flow statement of 2025 and check it against the change in cash</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Only the 40,000 actually paid for the shaker table are an investing outflow; the 20,000 owed will be an outflow of 2026.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>CFO 73,000; CFI −40,000; CFF +5,600 m.u.</li><li>Δcash = 38,600 = 73,600 − 35,000 ✓</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Net income</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">36,000</td></tr><tr><td>+ Depreciation</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Increase in receivables</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−15,000</td></tr><tr><td>+ Decrease in consumables</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+4,000</td></tr><tr><td>+ Increase in payables</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+6,000</td></tr><tr><td>+ Increase in taxes payable</td><td>$12{,}000 - 0$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+12,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= CFO</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>73,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Shaker table paid in 2025</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−40,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= CFI</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−40,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Loan repayment</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−10,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Dividend</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−14,400</td></tr><tr><td>+ Capital increase</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+30,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= CFF</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>+5,600</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Change in cash</strong></td><td>$73{,}000 - 40{,}000 + 5{,}600$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>38,600</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>$\\Delta\\text{Cash} = 73{,}600 - 35{,}000 = 38{,}600$ m.u. ✓</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the pay-out and self-financing ratios and explain how the investment of 2025 was financed</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compare the investing outflow with the cash generated by operations and by each financing source.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Pay-out 40%; self-financing 60% [Slide 23].</li><li>The 40,000 paid were covered by the operations (73,000); the capital increase (30,000) more than covered the dividend and the repayment (24,400).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Pay-out} = \\frac{14{,}400}{36{,}000} = 40\\%, \\qquad \\text{Self-financing} = \\frac{21{,}600}{36{,}000} = 60\\%$$</div>\n<p>The operations generated 73,000 m.u. of cash, enough to pay the 40,000 of the new shaker table on their own; the rest of the investment is supplier credit (20,000). The capital increase (30,000) more than covered the dividend and the loan repayment (24,400), so cash grew by 38,600 m.u.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Scrapping a fully depreciated asset</strong> changes neither profit nor cash; it only cleans up the cost and the accumulated depreciation.</li><li><strong>Supplier of fixed assets:</strong> a debt for an investment is a liability, not an account payable for operations; its payment will appear in investing flows of 2026.</li><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> all changes in m.u.; ratios dimensionless.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P26", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "year-on-year-cash", "theme": "year-on-year-cash", "unit": 3, "title": "Sirius Flight Academy: from accrual figures to collections, payments and cash", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 45, "tags": ["extra-practice", "cash", "accrual-vs-cash", "unearned-revenue", "cash-flow"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise); the cash account follows T3_Additional_SOLUTIONS.pdf, the indirect reconciliation is an extension", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P26 - Sirius Flight Academy Collections Payments and Cash Account.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p><strong>Sirius Flight Academy S.L.</strong> trains commercial pilots. Students may pay before their course starts (prepaid courses) or after finishing a module. Its income statement for 2025 is (m.u.): tuition revenue 500,000; fuel and maintenance 140,000; instructors' salaries 220,000; hangar rent 36,000; depreciation of aircraft and simulators 60,000; interest 8,000; corporate tax 30%.</p>\n<p>Selected balances at 31/12 (m.u.):</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2024</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">2025</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cash</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">?</td></tr><tr><td>Tuition pending from students</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">55,000</td></tr><tr><td>Courses prepaid by students, not yet taught</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td></tr><tr><td>Owed to fuel and maintenance suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">18,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td>Salaries payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,000</td></tr><tr><td>Taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10,800</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>During 2025 Sirius also bought a new flight simulator for 120,000 in cash, received a long-term bank loan of 80,000, and paid dividends of 12,000. Rent and interest were paid in the year in which they were incurred.</p>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Accrual vs cash</strong> [Slide 8]: revenue is recorded when the course is taught, and the collection may happen before (prepaid course, a liability) or after (tuition pending, an asset). For each flow:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Collections} = \\text{Revenue} - \\Delta\\,\\text{Tuition pending} + \\Delta\\,\\text{Prepaid courses}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Payments to suppliers} = \\text{Expense} - \\Delta\\,\\text{Amount owed to suppliers}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Course cash account</strong> (T3_Additional_SOLUTIONS.pdf): $\\text{Cash at end} = \\text{Initial cash} + \\text{Cash inflows} - \\text{Cash outflows}$.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the net income and the cash collected from students in 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Tuition pending grew: part of the revenue was not collected. Prepaid courses grew: students paid for courses not yet taught.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Net income 25,200 m.u. (tax 10,800).</li><li>Collections 495,000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Income from operations} = 500{,}000 - 140{,}000 - 220{,}000 - 36{,}000 - 60{,}000 = 44{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{IBT} = 44{,}000 - 8{,}000 = 36{,}000, \\qquad \\text{Tax} = 0.30 \\times 36{,}000 = 10{,}800, \\qquad \\text{NI} = 25{,}200 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Collections} = 500{,}000 - (55{,}000 - 40{,}000) + (40{,}000 - 30{,}000) = 500{,}000 - 15{,}000 + 10{,}000 = 495{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute every operating payment of 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Apply the same correction to every expense: expense minus the increase in what is still owed. The corporate tax works the same way. Depreciation is not a payment.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Suppliers 146,000; salaries 220,000; rent 36,000; interest 8,000; tax 9,000 m.u.</li><li>Total operating payments 419,000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Payment</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fuel and maintenance suppliers</td><td>$140{,}000 - (12{,}000 - 18{,}000)$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">146,000</td></tr><tr><td>Salaries</td><td>$220{,}000 - (10{,}000 - 10{,}000)$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">220,000</td></tr><tr><td>Hangar rent</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">36,000</td></tr><tr><td>Interest</td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8,000</td></tr><tr><td>Corporate tax (tax of 2024)</td><td>$10{,}800 - (10{,}800 - 9{,}000)$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total operating payments</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>419,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The amount owed to suppliers fell, so Sirius paid more than the expense of the year: it also settled part of what it owed from 2024.</p>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the cash account of 2025 and find the cash at 31/12/2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Add the non-operating flows: the simulator, the loan and the dividend.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Inflows 575,000; outflows 551,000 m.u.</li><li>Cash at 31/12/2025: 84,000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Inflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th><th>Outflows</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Collections from students</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">495,000</td><td>Operating payments</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">419,000</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80,000</td><td>Flight simulator</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">120,000</td></tr><tr><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"></td><td>Dividends</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>575,000</strong></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>551,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Cash}_{31/12/2025} = 60{,}000 + 575{,}000 - 551{,}000 = 84{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Reconcile the net income with the operating cash flow and explain the difference</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Start from the net income and correct it for each item that is in the income statement but did not move cash in the same amount.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Operating cash flow (direct): $495{,}000 - 419{,}000 = 76{,}000$ m.u.</li><li>Indirect: $25{,}200 + 60{,}000 - 15{,}000 + 10{,}000 - 6{,}000 + 0 + 1{,}800 = 76{,}000$ ✓</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Direct method</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{CFO} = \\text{Collections} - \\text{Operating payments} = 495{,}000 - 419{,}000 = 76{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Indirect method (extension)</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Net income</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">25,200</td></tr><tr><td>+ Depreciation (expense without payment)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Increase in tuition pending</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−15,000</td></tr><tr><td>+ Increase in prepaid courses</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+10,000</td></tr><tr><td>− Decrease in amounts owed to suppliers</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−6,000</td></tr><tr><td>± Change in salaries payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td>+ Increase in taxes payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+1,800</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= CFO</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>76,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Why net income and cash differ</h4>\n<p>Profit measures what Sirius <strong>earned</strong> in 2025; cash measures what it <strong>received and paid</strong>. The largest gap is depreciation (60,000), an expense that consumes no cash in the year. Students who still owe tuition reduce cash; students who prepay increase it; settling old supplier debts reduces it. Both methods give the same 76,000 m.u. because they describe the same transactions from two sides.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Full cash check:</strong> $\\text{CFO} + \\text{CFI} + \\text{CFF} = 76{,}000 - 120{,}000 + (80{,}000 - 12{,}000) = 24{,}000 = 84{,}000 - 60{,}000$ ✓.</li><li><strong>Prepaid courses are liabilities:</strong> the academy owes the training, which is why an increase brings cash in without being revenue yet [Slide 13].</li><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> every term in m.u.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P27", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "financing-leverage", "theme": "financing-leverage", "unit": 3, "title": "Zenith Launch Services: financing a new pad with a bank loan or a rights issue", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 40, "tags": ["extra-practice", "financial-leverage", "roa", "roe", "financing-decisions"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), using Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 20, 25-26, 31 and 36; the leverage formula is an extension", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P27 - Zenith Launch Services Bank Loan or Rights Issue.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p><strong>Zenith Launch Services S.A.</strong> integrates small satellites into rockets. It has total assets of 3,000,000 m.u., financed entirely with equity (300,000 shares of nominal value 10 m.u.), and no debt. Its income from operations is 600,000 m.u. per year.</p>\n<p>It wants to build a second integration pad costing 2,000,000 m.u., which would add 300,000 m.u. per year to the income from operations. Two financing options are on the table:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Option A:</strong> a long-term bank loan of 2,000,000 m.u. at 7% per year.</li><li><strong>Option B:</strong> a rights issue of 200,000 new shares at 10 m.u. each (no premium).</li></ul>\n<p>The tax rate is 25%. Assume the year-end balance sheet equals the opening one plus the new financing (no repayment in the year).</p>", "setup_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Option A (debt)</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Option B (equity)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Total assets</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,000,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Equity $E$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Liabilities $L$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td>Shares</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">300,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500,000</td></tr><tr><td>Income from operations</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">900,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">900,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The investment decision (what to invest in) is the same in both options; only the <strong>financing decision</strong> changes [Slides 3, 20]. Debt has an explicit cost, the interest; equity has an implicit, opportunity cost [Slide 37].</p>\n<p><strong>Extensions.</strong> $\\text{ROA} = \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{TA}$; $\\text{ROE} = \\text{NI}/E$; earnings per share $= \\text{NI}/\\text{number of shares}$. With $i$ the interest rate and $t$ the tax rate:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROE} = \\left[\\text{ROA} + (\\text{ROA} - i)\\,\\frac{L}{E}\\right](1 - t)$$</div>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Prepare the income statement of the first year under each option</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The income from operations is the same in both options: the financing choice only changes the lines below it.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>A: interest 140,000; income before taxes 760,000; net income 570,000 m.u.</li><li>B: interest 0; income before taxes 900,000; net income 675,000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Option A</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Option B</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Income from operations</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>900,000</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>900,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest ($0.07 \\times 2{,}000{,}000$ in A)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">140,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>760,000</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>900,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (25%)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">190,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">225,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>570,000</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>675,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Option B gives a larger net income in m.u., but it is shared among more owners' money: the next part compares the returns.</p>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute ROA, ROE, debt-to-equity, % equity and earnings per share for each option</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The ROA does not depend on the financing; ROE and earnings per share do.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>ROA 18% in both options.</li><li>ROE: A 19.0%, B 13.5%; $L/E$: A 0.67, B 0.</li><li>Earnings per share: A 1.90, B 1.35 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ratio</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Option A</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Option B</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ROA</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$900{,}000/5{,}000{,}000 = 18\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$18\\%$</td></tr><tr><td>ROE</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$570{,}000/3{,}000{,}000 = 19.0\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$675{,}000/5{,}000{,}000 = 13.5\\%$</td></tr><tr><td>$L/E$ [Slide 36]</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$2{,}000{,}000/3{,}000{,}000 = 0.67$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$0$</td></tr><tr><td>% Equity [Slide 36]</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$3{,}000{,}000/5{,}000{,}000 = 60\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$100\\%$</td></tr><tr><td>Earnings per share</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$570{,}000/300{,}000 = 1.90$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$675{,}000/500{,}000 = 1.35$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Verify the ROE of each option with the leverage formula and explain the leverage effect</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Substitute ROA = 0.18, $i = 0.07$, $t = 0.25$ and each option's $L/E$.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>A: $[0.18 + 0.11 \\times 0.667] \\times 0.75 = 0.19$ ✓</li><li>B: $0.18 \\times 0.75 = 0.135$ ✓</li><li>Debt raises the ROE because ROA (18%) &gt; cost of debt (7%).</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p><strong>Option A:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROE}_A = \\left[0.18 + (0.18 - 0.07) \\times \\frac{2{,}000{,}000}{3{,}000{,}000}\\right](1 - 0.25) = \\left[0.18 + 0.11 \\times 0.667\\right] \\times 0.75 = 0.2533 \\times 0.75 = 0.19$$</div>\n<p><strong>Option B</strong> ($L/E = 0$):</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROE}_B = 0.18 \\times 0.75 = 0.135$$</div>\n<p>Both agree with part (b). Each m.u. borrowed earns 18% in the company and costs only 7%; the difference of 11 points goes to the shareholders, multiplied by the leverage $L/E$. That is the positive <strong>financial leverage effect</strong>: as long as $\\text{ROA} &gt; i$, debt raises the return on equity.</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Recommend an option, taking risk into account</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Higher leverage tends to indicate higher risk for shareholders [Slide 36]. What would happen to Option A if the ROA fell below 7%?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>A maximises ROE and earnings per share, and keeps control with the current shareholders.</li><li>A adds a fixed charge of 140,000 m.u. per year: if ROA &lt; 7%, leverage turns against the owners.</li><li>B is safer but dilutes the owners and lowers ROE.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>With the expected figures, <strong>Option A</strong> is better for the current shareholders: ROE rises from the 15% of today ($0.20 \\times 0.75$) to 19%, earnings per share from 1.50 to 1.90, and no new shareholders enter. Its cost is risk: the company commits to 140,000 m.u. of interest every year and to repaying the principal [Slide 31]. If launch demand fell and the ROA dropped below 7%, debt would reduce the ROE instead of raising it.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B</strong> never has to be repaid and adds no fixed charge [Slide 25], but it lowers the ROE to 13.5% and spreads the profit over 500,000 shares. A firm with stable demand and moderate leverage after the loan ($L/E = 0.67$) can reasonably choose A; if demand is very uncertain, B (or a mix of both) protects the company.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Before the project:</strong> ROA $= 600{,}000/3{,}000{,}000 = 20\\%$; the project's own return is $300{,}000/2{,}000{,}000 = 15\\%$, still well above the 7% cost of debt.</li><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> ratios dimensionless; earnings per share in m.u./share.</li><li><strong>Key idea:</strong> the investment creates the ROA; the financing decision decides how that return is shared between lenders, the State (taxes) and owners.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P28", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "financing-leverage", "theme": "financing-leverage", "unit": 3, "title": "Borealis Composites: ROE in three scenarios and the break-even income from operations", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 45, "tags": ["extra-practice", "financial-leverage", "risk", "roe", "scenarios"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), using Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slide 36; ROE and the break-even analysis are extensions", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P28 - Borealis Composites ROE Scenarios and Break-Even Income.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p>The founders of <strong>Borealis Composites S.A.</strong>, a supplier of thermoplastic brackets for aircraft cabins, need 1,000,000 m.u. to start. They hesitate between two financial structures:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Plan X:</strong> 1,000,000 m.u. of equity, no debt.</li><li><strong>Plan Y:</strong> 400,000 m.u. of equity and a bank loan of 600,000 m.u. at 8% per year.</li></ul>\n<p>The tax rate is 30%; a year with a loss pays no tax. The income from operations of the first year depends on the market:</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Income from operations (m.u.)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Downturn</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td></tr><tr><td>Base</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td></tr><tr><td>Boom</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "setup_html": "<p>The assets (1,000,000 m.u.) and the income from operations are the same under both plans; only the interest and the equity base change. $\\text{ROE} = \\text{NI}/E$ and $\\text{ROA} = \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{TA}$ (extensions).</p>\n<p>Interest under Plan Y: $I = 0.08 \\times 600{,}000 = 48{,}000$ m.u. per year, in every scenario.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 1.5, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the net income and the ROE of each plan in each scenario</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Under Plan Y, check first whether the income from operations covers the 48,000 of interest. If it does not, there is a loss and no tax.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Plan X ROE: 2.8% / 7.0% / 12.6%.</li><li>Plan Y ROE: −2.0% / 9.1% / 23.1%.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Plan X (no debt)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{NI} = (1 - 0.30) \\times \\text{Income from operations}, \\qquad \\text{ROE} = \\frac{\\text{NI}}{1{,}000{,}000}$$</div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">IBT</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Tax</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">NI</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">ROE</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Downturn</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">28,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Base</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">30,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">70,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">7.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Boom</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">180,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">54,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">126,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.6%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Plan Y (debt of 600,000 at 8%)</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{IBT} = \\text{Income from operations} - 48{,}000, \\qquad \\text{ROE} = \\frac{\\text{NI}}{400{,}000}$$</div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">IBT</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Tax</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">NI</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">ROE</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Downturn</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$40{,}000 - 48{,}000 = -8{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−8,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">−2.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Base</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$100{,}000 - 48{,}000 = 52{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15,600</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">36,400</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9.1%</td></tr><tr><td>Boom</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$180{,}000 - 48{,}000 = 132{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">39,600</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">92,400</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">23.1%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Find the income from operations at which both plans give the same ROE, and relate it to the ROA and the interest rate</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Set $\\text{ROE}_X = \\text{ROE}_Y$ with the income from operations as the unknown $B$, assuming the result is positive so both plans pay tax.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>$B = 80{,}000$ m.u.; ROE = 5.6% in both plans.</li><li>At that point ROA = 8% = interest rate.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\frac{0.70\\,B}{1{,}000{,}000} = \\frac{0.70\\,(B - 48{,}000)}{400{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>Dividing both sides by 0.70 and cross-multiplying:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$400{,}000\\,B = 1{,}000{,}000\\,(B - 48{,}000) \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 600{,}000\\,B = 48{,}000{,}000{,}000 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; B = 80{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROE}_X = \\text{ROE}_Y = \\frac{0.70 \\times 80{,}000}{1{,}000{,}000} = 5.6\\%, \\qquad \\text{ROA} = \\frac{80{,}000}{1{,}000{,}000} = 8\\% = i$$</div>\n<p>Above 80,000 m.u. (ROA &gt; 8%) debt raises the ROE; below it, debt lowers it.</p>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the debt-to-equity ratio and % equity of each plan and the spread of ROE across scenarios; relate them to risk</p>", "hint_html": "<p>The spread (boom minus downturn) is a simple measure of how much the owners' return varies.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>X: $L/E = 0$, 100% equity, spread 9.8 points.</li><li>Y: $L/E = 1.5$, 40% equity, spread 25.1 points.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">$L/E$</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">% Equity</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">ROE spread</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>X</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">100%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$12.6\\% - 2.8\\% = 9.8$ points</td></tr><tr><td>Y</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$600{,}000/400{,}000 = 1.5$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$23.1\\% - (-2.0\\%) = 25.1$ points</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The same business risk (income from operations from 40,000 to 180,000) becomes a much larger risk for the owners under Plan Y: debt amplifies the ROE both upward and downward. This is what the course means by \"higher leverage ratios tend to indicate higher risk for shareholders\" [Slide 36].</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Which plan would you recommend if the base scenario is the most likely but a downturn is possible?</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compare the expected return with what happens in the bad case, including the obligation to pay interest whatever the result.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Base: Y beats X (9.1% vs 7.0%) because the base ROA (10%) exceeds 8%.</li><li>Downturn: Y makes a loss and still owes 48,000 of interest; X stays profitable.</li><li>A reasoned answer either way; a start-up usually favours less debt.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>In the most likely scenario, Plan Y gives the owners 9.1% instead of 7.0% while requiring them to invest only 400,000 m.u. But Plan Y commits the company to 48,000 m.u. of interest every year: in a downturn it makes a loss, consumes cash and depends on the bank's patience. For a <strong>start-up</strong> with no track record, whose income is uncertain, Plan X (or an intermediate structure) is the prudent choice; Plan Y suits owners who are confident that the ROA will stay well above 8% and who can bear a bad year.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Rule of thumb (extension):</strong> debt helps the owners exactly when $\\text{ROA} &gt; i$; the break-even of part (b) is where $\\text{ROA} = i$.</li><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> m.u. on both sides of the break-even equation; ROE dimensionless.</li><li><strong>Limitation:</strong> the losses of the downturn could be offset against future profits (as in the WIFINET exercise), which would soften the bad case slightly; it is ignored here.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P29", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "financing-leverage", "theme": "financing-leverage", "unit": 3, "title": "Meridian Avionics: choosing a capital structure with the WACC and a bank covenant", "difficulty": 2, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 35, "tags": ["extra-practice", "wacc", "capital-structure", "value-creation", "leverage"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), using Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 5, 36 and 37", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P29 - Meridian Avionics WACC and a Bank Covenant.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p><strong>Meridian Avionics S.A.</strong> needs 5,000,000 m.u. to finance its assets. Its banks and its investors have quoted the following costs for three possible structures:</p>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Equity share</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Liabilities share</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Cost of equity $K_{EQ}$</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Cost of liabilities $K_L$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>S1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">80%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">20%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10.0%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.0%</td></tr><tr><td>S2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">11.0%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5.5%</td></tr><tr><td>S3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">40%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">60%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">12.0%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6.0%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The main bank imposes a <strong>covenant</strong>: the debt-to-equity ratio must not exceed 1.0. The assets are expected to earn a return of 9.5% per year.</p>", "setup_html": "<p>The capital cost of the company is the weighted average of the costs of its financial resources [Slide 37]:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$K_{WACC} = K_{EQ}\\,\\frac{E}{E+L} + K_L\\,\\frac{L}{E+L}$$</div>\n<p>The company creates value when the return on its assets is above the cost of the funds that finance them (profitability &gt; cost) [Slide 5]. Both costs rise with leverage, because lenders and owners demand more when the risk is higher [Slide 36].</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the amounts of equity and liabilities and the debt-to-equity ratio of each structure</p>", "hint_html": "<p>$L/E = \\%L / \\%E$.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>S1: $E$ 4,000,000, $L$ 1,000,000, $L/E$ 0.25.</li><li>S2: $E$ 3,000,000, $L$ 2,000,000, $L/E$ 0.67.</li><li>S3: $E$ 2,000,000, $L$ 3,000,000, $L/E$ 1.50.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">$E$ (m.u.)</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">$L$ (m.u.)</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">$L/E$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>S1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$0.80 \\times 5{,}000{,}000 = 4{,}000{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,000,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.25</td></tr><tr><td>S2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0.67</td></tr><tr><td>S3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2,000,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3,000,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1.50</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the WACC of each structure and its annual cost in m.u.</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Multiply each cost by its weight and add. The annual cost is the WACC times the 5,000,000 m.u.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>WACC: S1 9.0%, S2 8.8%, S3 8.4%.</li><li>Annual cost: 450,000 / 440,000 / 420,000 m.u.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$K_{S1} = 0.10 \\times 0.80 + 0.05 \\times 0.20 = 0.080 + 0.010 = 9.0\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$K_{S2} = 0.11 \\times 0.60 + 0.055 \\times 0.40 = 0.066 + 0.022 = 8.8\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$K_{S3} = 0.12 \\times 0.40 + 0.06 \\times 0.60 = 0.048 + 0.036 = 8.4\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">WACC</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Annual cost of capital (m.u.)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>S1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9.0%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$0.090 \\times 5{,}000{,}000 = 450{,}000$</td></tr><tr><td>S2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8.8%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">440,000</td></tr><tr><td>S3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">8.4%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">420,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Choose the structure, taking the covenant into account</p>", "hint_html": "<p>First discard any structure that breaks the covenant; then compare the WACC of the rest.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>S3 has the lowest WACC (8.4%) but breaks the covenant ($1.50 &gt; 1.0$): not allowed.</li><li>Among the allowed structures, S2 has the lowest WACC (8.8% &lt; 9.0%): chosen.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<p>S3 would be the cheapest structure (8.4%), but its debt-to-equity ratio of 1.50 exceeds the limit of 1.0 imposed by the bank, so it is not available: the covenant is what decides the choice. Between the two structures that respect it, <strong>S2</strong> gives the lowest capital cost (8.8% against 9.0%), saving 10,000 m.u. per year. Moving from S1 to S2 lowers the WACC because more of the cheaper source (debt) is used while its cost rises only slightly; the covenant caps how far Meridian can go down that path.</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Check whether the assets create value under each structure, and by how much per year</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Compare the expected return of 9.5% with each WACC; the yearly surplus is the difference times 5,000,000. What does the covenant cost the company?</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>S1: +0.5% → +25,000 m.u.; S2: +0.7% → +35,000 m.u.; S3: +1.1% → +55,000 m.u. (not allowed).</li><li>Cost of the covenant: 55,000 − 35,000 = 20,000 m.u. per year.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Return − WACC</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">Value created per year (m.u.)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>S1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$9.5\\% - 9.0\\% = +0.5\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$0.005 \\times 5{,}000{,}000 = +25{,}000$</td></tr><tr><td>S2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$9.5\\% - 8.8\\% = +0.7\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+35,000</td></tr><tr><td>S3 (not allowed)</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">$9.5\\% - 8.4\\% = +1.1\\%$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">+55,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Under every structure the assets earn more than the funds cost (profitability &gt; cost), so the company <strong>creates value</strong> [Slide 5]. The chosen structure S2 creates 35,000 m.u. per year. S3 would create 55,000 m.u., so the covenant costs Meridian about 20,000 m.u. per year; that is the price of the protection the bank demands against a highly leveraged borrower [Slide 36]. If the expected return fell below 8.8%, the same assets financed with S2 would destroy value: the financing decision can turn a good investment into a bad one.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> weights and costs dimensionless; cost of capital in m.u./year.</li><li><strong>Extension:</strong> in practice interest reduces the taxes paid, so the cost of debt is often taken after tax, $K_L(1 - t)$; the course formula uses $K_L$ as given.</li><li><strong>Key idea:</strong> the optimum is not \"as little debt as possible\" nor \"as much as possible\", but the structure with the lowest weighted cost that the lenders accept.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "P30", "category": "extra_practice_problem", "archetype": "financing-leverage", "theme": "financing-leverage", "unit": 3, "title": "Polaris Space Hardware: self-financing, bonds and a rights issue in one plan", "difficulty": 3, "points": 4.0, "time_minutes": 45, "tags": ["extra-practice", "self-financing", "bonds", "rights-issue", "roa", "roe"], "official": false, "source": "Practice item written for the problem bank (not an official exercise), using Topic 3_Financial Management (I).pdf, Slides 21-29 and 36", "vault_note": "vault/05 - Business Management/Problem Bank/Problem - Bank P30 - Polaris Space Hardware Mixed Financing Plan.md", "page": null, "statement_html": "<p><strong>Polaris Space Hardware S.A.</strong> builds deployable solar arrays. Its net income of 2025 was 375,000 m.u., and the board paid 75,000 m.u. as dividends; the rest was kept in the company as cash. After that distribution, its balance sheet at 31/12/2025 showed (m.u.): total assets 3,000,000; contributed capital 1,000,000 (100,000 shares of nominal value 10); reserves (retained earnings) 800,000; a bank loan of 700,000 at 5% per year; accounts payable 500,000 (no interest).</p>\n<p>On 1/1/2026 Polaris starts a new clean-room line costing 1,200,000 m.u., financed as follows:</p>\n<ul><li>the 300,000 m.u. retained from the 2025 profit;</li><li>a bond issue of 500,000 m.u. at par, coupon 6% per year, maturing in 2033;</li><li>a rights issue of 40,000 new shares at nominal value, fully paid in cash.</li></ul>\n<p>For 2026 Polaris expects an income from operations of 520,000 m.u.; the tax rate is 25% and it plans to keep a pay-out of 20%. Assume the balance sheet of 2026 stays as it is on 1/1/2026 after the investment.</p>", "setup_html": "<p><strong>Map of the financing sources</strong> [Slides 21-22]: self-financing is internal equity [Slide 23]; a rights issue is external equity [Slides 25-26]; bonds are long-term external liabilities [Slide 29].</p>\n<p><strong>Effect on the balance sheet.</strong> The 300,000 of retained profit is already in the balance sheet as cash: using it changes cash into plant and leaves the total unchanged. The bonds and the rights issue bring 900,000 of new money, so the total grows by 900,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Extensions.</strong> $\\text{ROA} = \\text{Income from operations}/\\text{TA}$; $\\text{ROE} = \\text{NI}/E$.</p>", "subquestions": [{"label": "a", "points": 0.75, "prompt_html": "<p>Compute the pay-out and the self-financing ratios of 2025</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Use the definitions of [Slide 23]: both are shares of the profit.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Pay-out 20%; self-financing 80%.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Pay-out} = \\frac{75{,}000}{375{,}000} = 20\\%, \\qquad \\text{Self-financing} = \\frac{375{,}000 - 75{,}000}{375{,}000} = \\frac{300{,}000}{375{,}000} = 80\\%$$</div>"}, {"label": "b", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Classify the three sources of the plan by nature and origin, and give one advantage and one drawback of each</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Think about repayment, cost (explicit or implicit), and who receives future profits.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Self-financing: equity, internal; no repayment, no new owners; limited by the profit and lowers dividends.</li><li>Bonds: liability, external, long-term; owners keep control; fixed coupon and repayment in 2033.</li><li>Rights issue: equity, external; never repaid, no fixed charge; dilutes the current owners.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Nature / origin</th><th>Advantage</th><th>Drawback</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Retained profit, 300,000</td><td>Equity / internal (self-financing)</td><td>No repayment and no explicit cost; no new owners [Slides 23-24]</td><td>Limited by the profit; shareholders give up dividends now</td></tr><tr><td>Bonds, 500,000</td><td>Liability / external, long-term</td><td>Finances a long-term investment without diluting control [Slide 29]</td><td>Fixed coupon every year and repayment of the principal in 2033</td></tr><tr><td>Rights issue, 400,000</td><td>Equity / external</td><td>Never repaid; dividends are not compulsory [Slides 25-26]</td><td>New shareholders share future profits (dilution); usually the most expensive source (implicit cost)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"label": "c", "points": 1.0, "prompt_html": "<p>Compare the financial structure before and after the plan</p>", "hint_html": "<p>Before: the balance sheet of 31/12/2025. After: add 500,000 of bonds and 400,000 of capital; the retained 300,000 are already in equity.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Before: $E$ 1,800,000, $L$ 1,200,000, TA 3,000,000; 60% equity; $L/E = 0.67$.</li><li>After: $E$ 2,200,000, $L$ 1,700,000, TA 3,900,000; 56.4% equity; $L/E = 0.77$.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Rights issue</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$40{,}000 \\times 10 = 400{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}, \\qquad \\text{Capital after} = 1{,}000{,}000 + 400{,}000 = 1{,}400{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Structure</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">31/12/2025</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">1/1/2026</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Contributed capital</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,000,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1,400,000</td></tr><tr><td>Reserves</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">800,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">800,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Equity $E$</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,800,000</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>2,200,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Bonds</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500,000</td></tr><tr><td>Bank loan</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">700,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">700,000</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts payable</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500,000</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">500,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Liabilities $L$</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,200,000</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>1,700,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3,000,000</strong></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>3,900,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\%E: \\frac{1{,}800{,}000}{3{,}000{,}000} = 60\\% \\;\\to\\; \\frac{2{,}200{,}000}{3{,}900{,}000} = 56.4\\%; \\qquad \\frac{L}{E}: 0.67 \\;\\to\\; \\frac{1{,}700{,}000}{2{,}200{,}000} = 0.77$$</div>\n<p>The plan increases leverage only slightly, because 700,000 of the 1,200,000 come from equity (internal and external).</p>"}, {"label": "d", "points": 1.25, "prompt_html": "<p>Project the income statement of 2026, the dividend, the ROA and the ROE</p>", "hint_html": "<p>There are two interest-bearing debts: the bank loan and the bonds. The accounts payable bear no interest.</p>", "checkpoint_html": "<ul><li>Interest 65,000; income before taxes 455,000; net income 341,250 m.u.</li><li>Dividend 68,250; retained 273,000 m.u.</li><li>ROA 13.3%; ROE 15.5%.</li></ul>", "solution_html": "<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 1. Interest</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$I = 0.05 \\times 700{,}000 + 0.06 \\times 500{,}000 = 35{,}000 + 30{,}000 = 65{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 2. Income statement 2026 (forecast)</h4>\n<div class=\"table-container\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Computation</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">m.u.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Income from operations</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>520,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td>Step 1</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">65,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Income before taxes</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>455,000</strong></td></tr><tr><td>− Income taxes (25%)</td><td>$0.25 \\times 455{,}000$</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">113,750</td></tr><tr><td><strong>= Net income</strong></td><td></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>341,250</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{Dividend} = 0.20 \\times 341{,}250 = 68{,}250, \\qquad \\text{Retained} = 341{,}250 - 68{,}250 = 273{,}000 \\text{ m.u.}$$</div>\n<h4 class=\"step-title\">Step 3. Returns</h4>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$\\text{ROA} = \\frac{520{,}000}{3{,}900{,}000} = 13.3\\%, \\qquad \\text{ROE} = \\frac{341{,}250}{2{,}200{,}000} = 15.5\\%$$</div>\n<p>Leverage works in the owners' favour because the assets earn more than the liabilities cost. The ROE (after tax) and the ROA (before interest and tax) are not directly comparable; the right test compares the ROA with the average cost of all the liabilities, including the interest-free suppliers:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\">$$i = \\frac{65{,}000}{1{,}700{,}000} = 3.82\\% &lt; \\text{ROA} = 13.33\\%$$</div>\n<p>The leverage formula of Bank P27 (extension) confirms the ROE: $[0.1333 + (0.1333 - 0.0382) \\times 0.7727] \\times 0.75 = [0.1333 + 0.0735] \\times 0.75 = 0.155$.</p>"}], "interpretation_html": "<ul><li><strong>Mixed financing:</strong> combining the three sources keeps leverage moderate (0.77) while limiting the dilution to 40,000 new shares (40% more shares).</li><li><strong>Self-financing next year:</strong> the 273,000 retained in 2026 will finance part of the next investment, closing the cycle \"profits → retained earnings → investment\" of Topic 2 (unified vision).</li><li><strong>Dimensional check:</strong> all amounts in m.u.; ratios dimensionless.</li></ul>"}]};
